        <p>• '•‘</p>
        <p>SYMPATHY is ackittish and perverse nymph; demand too much of her, and she gives nothing. When a soldier has lost his arm, if he were to go whining about the world lamenting over it, every. 'one would despise him; but if he holds his tongue, .and carries his sleeve carelessly, all the girls are in love with him.—.L..ast Ingelow. LI the sphere of morals, as in all other depsrt- ments of human activity, we are bound to do fox our successors what our predecessors were bound to .40, ad mostly did, for us—transmit the heritage we have re, eived, with all the additions and adaptations which the new experiences and changing conditions of life have rendered ntcassary or desirable. WITHOUT steadiness of character in social life there can be no true fellowship. Accomplishments may please, beauty may charm, fluency and grace may attract; but to win confi lance and respect, to be trusted and relied upon, the man or woman mast be stable in character, self-poised, true to promises, ' punctual—uniting firmness to geniality, and stead- fastness to good nature. A LITTLE novelty of considerable interest is a card shuffling machine. It may be said to place all players at cards on an equal footing, and effec,ually prevents the cards being placed or picked as was frequently the case in the old style of hand shuffling. The process takes only ten seconds, and may be repeated indefinitely. The shuffling is perfect, and as the machine can be sold at a reasonable price,' lerge soles are enticipated. sss who wonia enjoy' lift and act with freedom must have the work of the day continually before his eyes. Not yesterday's work, lest he fall into despair, nor to-morrow's, lest he become a visionary ; not that which ends with the day, which is worldly work, nor yet that only which remains to eternity, for by it he cannot shape his actions.— Clarke Maxte,a. ALTHOUGH the desire to help and benefit men is a primary condition for doing it, the knowing how to accomplish it harmlessly and permanently is a large part of the work; and that must come, if at all, through intelligent reasoning. Strong feeling is nearly always one-sided, and some of the truth is thus overlooked. THE world is not a race-course or a battlefield or a prize fight. It is a place in which each man is given his own sphere to occupy and his own duty to do; and, if that sphere be occupied honourably and that duty be done well, every man is undoubtedly a victor and a "fit survivor." HAPPY is the man who can recognise in the work of to-day a connected portion of the work of life and an embodiment of the work of eternity. The foundation of his confidence are anchavgeable. for he has been made a partaker of lefiaity.—Clark Maxwell. - Pr is not so much the len ,th or the variety of our Opportunities as the way in which we use them that will decide how much they will benefit us. LET those who would affect singularity with Success first determine to be very virtuous, and he will be very sure to loe very singular.—Bacon. PLACE before children nothing but what is simple, lest you spoil their tastes, and nothing that is not innocent.</p>
