 an Italian Marchioness, woo carries about with her a bottle in which is imprisoned an insect of the sort called a "multiaed "—a wood worm with many feet. This lady, who Is not considered insane by those who know her, never does anything in.volving risk with- out taking out this bottle and bolding it in her hand This is simply the fetishism of the African negrotta. RECEET excavations in Rome go to show that the short-lived water pipes of this ago are sadly at a discount when compared with the work of the old Roman Flurnbers. The Coaunission in charge of the imprevemeet of the city of,Eto.ne has unearthed great quantities of lead water-pipes, each plainly stamped with the name of the owner of the house, the year e the plumbing, the name of the Consuls of that yew, and that of the reigning Emperor. 'FE muet not be too dogmatic. The more I see of lite, the more I see that we cannot lay down rigid dogmas for everybody. I will tell you who cannot take alcohol, and that is very important in the prtsent day. Of all the people I known who Cannot steel alcohol it is the twain workers, and you know it e: the brain workers who are increasing in numbers, and that the people who do not use their brains a/4: going clown.--air Henry Thompson. LITERARY LADY (she writes)—" The most essential punt in our intercourse with children is to be truthful ourselves. Every other interest ought to he sacra:lced to that of thruth." Tommy —" Ma, Mrs. Jones is coming in the gate." Literary Lady (angrily)—" If she asks for me. tell her I'm out of town." (ahe resumes writing.) "When we in any way deceive a child, we not only set a pernicious example, but also lose our influence over him for . ever.'</p>
        <p>Mr..Frauk S. Worthington, the private medical , attendant of the Dowager Duchess of Sutherland, one of whose duties has been to sign the medical certificates of the last few days, is a remarkably clever. cultivated, and interesting man. Ile was formerly in partnership with his brother) in large practice in Lowestoft. Several years ago he accepted a proposal to winter in Egypt as medical attendant to Mr. William Birkbeck, of Norwich. In &amp;opt Mr. Worthington's keen love of travel, sport, and adventure found full vent, and this initial experiment led to his subsequently giving up his Lowestoft practice in order to travel abroad almost constantly, sometimes with Mr. Birkbeck and, sometimes with other members of the family. It was when on the Nile that Mr. Worthington was one occasion suddenly summoned to attend a ease-of illness on the Duke of Sutherland's boat. The acquaintance thus began led, hst August, to Mr.oWorthington's being asked to attend the duke during his residence at Dunrobin. This appoint- 'meat he accepted, and since the duke's sudden death last autumn he has remained in attendance on the dowager duchess.</p>
        <p>Mr. Worthington is middle-aged, with pleasant, clear cut features, and a pale complexion. He is a delightful companion and a brilliant conversation- alist —to say nothing of being an ideal doctor. He is, moreover, an enthusiastic sportsman and a good shot—two characteristics not always combined.</p>
        <p>Dr. John Peile, who was fifty-five years of age on Monday, has done as much as any man of his time to influence the course of university politics at Cambridge, and it is significant of the esteem in which he is held that he should have twice been elected Vice-Chancellor. His little "Primer of Philology' may be compared with that of the late Dr..:Jevons on logic,as a brilliant attempt to present an intricate theme in an attractive form. But his work at Cambridge will be his best monument. Ten years ago he had a great deal to do in shaping the rcform of the Cambridge statutes, he has inter- ested himself in the work of the Cambridge Local Examination Board, is a warm advocate of Univer- sity Extension, and was one of those enlightened academicians to whose efforts is due the fact that Newnham College now exists in the heart of our oldest seat of learning.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. M. R. Haggard, who has just died at West Bradenham Hall. had for several years made his influence felt in the county life of Norfolk during his chairmanship of the Swaffham bench, and he proved himself -a strong deputy lieutenant, lie wrote a clear bold hand, which would have delighted a graphologist for its subtle indications of the purposeful character which lay behind it. It was from him that his better-known son,the acme.- isr, inherited his taste for amweur lawyering, which, in his case, however, developed into a pro- fessional vein. It was from him too that the creator of " Aysalia " derived, not only some of the details for his portrait of Allan Quatermain, but his own personal predilection for the life of a simple country squire, a predilection which he has latterly found it more and more aifficult to suppress in preer to fulfil the demands of the novel-reading</p>
        <p>The death of Mr. R. L Bensly, Arabic Professor at Cambridge, and Examiner in the University of London, has followed close upon his return from Sinai, whither he had gone to investigate the recently discovered Syriac Gospels. The expedition and the severe labor of deciphering the manuscript had probably overtaxed his strength, but, until the last few (la\ 8, be was working at the British Museum, lie was a far less prolific editor than his fellow traveller, Mr. Bendel Harris, but his labors have made solid contributions to Syria:, learning. His death recalls the chequed fortunes of the Lord Almoner's Professorship of Arabic at Cambridge. It is curious that two of his predeces- sors in that chair, which he had only held for five years, sLould have been very much his juniors. He himself was a sexagenarian, but if poor Edward Palmer had lived he would have been nine years younger, while the Hon. Keith Falconer, whose bright spirit left us far too soon, would have been only thirty-six. The late professor, although he had paid a personal visit to Syria, was essentially a bookish student, and the fact that he wit* on the one hand a member of the Bible Revision Comm!- tee, and on the other an examiner at Burlington , Houses, sufficiently indicates the unromantic char- acter of his career. It was far different with Palmer and Falconer, who acquired their colloquial Arabic in Bedawy tents, the one being murdered in the Wady Sudr, and the other having perished at Aden of Egyptian fever.</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. B. Upton, 8.A., B.Sc., who delivered the first of the Hibbert lectures on Tuesday after- noon at Portman Rooms, is Lecturer en Philosophy in Manchester New College, Oxford, and bound by the terms of the trust to he" heterodox." The very expression is used, and Hibbert strictlyideflned his intention as being" to elevate the position and the public influence of the Unitarian ministry." Mr. Upton has published two addresses entitled respec- tively " Free Teaching end Free Learning in Theo!ogy," and "The Present Agnosticism and the Coming Theology," besides contributing an essay on "Religion and Life " to a volume bearing that title published by the British and Foreign Unitarian Association in 1891.</p>
        <p>Robert Ilibbert, founder of the trust, was a Jamaica merchant, but graduated at Cambridge, in 1791. After returning for a time to Kingston, and running a tilt with Thomas Cooper, whom he enikilnved as a missionary among the slaves on his estate, he died in England in 1849. conveying to trustees 50,000 dols. in Ohio Six per Cent Stock and £B,OOO in railway shares, directing them to apply the income "in such manner as they shall from time to time deem most conducive to the spread of Christianity in its most sirolile and intelligible form, and to the unfettered exercise of the right of private judgment in matters of religion' The first series of nibbert lectures was delivered by Profes- sor Max Muller in 1878, when Dean Stanley offended the" unco guid " by granting the use of the Chap- ter House at Westminster for the purpose.</p>
        <p>The trial of the Hansard Union c,aie was concluded on Wednesday. The only remaining defendants were Mr. Horatio Bottomley and Mr. C. Dolman. The jury re- urned a verdict of "Not guilty" as te both defend- ants.</p>
