        <p>GIVE EAc3E &amp; COMFORT CURING TEETHING. Pleasant, Cooling, Aperient for Children of all Ages. Try them and prove tleir valne for yourselves. Sold everywhere in packets, Is. lid. and 9d,, or poet 14 or 54 stamps. J. PRITCHARD, CHEMIST, ILEADLE, MANCHESTER Oate'Chorlton Road).</p>
        <p>Mv DEARESt N'ELLM I think we can safely hssume that what may be ' termed the crinoline scare is over, and so Bioo6 we have heard no more about crinoline, we may consider the idea abandoised. Skirtsare, however, trimmed ; some have three small flounces arranged with spaces between, so that the third comes Just above the knee. Others have crosaway folds or tucks, laid on quite flat and plain; but these are gcenerally of velvet or silk or satin, or of different shales of the same material. In contrast with this, there is a mode which is convenient for those whoin the too close-fitting stylt+ does not suit.</p>
        <p>Apropos of headgear a very pretty bonnet is made with the crown entirely of a tuft of black feathers, two erect plumes standing up in the centre, this can be trimmed with magenta ribbon, and roses of the same hue rest in the centre of the front on the hair. An exceedingly good way of brightening up a black dress that needs renovating is to train it round the skirt and bodice with an open-worked jet pion, through which different- colouyed narrow ribbons are run. The under sleeves, tight to the elbow, would be of this be-ribboned trimming, iL also the collar. A l3Taelr beaded silk thus ornamented, the colour of the ribbons being pink and green, will have a remarkably good effect. Black lace insertion, with baby ribbon run through the holes, is also used for the same purpose. If you like the comfortable serge skirt and silk blouse style of clothing you will be glad to hear that this very sensible form of dress will be greatly worn this season, especially for country and morning wear, for which it is admirably adapted. The skirt will be made full, and much wider than was the case last year; and the blouses cut in a some- what more elaborate manner, with large gigot sleeves falling off the shoulders, and either wide revers, capes, or crossed folded bodices. The new printed silks are useful for these blouses.</p>
        <p>I fancy you will find the present time of the year a most difficult one for dressing little ones comfort- ably, for if kept too warmly cloth'ed they are ap- to become over-heated, and as suddenly chilled after it. The best way of avoiding such risks is to let them always have a substantial layer of warm woollen underwear, and then to suit the outer additions as closely as possible to the varying temperature. Frocks both warm and light can be made of the soft hornespuns or serges :hat are now offered to the buying public in such variety and abun. dance. The small skirts are now cut rather fuller. A very pretty cloak for children's wear is of biscuit-coloured faced cloth. Tois is trimmed with four or five rows of dark brown silk braid, not more than half an inch wide. The cape must be lined with silk or sarcenet of the same shade as the cloth, but unless extra warmth is needed, the coat itself need not be lined, but each seam must be sewn over with Prussian binding, for neatness sake.</p>
        <p>The reign of elaborate pitticoats, which began when the fashion for trained skirts set in, shows no signs of coming to an end. Indeed, the make and shape of our petticoats is, with the fashion mongers, one of the questions of the day. Many of the leading modistes provide petticoats to each gown, for they one and all declare that without the necessary " extension " it is impossible for the pre- sent-day skirts to look their hest. These petti- coats are made of silk (often to match the gown) or watered moreen, in a thin make, with a shaped band for the waist. From the knees downwards is an all-round broad box pleating of " crin," or, in other words, thick and stiff crossbar muslin. This make is out-lined in checks with a kind of fine cord, and is procurable in both bints sad white, Over this flounce is another one of silk or !bora% th match the upper part of the petticoat, made exactly on the lines of the stiff flounce, but rather longer, in order to hide the pleating of " crin." White petticoats are being made in the same style, with an extra deep flounce of lace or embroider:, to hide the cross-bar muslin. These petticoats may be easily contrived at home.</p>
        <p>A charming gown of the coat and skirt genus is made of material of very dark grey cloth, 46 inches wide, the revers, ,collar, band, and sash being of black moire silk. The make of the coat is some- what novel, it is all in one with the basque at the back as far as the side seams, when it divides, the upper portion to form an Eton jacket, the lower fastening to the sash. The beautiful roco'co Empire fans, from old designs, in silk, gauze, and chicken skin, for using with the Empire gowns, are among the novelties of the present season, and there are some lovely fea- ther ones in shaded petunia or pale green; but the most enticing are the more inexpensive ones in gauze, and among them are the bridesmaids' dahlia fans. Each fan, whether in white or colour, has a row of detached sil gle dahlias, life-sized, of white gauze, set mend the edge' partly over-lapping, with "eyes" of the colour of the fan, and petals touched with silver.</p>
        <p>Yours sincerely, JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Picturing a 'Bathroom.</p>
        <p>.aist;</p>
        <p>person can easily paper his bathroom with such coloured pictwres as are given with the illustrated week- lies, using ordinary flour paste for the purpose. If the bathroom paper has turned shabby through dampness, it would be advisable to strip it off before putting on the pictures. Before vareishing the paper it must be sized, tne sizing to be made by putting to ounces ot glue (cost one penny) into one pint of water in a jug, stand the jug in a pan containing water upon the fire, and let the water boil until the gluein the jug is thoroughly dis- solved; brush the sizing very thinly over the paper and allow it to dry: When dry, repeat the operation • and when the second coating is properly dried, proceed t 6 varnish, for which purpose pale oak varnish at is. 6d. per pint will do very well. .</p>
        <p>Perfumes used by Royalty. The Empress Eugenie had, and still cherishes, a true Spaniard's taste for strong scents, her favourite odours being sandalwood and lemon verbena. So writes Lucy Hamilton Hooper in a delightful article in the "Ladies' Home Journal." - Queen Victoria seldom uses any per- fume exept the homely and refreshing lavenda.r water, of which large quantities are annually manufactured for her use. 'Me Princess of Wales delights in that delicate scent known as wood-violet. The Empress Frederick uses quantitics of cologne water, especially in her daily bath, which she, as a typical Englishwoman, never fails to take unless she be hindered by illness, employing 'in this way a quart of cologne water daily. The Empress of Austria prefers for toilet uses the delicate orange scent known as Porttigal water. The Qtleen-Begent of Spain has a delicious perfume especially made for her pse from the spice-scented:blossoms oflthe:carnatiOn..raft</p>
        <p>Bread and Butter Pudding.</p>
        <p>• Slices of bread, half an inch thick, spread with butter; pack in layers in a greased dish ; sprinkle between the layers a few currants,a little lemon-peel, and a teaspoon- ful of coarse sugar (jam may be used instead of currants if preferred) ; when the dish is packed, pour over the whole sufficient milk to cover it ; bake for an hour and a half.</p>
