        <p>CHAPTER. IRTo-PBRIL.</p>
        <p>Gwcn arrived at Shrewsbury late in the after- noon, and parted at the station (rout the attentive old woman eerve.nt.who had eatated Wr.</p>
        <p>The latter was anxious to return to Lemlon by the night express, but she went out to the Darkwood carriage with her young charge, saw Gwen assisted into the vehicle, and watched the equipage, in all the bravery of high-stepping horses, coachman and footman in livery, and emblazonment on panels of the Darkwood arms and crest, roll away in a cloud of dust.</p>
        <p>Quite convinced that Gwen was to live in the utmost luxury, and that she would he supremely happy in its enjoyment, the faithful old woman turned her attention to her own journey. The November day had been dark and dull and unpleasant. The clouds promised rain. There was chill ir the air, which seemed dense and heavy. Gwen shivered under her warm wraps, but her eager eyes scanned the landscape without missing an object of interest: Like all young people; she loved change and novelty, and her spirits rose with ever!. step of the horses. " know..l. shalt like .Vunholm Castle and the Lady -.Georgina Charteris," she said to herself, joyously."l never had a home in all my life, not a real home. I wonder if this will be one."</p>
        <p>The carriage rolled on over miles of pleasant road, and swept into the paved high street of Dunholm village, where it slackened speed. Gwen regarded the house; shops, the inn and smithy, and the two churches with the same eager interest she had displayed since leaving Shrewsbury. "How grand it must be to own such a magnifi- cent property," she thought; "to have it in one's power to make so many lives better and happier. To be Lady of Dunholm—what ceuld be grander than this? I must try to teach the Lady Georgina •what great responsibilities are to be her's some day." _ „ • • ao'Passed through the village and turned into the park, follow:ng the stately avenue that led between and beneath majestic oaks to the castle.' Ascending the rise gradually, the carrtage and. denly quitted the shadows of the park and came out upon the open expanse where Dunholm Castle sat enthroned in state. A minute later the vehicle passed into the carriage porch, and caine to a halt. The footman sprang down from his perch and opened the door. Gwen alighted, and slowly ascen- ded the marble steps. The door stood open. As she paused on the threshold the hull porter came forward to meet her.</p>
        <p>Her arrival in the carriage declared to him her identity. Without waiting for her to speak, he said, respectfully-- "If you please, miss, my lord is engaged with visitors, and requests you to go up to your own rooms.. His lordship will see you in an hour." The words were scarcely spoken when the house- keeper, Mrs. Dover, 'came down in the hall, her rustling black silk robe, her fair, placid face kind- ling with surprise as her eyes rested upon Gwen. "You'are Miss Myner?" she asked. 'Lord Dark- wood has visitors, and requested me to receive you in his steaiL :I am Mrs. Dover, the housekeeper. Permit int.tw show you to your rooms." Gwen bowtsl and followed the lady up the Wide stair to the utryer hall, along the corridor to the second hall, off ‘which the Lady Georgina's rooms opened. Upon the 'same side of the hall as the Lady Georgines was a suite of rooms over the state ban- queting hall, which had been extended from the main building, and projected beyond it. Into these rooms Gwen was ushered.</p>
        <p>"Lord Darkwood received a telegram from Mr. Button this morning, Miss Myner," said Mrs. Dover. "Stating that you would arrive to-day, and giving your name. Lord Darkwood ordered the carriage to meet you at Shrewsbury station. I hope you will like your rooms, Miss Myner. The Windows of your sitting room look out upon the park. Your dressing room fronts upon the old castle ruins." • _</p>
        <p>"I am sure that I alall like them," said Gwen, warmly, yet gently. Mrs. Dover regarded Gwen with a sort of tender- ness mingled with admiration. How different was this grand young beauty, in the bloom of her early childhood, and in her grace and haughty sweetness, from the loutish hoyden who was likely to be Marchioness of Darkwood !</p>
        <p>There were likely to be sorrowful days Ia the experience of the young "governess-companion," and she would have sore need of a friend within those grey stone. castle walls. The housekeeper, allured by the girl's splendid loveliness, vowed to herself that she would be that friend. . "Won't you be seated, madam ?" asked Gwen gravely and shyly, pushing a low chair towards the housekeeper. "Only for a moment, thank you," was the res- ponse. "I wish to say' to you, Miss Idyner, that I have been housekeeper here for many years, even before the death of the late marchioness. I knew her son, the late marquis, in his boyhood. Do you know, Miss Myner, exclaimed Mrs. Dover, inter- rupting herself, "that your face seems familiar to me ?, You bear a strong resemblance to the Char- tens family."</p>
        <p>• Gwen started. "I do not mean that you look like the,present marquis. He resembles his mother's family, and his daughter is like him. But you have the Char- tens features, Miss Mynerrit seems to me. It is a very singolar eqincidengc." Gwen thought so Also, IV cast a glance at her reflection in a convenient mirror,</p>
        <p>7,4 1-.)-ei.;; to tell you what my position is b,e,re," continued Mrs. peyer, presently recollecting her- self. "I afii 'virtually mistress of the house' the Leily Uorgina being still in the schoolroom. Any Complaints you have to make of servants, please make to me, and anything that I can do for xpur comfort, I beg that you will allow me to do. You will be lonely here many times. When you are lonely you will be welcome to my mem." Gwen expressed her thanks eagerly and impul- sively. She liked Mrs. Dover at sight as much aS that lady lie her. The housekeeß Presently withdrew. Gwen's box was brought up and placed in her dressing-room, and she made a hasty dinner toilet. Her wardrobe had been prepared in Paris during her stay there, and she had recently acquired two or three pretty new gowfis that were likely to prove very useful. Her clothing was not expensive, being such as is considered in Paris suitable for young girls. Her dinner toilet on this occasion consisted of a navy blue silk dress, with white tulle at the neck and wrists. A white tie at her throat, with a pink rose in it, and a pink rose in her massive bronze ripples of hair, completed her adorning. She had just finished dressing, when a loud knock sounded on her dressing room door, and the Lady Georgina Charteris, red and panting, burst into the MOM. Gwen appeared from her dressing room, cool and calm with a suspicion of wonder in her dusk eyes. The Lady Georgina stooa still and surveyed her " governess-companfon" for a few moments in utter silence. The beauty of that pure Greek face annoye'd her, and excited her angry jealousy. The repose of Gwen's manner made her envious. But when she met the full gaze of those warm, sweet eyes, she began to be ashamed, and cried, awkwardly— " Are you Miss Myner "Yes," said Gwen. "Are you the Lady Geer-. glna Charteris?" "Yes,' assented Lord Darkwood's daughter, "I am Lady Georgina. You can call me Georgina. I like you, Miss Myner, though I had made up my mind that I wouldn't and went out to ride on pur- pose to be away when you should come. Ever since father sent that advertisement to London, I've heard'nothing but when the governess comes,' and I'm sick of the very name of governess. But you're only a young girl, not much older than I. Pm sure we shall be great friends."</p>
        <p>"I hope so, said Gwen sincerely. "I'm glad our rooms are upon the same side of the house. The schoolroom is between your TOMS and mine. Mrs. Dover has had a piano put in it, and globes, and books, and maps, and a blackboard, but I told her I wouldn't have a desk, and I won't. I intend to do just as I please, Misrl Myner." "Then I am sure you will please to do right, Georgina," said Gwen gravely. The Marquis's daughter stared; but Gwen had not attempted to preach, and the former assented with the ,same awkwardness she had before dis- played. "What is your Christian name, Miss Myner ?" asked the Lady Georgina,. after a pause. "You can call me Marian," said Gwen remem- bering that she was to be known henceforth by that name. "Are we to dine with Lord Darkwood, Georgina 1" "No," was the response, given sulkily. "I've not manners enough to dine with my father. I should make mistakes before the butler, or do something</p>
        <p>Improper. am to taxe my meals m the School- ; room with you for the present. We dine at six; my father dines at seven. ' ' Gwen glanced at the Lady Georgina's attire., The new wardrobe of the latter had been ordered by -Mrs. Dover, and the young lady had not been able to gratify her own very singular tastes. • She wore now a pink cashmere dress, in honour of Miss Myner's arrival, or in compliance with the popul, usage of dressing for dinner: but it was ivrirl and stained already, and had been donned ir haehs and the fact was apparent. Her hair;„4es 47,2 'as 2.1 ways dishevelled;.....and the lace at her uee'i bLorpi4r4. Gwen mentally clecidae,. mat tne isbiereisement had told the truth—that the tiung lady had in- deed been neglected. And in the same manner the Lady Georgina resolved that she would copy Gwen in manners and dress. "I foresee that we shall be great friends, Georgina," said Gwen. " I intend to do all I can for you to make you wiser and better. lam all alone in this world, and now among strangers. I need friends. I mean to make you love me." "I do now, Marian," cried the girl. "No one I can help loving you. What walks, and rides, and drives we'll have together! I was so dull here alone, with father pecking at me continually, and no one to talk to. Yes, we'll be great friends." The two girls sat down side by side. They were in the midst of a merry conversation I when a servant rapped at the door, and announced- that Lord Darkwood desired to see -Miss Myner in the Library.</p>
        <p>"I'll wait hero till you come back," said Georgina. "It will be nothing but reproofs if I go down." She settled herself cosily upon u sofa, while Gwen followed.the servant below to the library. He opened the door, announcing her. Owen passed in, her heart beating violently. The lights were glowing in the gaseliers, the fires were burning in the grates. A sombre Warmth prevailed in the great vaulted apartment, with its many thousautl tomes. A man rose up from a lounging chair and advanced towards her. He was. short and stout, his round, fat face WM smiling, ginall; sinister eyes fixed themselves upon the girl In a strallgv,int4M gaze, He was Lord Dal.kweol '</p>
        <p>Poor little Gwen was in the presetMe of her seceet enemy—of an enemy who would not have scrupled to destroy her had he known her real identity. The bird deliberately entered the snare</p>
        <p>CHAPTER, XXVItt</p>
        <p>SUSPICIONS OP PI STRO.</p>
        <p>No warning came to Gwen, as she stood face to face with Lord Darkwood, that this man was more to be feared by her than the deadliest tiger of the jungle. No subtle instinct told her that she was in mortal danger, even as she stood in the warm, well-lit, luxurious library of Dunholin Castle. , Yet, as she met the keen, stealthy gaze of his lordship's small, steely eyes, she felt an Instinctive eversion to him.</p>
        <p>And he —the girl's beauty startled him. The massive ripples of bronze-coloured hair, the pate clear complexion, like the tintinl of a calla- petal, the straight Greek features, the haughty sweetness of the face, the slim, graceful figure, impressed him strangely. Gwen came forward, calm and seMpossessed, although her hedrt quickened its beatings, and her dusk purple eyes were intense in their lustre and expression. "I am Lord Darkwood," said the Marquis. " am happy to welcome you to Dunholm Castle, Miss Myner. I received a telegram from Mr. Sutton this morning, in which he spoke very highly of you and of your relative, the Reverend Thames Myner." Gwen bowed, and presented her letter of intro, auction from Mr. button.</p>
        <p>The Marquis placed a low easy chair near the hearth, and the girl gracefully took possession of it.</p>
        <p>Her lordship perused the letter carefully. "Mr. Sutton extols your accomplislahents in the very highest terms, Miss Myner," he said, when he had concluded. "And, what is of even greater importance to me, he tells me that your family is excellent. Still, that I should have known alter seeing you," he remarked gallantly. "Birth and breeding show themselves unmistakeably. I require as governess and companion for my (laughter a lady—a perfect gentlevloman." A slight c.oud dimmed the lustre of Gwen's eyes. If he knew her real history, would he retain her as a companion to his daughter? She felt impelled to tell him the entire truth, as if in withholding it she were imposing herself upon him under false p.m, tenet, ;tut how could she tell hirg 0)14 rztrn'y of shame and sorrow begun and en(led ha a mystery that was impenetrable? Shs felt that she would rather die than rehearse to this man, with his fat sinister face and crafty eyes, the story of how a wandering girl came to Lonemoor in storm and dark- ness,,,gave With to a child, and later, fled again, to perish on the wind-swept moors, leaving that child to the charity of two old servants. She could not speak of that dishonoured grave at Penistone—she could not tell hew and why she had quitted the roof that had sheltered her earlier years. She must keep her own counsel, must bury her past, and must be henceforth simply Miss Myner, the governess-companion of tt.e Lady Georgina Char teris.</p>
        <p>' "36 daughter,' continued Lord Darkwood, not noticing that shadow in her eyes, "15 not what I could wish. Ii pains me to confess that I have neglected her. She has long been motherless, and I have found myself incompetent to manage and train a growing girl. Her life has been in Maltese boarding- honses and Maltese boarding-schools, not of the best class. lam lately come into my title I was formerly Captain Tollish, of the army," he explained. "I choose that you should hear all this from me, Miss Myer, rather than from others." Gwen bowed. -Bile scarcely knew what to reply, and tin remalare silent. "Such as she fg,i' continued Lord Darkwood, "hoydenish, ill-trained, rule, and awkward, the Lady Gorgina is my heiresi, and as affairs now stand is likely to be Marchioness of Darkwood in her own right. Having been so long her own mis tress as I may say, she absolutely refuses to submit to the authority of a governess. It was upon this account that I advtrased for a, young lady who would be gOverness and companion both in one. The Lady Ceorgina must be ruled by persuasion rather than by authority. I desire you to win her into all gentle to teach her all accomplish- ments. To be guide and friend in one. The charge is difficult, Miss Myner, but I am sure that you are competent to the tea.' 4'1%11 do all I can to meet your expectations, my:lord," said Gwen.</p>
        <p>" This is all I ask, or can ask," responded the marquis. "You 81141 have a pleasant home here, and all the privileges you can desire. The amount of remuneration has been settled—a hundred guineas a year, payable quarterly. I hope that you will become attached to your pupil, and that you will make her like yourself, Miss Myner," he added, a gleam of admiration glowing in his eyes as his gaze rested upon her. " I will send for her."</p>
        <p>He rang the bell, and ordered a aervant to con- vey his complimen ts to the Lady Georgina Charteris, with the request that she should come to him in the library. The servant vanished. Lord Darkwood loosened his hold upon the embroidered bell-pull and advanced towards Gwen. Suddenly he started, paused, and stared at her with dilating eyes. The red glow of the fire fell tpon Gwen's face, revealing every feature with the clearness of a cameo-cutting. The haughty sweetness of expnes- Rion, the noble carriage of the small head, the pure, wide forehead, shadowed by the massive bronze ripples of hair, the thin scarlet lips, dusky pansy eyes—he marked all these anew, and with eyes suddenly opened to a strange resemblance to some one he had once seen.</p>
        <p>"Miss Myner," hc exclaimed, a curious pallor gathering about his mouth, "you bear a singular likeness to the Charterisifamily. It is very singular —very singular." Gwea's colour changed. "Mrs. Do4r has already remarked upon the resemblance, my lord," she said.</p>
        <p>, • "She has ?. Then it is not my fancy," exclaimed Lord Darkwood. "It is a:most remarkable coinci- dence, but, of course, it can be nothing more. The Charteris family are not connected with the Myners. I never even heard of the Reverend Thomas Myner until the other (lay." . _ _ _</p>
        <p>Yet Dord Dash wood looked and felt uneasy. How came this girl withher resemblance to the Charter is family ? " • "My own daughter should have had your face, Miss Myner," he said. " Ah, here she comes." The rush of feet in the hall was followed by the opening of the door, and the Lady Georgina burst into the room noisily. "Georgina," said her father,",trying to hide his annoyance, "Your governess companion, Miss Myc er." -‘‘ I have seen her," interrupted Georgina. "We are friends already. I like her better than I ex- pected. In two days we shall be as thick as pick- pockets." Lord Darkwood frowned angrily. _1 One would think. Georgina." he said harshly.</p>
