      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>BELECTED READING&amp;</head>
        <p>BELECTED READING&amp;</p>
        <p>ENLIGHTENING THE CHRISTIAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frederic Mocatta to k the' chair on Thursday evening at the Ath st um, Tottenham- court-rd., when the Rev. Isidore Myers, 8.A., de- livered a farewell lecture on the Talmud. Mr. Myers is a visitor from Melbourne, who has created a sensation in Jewish circles throughout the coun- try by travelling about England, Scotland, and Ireland, enlightening the Christian (and perhaps as cften the Jew) on the contents of that wonderful encyclopedia which mediteval ignorance imagined to be a flesh-and-blood Rabbi. Everywhere mayors have presided for Mr. Myers awl (what perhspa shows the growth of tolerance most) in some places deans and bishops. Mr. Myer'a next step will be carry his message to the Americans. He is an enthusiast, young, fluent, and spectacled, with plenty.pf the tribal witland humor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>LEARNED PROFESSOR</head>
        <p>A LEARNED PROFESSOR</p>
        <p>It would have been difficult to select a more fit- ting respondent to the primary toast at the Literary Fund Dinner on Wednesday evening than Prof. Richard Claverhouse Jebb. That he can write good English is proved by his Bentley in the "English Men of Letters," while his supremacy as a Greek scholar has been marked in every conceivable way by his appointment to the Regius Professorship at Cambridge and to the presidency of the , Hellenic Society. The interest he took in the British Archteological School at Athens led the King of the Hellenes to bestow upon hiln the Gold Cross of the Order of the Saviour, a distinction which had been received a year befole by Professor Mahaffy. He holds honorary degrees from no fewer than seven universities, and thus has almost as wide a choice of "gowns" as a tlociety bride. He is only just past his half-century, is engaged on a profound study of Sophocles. and finds time to assist Sir John Gorst n t ht. representation of his university in the House of Commons</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>ANTI-CATHOLIC .6NRIOTS IN BELFAST.</head>
        <p>ANTI-CATHOLIC .6NRIOTS IN BELFAST.</p>
        <p>Most of the Catholic employees of Messrs.Efarland and Wolff, about 1,000 in number. absented themselves from work for the greater part of Monday, consequett on the threats made against them by their Protestant fellow- werkmen on Saturday. The few who did appear were chased out of the yard at, breakfast time, some of them , being badly beaten. Before dinner a large company of I military appeared, but prior to the resumption of work they were attackPd with stones and other missiles by several hundred men. It was only when the military prepared to charge with bayonets that the rioters fell I back, several soldiers and policemen being so badly in- jured that they had to be removed to the hospital. 1 Five men were each lined 40s. and costs by the Bel- 'fast msgistrates on Tuesday, for riotous conduct in the course of the religious disturbances on the previous day. 'the feeling of animosity between the Protestant and Cat belie workmen in Messrs. Harland and Wolff's shiparci was furtherintensified on Wednesday, all the Catholic workmen having been ordered off the premises by the Protestants. The Unionist and Orange leaders have issued urgent appeals to the Protestants to desist, and on Wednesday Mr. Wolff strongly addressed the men in the same sense, pointing out that if they wanted to have any assistance from the English people and the mernhPr. of Parliament they would have to behave themselves. _</p>
        <p>TI e letnale etzipioyees in Bloomfield Factory, of which Messrs. It.as Brothers are the proprietors, have unfor- tunately allowed the religious question to interfere with their friendly relation!, There are 200 hands employed at these works, of wboill about tlYetity fioUlact Catholics, Qn Tuesday the ProteStintortion ditinal.PlZ,l that the minority should be ala arg4 kid dated that iif this were not done they, eroultl strike. The proprie- tors refused, and the Monsetlitettitt was that the girls went out on Wednesday 'horning. The strikers marched in a body through the Neevtownwards-road, but they were dispersed by a few constables. The Roman Catho- lic employees are still it work. Mr. A. V. Dieter, has the follswing letter in the " TinVes urgent duty is laid at this mo- ment upon all Unionists, and especially upon the Unionistso Ulster. They are bound as.honest men, as siciddre Ucifekilats, as loyal British subjects, to aid by word and act in the suppression of the riots at Belfast. They are bound to do this as honest men. For every Uniolvist has maintained for years past that the main- tenance of the law is the maintenance of justice, and 'that his one desire is to maintain throughout Ireland the equal rights of Catholics and of Protestants. They are bound to perform this duty as Unionists. For mob law at Belfast imperils the policy of Unionism far more seriously than does the second reading of the Home Rule Bill ; every blow struck by a Protestant rough at his Roman Catholic fellow-workman is a blow struck at the maintenance of the Union. They are bound toprform this duty as loyal subjects of the British crown. We Unionists have boasted again and again of our loyalty to the law when enforced by the representatives of the British Crown and of the Imperial Parliament. Mr. Morley is at this moment the lawful representative of the British Crown. Fie is enforcing the rule of the Imperial Parliament. He has, therefore, a right to the moral and, if necessary, the rhysical aid of every loyal citizen. This, I suppose, no one disputes. But I mark here and there a tendency on the part of Unionists to palliate the outrages at Belfast. We are told of " provo- cation." By provilation seems to be meant the lighting of bonfires by Roman Catholics. If this be provocation, it is a kind of provocation which every citizen is bound to endure. The Roman Catholic al titan at Belfast has as much right to light a bonfire because the Homeßtile Bill has'passed its second reading as I should have had to light a bonfire at Oxford to celebrate its rejection. All this is elementary,buc the worst of revolutionary periods is that it is then that the elements of justice are forgot- ten., My object in writing this letter is to insist that the elementary rules of fairness shall be enforced. The roughs at Belfast should be punished neither more nor less rigorously than the roughs at Hull. Catholic labour- ers must be protected from ruffians who disgrace the name of Protestants as strenuously. as free labourers must be protected from ruffians who disgrace the cause of trade unionism. lam told, indeed, that the Roman Catholics began ,the attack. I can hardly believe it. The Papists are the feebler party in Belfast. Weakness does not insult strength. But I care not how the riot began. Our one duty is to see that it comes to an end, and to support the Government when it is sup- porting the cause both of order and of justice.</p>
