      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>GERMANY TAKES THE LEAD I*l`.</head>
        <p>GERMANY TAKES THE LEAD I*l`.</p>
        <p>"that you had b hood of St. GP ..ain brought up in the neighbour. sions." ..es. lam shocked at your expres. " I wn, • "but brought up near St. Giles," shereplied, and A WAS brought up in second class ach°ol° ooardnig-houses, as you ought to remember, Att."</p>
        <p>Lord Darkwood winced. • ed. " You are unbearable, Georgina," he exelnllll "Miss Myner, you will have a severe trial in Ye pupil, but if can polish her into anything 11k" lady I shall be grateful." The Lady Georgina whirled over the floor waltz, her fat face beaming, her strange figure awl' wark and unmanageable her a rats akimbo, and Le' Darkwood's face grew black with his anger and aversion to her. "Yon may go to year room, Georgina, , n exclaimed, peremptorily. "Let me hope that wn€ I see you again you will have e:trued the 0: nsages of decent society." The Lady Georgina waltzed tnwards the d00r..., "If you will permit me. my lord," said Gwen, -7 will retire with the Lady Georgina. lam what fatigued with my journey.' "I beg your pardon, Miss Myner. CertainlY, are at liberty to retire," said the marquis courtem "Georgina will conduct you to your room. 1 see you again in a day or two in regard to t studies. Meanwhke, use your own judgment in regard to her." He escorted Gwen to the door and closed it her.</p>
        <p>Then he paced the floor hurriedly, his • contracted into a harsh and anxious expression, lips set firmly togethet in an unpleasant rigidb "What does it mean 7" he asked himself. "J Mrs. Dover remarked the resemblance—so it is my fancy. This girl has the Chartens look. ought to have been my child in place of that ts loutish creature I own. What a beauty this Myner is She looks like a royal princess. r Myners must be people of family." Gradually he dispelled the sense of um ive that had taken possession of him. But a new res° had been born of this uneasiness. "I have deceived myself possibly, and Mrs.- , has done the same," he said to himself. "I wit,' through the picture gallery and examine the 1.;01 tens portraits. That will end this wild fano," mine." He rang the bell and ordered the picture to be lighted.</p>
        <p>To be continued.</p>
        <p>A Parisian journalist has interviewed Mr. ci Governor of Quebec,who is making a short stay in lg regarding Mr.Mercier's recent manifesto in favouro• independence of Canada. The Governor is rept- . _ _</p>
        <p>to hive stated that the independence of Canada t certainly be declared one day, and with the conger' Great Britain herself. It was necessary, however:4 added, to wait until the countty was strong euoll6 resist absorption by the United States.</p>
        <p>• A MADMAN SHOUTS TWO BANS CLERKS. A man, afterwards identified as William Ryan, weiglarian at Bear Park Colliery, while labourini supposed, under an insane impulse, entered Backi. Bank, at Durham, on Wednesday forencon, and. "it saying a word, drew a six-chambered revolver, and over the cquntet among the clerks, wounding one Cluff in the arm and another named Brews in the After firing the shots, the man went out of the bank,' the Market-place, placed the revolver to the side head, and fired, inflicting a wound from which 11? - soon afterwards.</p>
        <p>POST OFFICE BANK IMPROVEMENI</p>
        <p>A deputation of provincial bankers waited on Chancellor of the exchequer and the Postmaster-Gse on Wednesday, and urged objections to the clause,. the Savings Bank Bill increasing the limit Of the On deposit per individual in savings banks frcm £3O tu • The Chancellor of the Exchequer held that the iner was desirable, especially in country districts, 10r classes not served by commercial banks. •</p>
        <p>ARBITRATION' p 1m391-a, nisfe</p>
        <p>ME. MUNDELLA'S 'BILL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mundella's Bill to tnake provision for comeill, and arbitration in tabour disputes was issued „', day. It baurs upon it also the names of Mr. As; and Mr. Butt. It provides that wkere a diff'l.' exists, or is apprehended, between an employ any class or fcmployers and workmen, with resP, terms or nature of their employment,the Board of may, on the application of any of the employe"( workmen, appoint a person or persons to act as corl• ator or as a board of conciliation, who shall inquire: the causes of the difference by communication WWI parties and otherwise, and shall endeavour to about a settlemerit of the difference. In certain the Board of Trade shall have power to aid in establishment of local boards of conciliction, wil°(sl furnish returns of their proceedings to the Boar Trade, to be laid before Parliament.</p>
        <p>A few particulars have now leaked out at Wash concerning the proposed alterations in the tariff. A new bill arranges for wholesale reductions ' made affecting,amongst other rticles,pig and stre iron, castings blooms and steel rails, while. ties are to be placed on the free list, As to tini Mr. Springer, chairman of the committee WhiCil appointed to prepare a revised scale of duties, rola "the provisions in the McKinley bill hive, not A plished what was expected by those who framed After an experience of two years the domestic facture is so small that it does not supply a al portion of the Atnerisan demand. The amount 0 plate consumed when the present law went into tin WAS 650,000,000 lb. After the exhaustion °,, overstocked market and after trade had returneonL, its ordinary channels the imports of foreign tiqvFc enormously increased. The uses of this article ha greatly increased in the manufacture of canned, of all -descriptions in the United States that the of the home plants will (Mr. Springer avers) be en inadequate; and it is estimated that the ameurt,e, ported tin in the next year will reach, 800,00004 Instead of 650,000,000 lb. when the McKinley bi M ll'ox into operation." At present it is only intended ts.„ the McKinley five year's provision, but if the a'," Act, which has proved so disastrous to the Nona' party, be eventually repealed altogether, " Tlienyo' Age," of New York, believes that the tinplatebs along will likewise be quashed. Mr. Springer r new bill so near completion that if the Preside,ls, call an extra session for May 1, he would, he 9.59' ready to submit it as soon as the Committee 00 and Means is appointed.—lron</p>
