        <p>NEWfat</p>
        <p>AT OUR LOCAL CIUR°III-1. "ON. '</p>
        <p>OPENING OF A—W—ESLEVAN MI - •aion in The opening services of the Wesileritl j. Ellesmere street, were held on Sunday. Thb' Kidlan, and Mr. W. Brough preached tcrlartp own's!, ,t tons in the afternoon and evening. On Monthly, abrk. 150 sat down to tea, after which a meetmovrawhela..Mr..l J. B. Isaac in the chair. The Rev. A. Ferman, ,in a short speech congratulated the workers upowelu-sucoss of their work, and hoped they would contiimerto, per. The Chairman was glad " that they had got out ol lodgings" and had got a place of their own. Elftmeed- ing to give a history of the scheme he said that a number of years ago, a band of men from Buttermar- ket-street Sunday School, organised a mission band for the district round Ellestnere-strect, and in 1888 a Sunday school was started in a room over a coal yard in the neighbourhood. In 1887, they went to the old srthool in Buttermarket-street, and a society class was also formed. In course of time Buttermarket-street school was sold, and the present scheme was then instituted. They had now 200 scholars on the books. They had not yet paid off the money owing for the structure, and he made an appeal for funds. The Rev. T. Ross, Mr. W. C. Nichol- ' son and Rev. T. Kirtlan also addressed the meeting, the latter of whom made a strong appeal for promises of sub- scriptions from those present, and as a result £2O was promised. During the evening,songs were given by Mr. Fieldhouse, Miss Roff and Miss Kent, the latter of whom was encored, and Miss Bessie Dutton gave two readings "Her last look," and Tennys "May Queen." A vote of thanks to the Chairman closed the meeting. THE REV. THOMAS WAUGH AT BOLD-STREET.</p>
        <p>- • The Rev. Thomas Waugh, the well-known Wesleyan Connexional Evangelist, paid a visit to Bold-street Chapel on Tuesday afternoon and night. The proceeds of the two services are to go towards wiping off the debt remaining on the new schools at Sankey' Bridges. The rev. getitletnan took for his text in the afternoon Ephes. sc. 18v. In his sermon he mentioned that it was more difficult at the present time to obtain the same results in the churches as they did twenty years ago. He was of opinion that the cause of this was, that there was less prayer, and until there was more earnest prayer, they would continue to see small results of their labour among the people. In the evening, Mr. Waugh preached a powerful sermon upon the" Sln of Judas," taking as his text St. John 13 c., 30 v. He said that when the world heard of the fall of some well-known person, that fall was only the final result of previous misdeeds as the betrayal by Judas was the last of a series of delinqu- encies as in a former part of the gospels it was said that Judas was a thief. Mr. Waugh made an earnest appeal to young men to keep free from gambling, drunkenness I and other forms of vice, as most of the cases bf embez- zlements they read of in the papers were merely the result of small beginnings. Another thing to be learnt from the sin of. Judas was that to live amid good sur- roundings did not keep a man in the right path. This was proved by the ' fact of many scoundrels being brought tap in Christian !limes. Mr. Waugh's sermon was listened to with great interest by the congregation.</p>
