      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE.</head>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>A large gathering of men assembled at the P.S.A. meeting held on Sunday afternoon in Wycliffe Church. The attendance notwithstanding the tempting weather, increases each Sunday. The members' roll has now reached 500. an increase for the week of about 20. The chairman was Mr. T. Longdin, who delivered a short address on "Faith and Prayer." The Rev. John Yonge, the president of the society delivered an excellent ad- dress alluding to Christ aa the ideal workingman. Soles were rendered by Miss B. Rathbone and Mrs. S. W. Jeffery, which were greatly appreciated by the audience. On Monday evening, Wycliffe Hall presented quite an animated appearance.. the occasion being the first tea meeting in connection with the Wycliffe P.S.A. Unlike the Sunday meetings, this was not confined to men only, but included wives and sweethearts, altogether the company numbered about 500. An excellent tea had been provided be a commit- tee comprising Messrs. Hayneis, G. Wallington, Atkin- son, Beswick, T. Wallington and Mitchell. The follow- ing ladies presided at the tables :—Mrs. Yonge, Mrs. G. Molyneux, junre Mrs. G. Plinston, Mrs. Gough, Miss Paul, Miss Legge, Mrs. Clay, Mrs. T. Wallingten, Miss Knowles. Miss Pickavance, Miss Lizzie Pickavance, Mrs. Wylie, Mrs. S. W. Jeffery, Mrs. Gleave, Miss Beswick, Mrs. Fleming, Mrs. Atkinson, Miss Gray, Mrs. T. Malkin, Mrs. F. Birch, Mrs. Kershaw, Mrs. Hew. thorn, Mrs. Butterworth, Mrs. Winstanley, Mrs. Rowe. After tea an adjournment was made to the church until the tables had been removed, and the room prepared for the subsequent meeting, which was presided over by Mr. T. Morris, F.G.S. there were also on the platform, the Rev. J. Yonge, Councillor H. Roberts, 'Messrs. J. S. Garland, A. H. Lee, W. Hickman, T.' Malkin, and T. " Wallington (secretary). After a hymn had been sung' the chairman alluding to the P.S.A. motto said hisspeech would be brief and brotherly, but he was afraid not very bright. (Laughter.) He didn't know a movement which had taken forgemen by storm, more than this one. He therefore considered great credit was due te Mr. Yonge and his assistants, who, he considered, were the originatore of this movenient in Warrington. Mr. Morris had been present at one of the meetings and his 'opinion was that they were just what was wanted to keep men from lounging about the streets on Sunday afternoons. He was sure if they attended once they would want to go again. They would also find that it didn't matter bow shabbily they were dressed,they could always depend on a brotherly welcome, and he was cer- tain they would come away wiser men than when they went in. Mr. Morris addressing the wives and sweet- I hearts present, pointed out how they could help tie makel the P.S.A. a success and caused considerable merriment I by saying that if he were a young woman he wouli have nothing to do with a young man unless he was a P.S.A. man, and also that when a P.S.A. for women was I started he would come and give them the first lecture.— The Rev. J. Yonge in a short and encouraging speech, urged the men to do their utmost to double the present membership, which now stood at 500. They were to " please understand that he was not going to rest satis- fied until they got twice five hundred. He also wished the ladies to take an interest in the success of the P.S.A., and hoped they would keep their husbands up to their duty, and success would be secure. Once a quarter they intended to ask the ladies to favour them with their presence, and of course there would then be a collection, taking no doubt Alderman Peache's suggestion at , the opening meeting, that it was better to have the chancellor of the exchequer there when they intended to have a collection —The vocal and in- strumeetal part of the programme was then proceeded with. Mrs. R. H. Pearson, Mr. W. H. Wallington and Mr. A. Griffiths were the vocalists, and all sang in such good style as to secure hearty receptions. The instru- mental part of the programme rested in the hands of Mr. R. H. Pearson, Mr. Kirk, and Mr. W. H. Podmore, Mr. Pearson contributed a violin solo, Mr. Kirk gave a very clever performance on the clarionet. A piccolo solo was well rendered by Mr. W. H. Podmore. The pianoforte accompaniments were Played by Mr W. H. Payton. Two readings were contributed by the Rev. J. Yonge, and Mr. A. Corney. " John Jailpin " was a piece calcu- lated to "take well" and the rev, gentleman made the best of it and received loud applause. Tennyson's " Spinster's Sweetheart" was the title of Mr. Corney's reading and his representation of the various characters was all that could be desired. The programme as ' it began—with speeches—ended likewise. Mr. W. Hick man was the first to speak and proposed a vote of thanks to the chairman and all who had assisted him. Coun- cillor H. Roberts seconded the proposition, and referred to the subject he intended to speak upon when his turn arrived for addressing them in the church—the evils of betting and gambling. Mr. Malkin supported the motion and it was put to the meeting and carried. A pleasant and enjoyable meeting was brought to a close by the singing of the Doxology.</p>
        <p>• TRADITIONA L CHRISTIANITY. A large congregation attended the Unitarian Chapel, Cairo-st., on Sunday, when the Rev. W. H. Drummond, peached on Christianity. He said the thoughts of people about Christ and his religion were largely deter- mined by conventional types of theology and piety. Fhey lacked spontaneity, directness of insight, spiritual freshness. faith should prompt to energy and enter- prise, but experience see med to show that there was hardly anything in which men were so timid, n. conserva- tive, and lacking in origin ality as to the religion. The result of this was twofold. With its desires and affections confined to a narrow range the life and the soul became artificial. Hemmed in by the wrappings of religion they could not freely rise above the Church and creed and ceremony and time-honoured custom into the boundless light and love of God. And this narrowness of soul brought with it as its necessary complement and penalty a narrowness of aim and influenco. Incapable. of complete surrender to the revelations of the spirit they could not in the freedom of self-forgetfulness .become workers for God upon the broad field of human- ity. They set up their theological and ecclesiastical traditions as the conditions of their service, and fondly hoped that the world might be saved by conforming to their standard. Could they wonder that they so largely failed to touch the world's deepest needs, to lighten the burden of its infidelity and sin, and lift humanity with its wildly jthrobbing life, its boundless capacity for good- ness and joy, and the tragedy of its suffering and un- rest into the strength and repose of victorious faith? Of all the errors with which traditional Christianity had been affected, the distinction between the Church and the world, the separation of human affairs into the sacred and the secular, was one of the most disastrous in its influence. Springing originally from a deeply stirred moral enthusiasm it had come to denote a rigid, might he not say an arbitrary and artificial, division of men into two mutually exclusive if not hostile, camps. This distinction, familiar to all of them, was an inheritance from the mediteval Church. It clung to ordinary thought, from whatever side religious questions were approached, with wonderful tenacity, rising in the self- confident assertiveness of the partizan, or slumbering almost unnoticed in the background of their theological convictions. But in either case it was a dead weight upon the freedom of the spiritual life ; divorcing religion from its natural healthy contact with human experience and handing over the ordinary affairs of the world to the play of impulses and motives which were not divine.</p>
      </div>
