        <p>Ma. stk, ON-KARR AND THO DIRECT VETO. Mr. Setori-Arr,. M.P. for St. Helens, writes to Mr. R. Shaw, a local temps, ranee leader, as follows :—" I wish to state that I have a:lv/aye been in favour of equitable compensation when l. 'es are taken away fr no fault, and I shall therefore fed bound to support Mr. Long's 'a mend ment." . PROpOB2D NEw c'UBLIC HALL. The Chemical and Copper St. Helens) won-kers' Union, the head- quarters of which is at have under csnsidera Wm a scheme for erecting new club buildings, to include a large hall for meetings, in the .eee,reof St. Helens. The difficulcy in obtaining a public in which to hold larger katherings than can now be I,:coommodated at their head offie.es seems to have been t..2e principal faster in commening this movement. HOSPITAL ARRANGEMENT. At the St. Helens Health Committee, on Wednesdk" afternoon, a letter was read from the Newtrn Improve- ment Commissioners asking upon what terms the Cor- poration would receive smallpox patients from Newton. It was decided to reply that the arrangement with the liaydock Local Board not having been fully completed, each application could not be considered.</p>
