      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>GOOD STORIES OF LORD DERBY.</head>
        <p>GOOD STORIES OF LORD DERBY.</p>
        <p>Lord Derby's best speeches were his unreported ones. I remember (writes a correspondent of the" Liverpool Mercury ") the only speeches he delivered at the National Liberal Club, of the building committee of which institutiOn he was the original chairman. He had had his own special bottle of sherry, which was sup- posed to ward off the gout, and the best champagne in I the club cellars when he asked for his coffee and liqueurs. Then, turning to his neighbour, he demanded if any reporters were present. "No," cams the answer. " Thank heaven ! Then one can say any darned thing that comes into one's head." He was in a merry mood, for he had been regaled all the evening by all the best of Professor Thorold Roger's huge repertoire of stories. So the Colonial Minister of the Queen rose and spoke some- thing like this (Lam giving my recollections and toning them down) :—" Gentlemen,— I rise to propose the inevitable toast. Gentlemen, the working of our Con- stitution depends upon the manner in which those who acquire powers under it take care not to push their pri- vileges to lengths which might be dangerous. We have to be thankful, therefore, that we have such a Sovereign as the Queen who has never been unreasonable. If we had had suet :a creature as George!, a monarch so silly and dissipated as George IL, Sir an utter blackguard as I George IV., or even so well-meaning but entirely stupid ' person genrlemaesnG, Lorrg I think, and dn youwi think, agree don't wylolaut with me, that we should have had very rough times. Gentlemen, I propose The Queen." On the same even- I ing he described the Transvaal Boers who were negociat-1 lag the Transvaal Convention with him as " extremely I pig-headed persons." A well-known member of the Ministry lifted his hands in horror, and declared that the negociations would now be abortive, but nobody" split" on Lord Derby, and the Dutch Boers were quite satis- fied to carry away their treaty of autonomy. His 1878 opinions of Disraeli was very smartly indicated. M. Waddington, who is still with us as French Ambassador, was named as the probable successor in the French Premiership to M. Dufaure. " I am told," said a friend, "That the new Prime Minister of France is an Englishman." " Yes," replied Lord Derby, "and I wish the Prime Minister of England were the same." Lord Derby could be very absent-mincled,and once on a time he walked with Lord Clarendon, his opponent, and told him all the secrets of the Cabinet. Lord Clarendon listened amazed, but thought it too large an order when he was asked for his advice. It was not for him to coutisel,his political foes. At this intimation Lord Derby woke up, saying, " Really, I thought all the time I was talking to a colleague." He had continued, hardly recognising the fact, a controversy he had been having with other Ministers at the Foreign Office. 02 course, Lord Claren- don honourably preserved the Cabinet secrets; but he told his story against Lord Derby, and made a laugh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RUNCORN RIFLE CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The second gold medal competition for 1893 took place on Runcorn Heath, on Saturday, when 24 members put in an appearance. The light was not very good, and a rear fishtail wind required some carefisl manipula- tion. The following were the seores made :- 200 500 600 Points To- yards. yards. yards. all'ved. tal. Sergeant D. Edwards 30 ... 29 ... 27 ... ... 86 Sergeant J. A Rushton 27 ... 29 ... 29 ... ..85 Seageant W. H. Harvey 28 ... 30 ... 25 ... 83 Sergeant W. Cooper 26 ... 27 ... 29 ... 82 Sergeant E. Birtles 27 ... 26 ... 26 ... ... 79 Lanee-CorporalDavies 2l ... 29 ... 27 ... 77 Colour-sergeant J.Mort 2.7 ... 31 ... IS ... 77 Lance-Corpt J. Gittens 30 ... 27 ... 19 76 Private G Farrell 24 25 ... 26 75 Sergeant G. R. Joynson 27 ... 23 ... 24 ... ... 74 Priv te J. Houghton 26 ... 26 ... 22 ••• 74 Private S. Thompson 26 25 22 '73 Private S. Joynson 2B ... 17 ... 24 ... 2 ... 71 Corporal Palmer 26 ... 23 ... 20 .•• 2 ... 71 Lance-CorporalW.Nield 27 ... 20 ... 19 ... 5 ...s 71 Corporal Phcenix Corporal Holland ..... 22 ... 12 ... 21 ... 14 ... 69 PrivAte R. Shaw Private G. Platt 2l 16 15 16 ... 68 Bugler J. Edwards lB ... 16 ... 17 ... 17 ... 68 Private R. Bowen 22 ... 13 ... 11 ... 23 ... 68 Private R. Foulkes 2O ... 6 ... 11 ... 31 ... 68 Private Forster Corporal Gasket 22 . 10 ... 2 ... 34 ... 68 The above scores will count in the competitions for Altcar and Bisley, and constitute the second of three competitions for places at these meetings. Next Satur- day will be the third club shoot and the final Altcar and Sisley competitions. The aggregate of the best two shoots will decide.</p>
