      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>CHOLERA PRECAUTIONS.</head>
        <p>CHOLERA PRECAUTIONS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Health Committee of the St. Helens Corporation on Wednesday, the medical officer reported that there was reason to fear that during the coming summer there would be greater danger of the importation and spread of cholera throughout this country than there was last year. He recommended that precautions should be taken in St. Helens by the systematic cleansing of all sewers, by the more frequent emptying of privies and ashpits, and by arrangements for the isolation of patients.—After some conversation, the medical officers and borough surveyor were instruct- ed to report at the next meeting as to how these arrange- ments could be carried out. THE BISHOP OF LIVERPOOL AT Sr. HELENS.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon, the Bishop of Liverpool visited St. Helens Parish Church, and addressed the men's Bible class. The class has an interesting history. It was started by the Rev. Canon Eyre, M.A., in 1888, being the outcome of a mission held' in the parish. Commenc- ing with a membership of 173, it rapidly extended,and two years later, when Canon Eyre accepted the living of Tiverton, there were 700 members on the books. On the appointment of the Rev. J. W. Willink, M.A., the new vicar threw himself zealously into the work,and the last report of the class showed a membership of 800. List year the average attendance was 380. On Sunday 720 men were present. The Bishop delivered an earnest address from the words " Quit ye like men." The col- lection amounted to £4 9s. ad. PARA PRIHITIVE METHODIST SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A concert in connection with the anniversary meeting was given on Monday evening when an interesting pro- gramme of music, speeches, and songs, excellently per- formed, was,enjoyed by the assembled audience. The school brass band gave a creditable rendering of the march " Albion" and " Ora pro noble" ; Miss Smith sang' Simply to Thy cross I cling " ; and Miss S. Smith" When the children fall asleep "in cultured and sympathetic style. Mr. Edmonson, who possesses a a gwod tenor voice well under control, sang excellently "The Holy night " and "Star of Bethlehem "Mr. Owen Edwards was successful in "The Mighty deep "and " I fear no foe," while Food service was rendered by Mr. S. Smith whose accompaniments to the several pieces were marked by effective and refined expression. Two humorous readings by Mr. Lievesley and a dialogue by seven members of the band were pleasing variations to the entertainment which was ably presided over by Mr. Wm. Moss to whom a hearty vote of thanks was accord- ed at the close. POLICE AND FIRST AID.</p>
        <p>On Thursday -a-f-t-eino-On-tile-liaic-iie-ss of St. Helens (M s. Sinclair) distributed St. John Ambulance certifi- cates and medallions to twenty members of the borough police force. Of this number three had passed first examination,seven second examination,and ten the final examination. The total strength of the force is seventy- four, and of these sixty-two are fully qualified to render first aid, seven have passed the second stage, and three have passed the first stage, leaving only two members unqualified, and these two have joined the force since the last examination. The police have rendered aid in 104 cases of fractures, wounds, fits, poisoning, dm., and have in many cases been highly commended by medical gentlemen of the town for their promptness in attending to injured persons. 'The Mayor, who presided, said he thought they might congratulate themselves on the general success of the ambulance work done at St. Helens, there having been 791 certificates and medallions taken at that centre since its establishment. Votes of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress were passed. THE FATAL ACCIDENT TO A SCHOOLBOY. Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at St. Hens Town Hall, on Tuesday, on the body of Jllin James Gormley, aged eleven years, son of Bernard Gormley, a collier, living in Unioa-st. The boy attended Lowe House Catholic School, and according to a state- ment he made to his mother before death, he was playing " relievo "in the school en the 13th inst., when he fell on his left knee and burst his trousers. He did not say anything at home, but patched up the trousers himself. On the 15th. however, he told his mother he had a pain in his knee, and went to bed. He could not eat his dinner that day. and could not get up. Dr. Fred Knowles, who was sworn with uplifted hand, said he was called to the boy on the 17th, and found a slight bruise on the inner side of the knee. There was no swelling, but in the course of a couple of days the bruise had gone very much worse, and there was inflammation in the knee joint, with a lot of swelling. The lad died on Saturday night. Exhaustion from blood-poisoning was the cause of death.-1 verdict of " Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>The Royal Laundresses say 0-Itchier' .• Branca GLOBS unequalled for imparting a beautiful finish to starched linen, Sze., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. Corn- wall, says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without your " Starch Gloss." Prom Mrs. —, Bradford : "Dear sit,— lam pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at Id., 3d., and 6d., by most starch sellets. If not in stock will procure it. T. CBITCHLEY. BiBeBbUILI. P.roprietor.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The annual—movement of Russian peasants from the central provinces to the south in earch of work has commenced. Every year this emigration of Central Russians increases. Last year it reached the enormous total of 4,250,000. Owing to the dis- tress in the central provinces last year, it is com- puted that some 4,500,000 of peasants will come south this spring.. On Wednesday night, in Grand Lodge, the Prince of Wales was declared installed as Grand Master of the English Freemasons. AREs Pm Gravel, Lumbago. Piles, Pains in the ' wind and Water Complaints, Diseases of Bladder, Urinary Organs, Stone, Gleet, Stria- XI; uairdceki,;eBy nm cs, ia ti ea , Rheumatism, and Gout, will find a positive cure in lioldrayd's Gravel PUN. Try a small box, and if not satisaed your money will be returned. Priee Is 10. Of all chemists, Or post free for 12 stamps from Holz,- ItoYD's Medical Hall, Cleekheaton, Yorks. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor, and a box will be sent next post. —Advt.</p>
        <p>L. Stevenson, the novelist, has arrived at San Fr ineisco from Sydney. He is going to Mat posa, where he will remain some days before eon. tinuing Ma journey to England. He has been passing. some time in Samoa to recruit his health.</p>
