      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ADMITTING CHURCHWARDENS.</head>
        <p>ADMITTING CHURCHWARDENS.</p>
        <p>CHANCELLOR RSIIN'S CHARGE. CHURCHYARD DISFIGUREMENTS.</p>
        <p>-- The churchwardens and sidesmen for Warrington district were " admitted " to their office at the War- rington Parish Church,on Wednesday morning. Chancellor Espin in delivering the charge to the wardens said that it was now nearly 20 years since he had been made Chancellor of the diocese of Chester, and when Liverpool was separated from Chester, he became Chancellor of that diocese also. He could congratulate his hearers an the progress of the church during that time. On his first visit in 1876, there were 360 churches in the two dioceses, but now there were 475 which meant that there was an 1 addition of 115 parishes to the working machinery of of the Church of England in 20 years. They had great ‘2[ifficulty new-a-days in getting in subscriptions for the Diotzssan Finance Society,which provided for the widows and orpkans of the clergy who died in the work of God in the diocese. It was the fact that the year 1892 was the worst the society had ever knawn since it was foUnded 20 years ago. He also complained of the less number of parochial collections. In 1890, there were 169, in 1892 there were 150. Each collection less of course meant a dead loss, as the amounts collected show. There was £lO2l collected in 1892 against £1.310 in 1890. The gentlemen present, had a great deal to do with the finance department of the church, and they ought to impress it upon the incumbents to have at least one collec- tion in every church, during the year for diocesan , finances, IF was most unsatisfactory that there should be 53 or 54 chtirelei, Which, during 189 Z hcd had no collection for this object. It had been ifugieit:;:: some remarks would be desirable with regard to the mutual positions of churchwardens and sidesmen. Before speaking on this subject he would mention that the position of churchwardens seemed extremely Nicely ' to be greatly inodified by legislation. The Parish Councils Bill appeared likely to pass in some form, if not In the present session, which was very crowded, in the next session, because the Bill in its main features had been favourably received on both sides of the House of Commons. The effect of this Bill would be to denude the vestry altogether of its general administration of secular duties. Whatever administration the vestry had now in parochial matters would be in the hands of the Parish Councils, which were to be elected at parish 1 meetings, and if a poll be demanded, the council would be elected on the principle of" one man one vote." He r mentioned this because, should the Bill pass, the church- ' wardens who in ancient parishes were overseers of the poor,would have that office taken off them. They would return to their ancient character of ecclesiastical officers only. Sidesmen were properly auxiliaries or assistants I of churchwardens, and it was desirable to have excellent 'laymen as sidesmen to give the benefit of their assistance to the church. They were sometimes told that they 1 1 must have parochial councils. Be did not see any abjection to them, provided that they were composed of churchmen only. He had been told that in some church- yards, there was reason to complain of their [ being frequently disfigured by glass shades put 'upon the graves. Of course the immediate custody of churchyards belonged to the incumbents,who would in 1 these matters act upon the advice of the churchwardens. He might say that the jurisdiction of consecrated ground , belonged to the Chancellor in the Consistory Court, but I was left in the discretion of local authorities. The Chanceller did not interfere unless he was invoked, but it was quite proper when the shades become an incon- denience and disfigurement to the churchyard to forbid their introduction ikto the churchyard. Some church- "'bra in consequence of these shades were almost like forcing:grounds in nursery gardens. They were ex- tremely' ineonvenienb and unsightly, and he did not think that incumbents and churchwardens were unduly exer- cising their/authority when they declined to allow the introduction of any into the churchyard. The Chan- cellor next urged the importance of copies of the register being sent to the diocesan registry. He had heard with</p>
        <p>satijaction that a very goodixreeiing had been held In Warrington against the Welsh 'Suspenrory Bill, and also that they had acted very properly in sending petitions against it from _every parish. • This Suspensory Bill was so flagrantly unjust, that he thought it gave them a favourable opportunity of .repelling permanently the attack upon the Church. In ' his own neighbpurhooa, notwithstanding that the church was ratherfeeble, there probably being more dissenters than, churchmen, a peti- tion was largely signed and in many cases the dissenters also signed it. He therefore thought that they would succeed in so effectually repelling the attack upon the church, that its assailants would be discouraged for some time to come. The churchmen only deSired to be left to do their churchwork in peace. In conclusion the lhancellor said that the outgoing churchwardens and sidesmen had done their work with regularity and diligence and he hoped that the incoming ones would de the same. The strength of the church lay largely in her laymen.</p>
        <p>The wardens and sidesmen of the various churches in the district then subscribed to the declaration and the</p>
        <p>proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CASE FOR THE ROYAL HUMANE SOCIETY. GALLANT RESCUE FROM DROWNING.</head>
        <p>A CASE FOR THE ROYAL HUMANE SOCIETY. GALLANT RESCUE FROM DROWNING.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon a lad about H years of age named J,oseph Wrigley, living at Vitriol Square. Earlesto4n, had a narrow escape from drowning, owing to the prompt action of a young fellow named Joseph Kynaston. It appears Kynaston and his wife were sitting on a grassy slope near the Rock pit, which overlooks the canal near the Vitriol modntains. A number of lads were playing on the swing bridge which crosses the canal at this place, and Kynaston's wife remarked that it was a wonder some- body was not drowned. Some of the lads were on the bridge and others on the canal bank, the latter amusing those on the bridge by turning it, and others swinging it violently back. Suddenly Kynaston noticed one of the lads pitched head first over the bridge into the water, which at this point is about 10 feet deep. He immedi- ately rushed to the canal, and on the way he saw the lad come to the surface and sink again. On reaching the towing path he could see nothing but bubbles and ha at once dived into the water without removing a vestage of clothing, and when under the water he met the lad coming to the surface again. He seized him by the waist- coat, and brought him up, but the lad feeling there was something near him frantically clutched at it, and un- fortunately seized Kynaston round one leg and arm, thus preventing him from reaching the bank. Kynaston who is a good swimmer, asked the lad to loose his hold, and eventually had to threaten to throw him off, but he only held on tighter. Matters were getting desperate, and Kynaston had much difficulty in keeping above the water. After a while he succevered in freeing the lad's hold, but he at once seized him round the neck. Hay- ing his limbs free, Kvnaston swam to the bank, and landed safely with his charge, who in a few minutes re- covered and was able to go home. This is the second life within six months that Kynaston has saved at the same spot, and makes altogether six that he has been 'n- strumental in rescuing from a watery grave. He deserves every praise, and the Humane Society would do well to suitably acknowledge his bravery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>TRADE DEMONSTRATION AT ST. HELENS. THE STRIKE OF BOTTLEIVIAKERS.</head>
        <p>TRADE DEMONSTRATION AT ST. HELENS. THE STRIKE OF BOTTLEIVIAKERS.</p>
        <p>The trade unionists of St. Helens took part in a great demonstration on Saturday afternoon. The gathering was arranged by the St. Helens Trades Council, in aid of the glass bottlemakers of Lanca- shire and Yorkshire who have been out on strike against reductions in their wages, for a considerable time past. The members of various trades walked in possession, headed by a brass band, through the leading thoroughfares, and afterwards gathered in the new People's Palace, near the railway station, where a public meeting was held. The chair was occupied by Mr. Joseph Devine, president of the St. Helens Trades Coun- cil, who was supported by Mr. Thomas Glover, J.P., miners agent and secretary to the council; Messrs. C. Rouse, secretary to the Liverpool Trades Council ; G. D. Kelly, secretary to the Manchester Trades Council; M. Gladdle, T. Hamblett, and A. Greenwood, represent- ing the Yorkshire Glass Bottle Makers ; D. Nblihrray, secretary to the St. Helens Bottle Makers Associotion ; J. Shewell, A. Craig, J. Chisnall, T. Ferrall, W. Smart, and other local trade leaders.</p>
        <p>Messrs. M. Gladdle and T. Hamblett, glass bottle- makers, detailed the circumstances which led to the strike of the bottlemakers, and urged the men were justified in claiming the support of other trades. The glass bottlernakers had always been noted for their generous financial contributions to other trades and they thought they had a claim upon others for help in the present fight. —Mr. A. Greenwood, secretary of the Yorkshire Glass Bottle Makers' Association, referred to the partial settlement Of the Yorkshire dispute, and said that they were all glad there was a silver lining to the cloud vMich had hung over them for so long a period. They were hoping for a complete cessation of hostilities shortly, and that the men would have the opportunity of resuming work on the rates of 1892. (Applause.) A great deal of the present depressed state of trade was due to the manufacturers cutting down their prices for bottles. During the sixteen weeks of the strike there had been a loss in wages and in the funds of the society of £lOO,OOO, and for that great loss the masters were alone responsible. (Hear, hear.) Mr. T. Glover, J.l. , (St. Helens), moved the following resolution :—" Tha't this public meeting of the working men of St. Helens heartily sympathises with the glass bottlema kers of Lancashire and Yorkshire in their present struggle, and we hereby pledge ourselves to do all in our power to support them both morally and financially." Mr. A. Craig (St. Helens), seconded the resolution. Other speakers followed, and the resolution was carried with applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EABLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EABLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>A NEW THEATEN F EARLEBrowN. We understand that a new ,heatre is about to be erected in Earlestown. The structure like the old one, which has been dem3lislied, is to be of wool and will be erected on the Mai ket-place, adjoining the present buildings belonging to th,t Commis- sioners. The plans have already been drawn, and the ground was measured out on Tucs lay. The proposal emanates from the lessees of the Court Theatre at Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>DRUNKEN CASES IT THE TOWN HALL.</head>
        <p>DRUNKEN CASES IT THE TOWN HALL.</p>
        <p>_ At tl7l3—'-f-Own Hall, Newton, on Monday, before Mr. T. J. Gillespie, John Grady, was charged with being drunk at Earlestown on Saturday night, and was fined ss. and costs.—John Quinn and Thomas Callon were charged with a similar offence com- mitted on Saturday night, and were each fined 2s. 6d. and costs.—On Tuesday morning, John Ben- ton, an old man, described as a hawker living at Gibraltar Cottages, llaydock, was charged with being drunk on Monday, but on accoint of his age ' and infirmity he was dismissed with a caution. NEWTON CONSEEVATIVES AND HOME RULE. In response to the resolution passed by the New- ton polling district of the Conservative Association, and recorded by us last week, the following letter has been received from Lord Salisbury :-20, Arling- ton-street, London, April 19, 1893.—llear Sir,—l am directed by the Marquis of Salisbury to express his thanks to you for your letter enclosing a resolution passed by a meeting of the Newton polling district, with regard to the Government of Ireland Bill. I at i 0 fay tbuc çie very glad to receive this strong protest igihst the disastrous proposals of that bill. —Yours faithfully, It. T. Gunton. T. J. Gillespie, Esq.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH AT HAYDOCH, On Tuesday night a young man named Isherwood. employed as a carter by Messrs. R. Evans and Ce, and who lived in Haydock Old-road, died in an fully sudden sudden manner. He was at his works until nearly seven o'clock and was then apparently in good health. He went home and had his tea, and later on retired to rest without making any complaint. Some time during the night his relatives heard a peculiar noise proceeding from his room ,but thought he was dreaming. As u; continued,howe ver, they went to the room, and seeing that he was evi- dently unwell, they summoned medical aid, but the unfortunate young fellow died in spite of every effort.</p>
        <p>SAD BURNING FA.TITATY AT EARLRSTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening a lad named .Charles Mclveen</p>
        <p>11 years of 'age, who resided with his mother a widow, at 56, Earle-st., received such injuries from the upsetting of a paraffin oil lampt that he died the following night. It appears the deceased lad, and a younger brother, were playing on the sofa in the kitchen with a bell about half-past nine. The mother had occasion to go into the parlour for a book, and whilst there, she heard a crash, and rushing into the kitchen found her son in flames. The lad ran out into the back yard, and his screams attracted the attention of Mr. Kent who lives next door, and he scaling the yard wall, apeetdilv extin- guished the flames, with the assistance of one or two other people. He then brought Dr. Valentine who found the poor lad was very badly barnt,about the head, face. back, and legs, and held out no hope of recovery. He dressed the injuries, but the lad died on Monday night about 10 o'clock. It is sup- posed that whilst playing on the sofa the deceased tell off, and getting entangled in the table cloth pulled it off the table, and the lamp with it, with the result stated. IiTTIACULOITS ESCAPE or A LAD.—A WANING TO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>CHMDREN.</head>
        <p>CHMDREN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon a lad named Jno. Ault,aged about six years. had a marvellous escape from serious injury. He resides with his parents at Wargrave Moss, and during the afternoon had attended the Vulcan School. When coming from school, he and several other youngsters met a lurry belonging to Mr. Austin, of' Earlestown,-which was being driven in the direction of the Vulcan. As often happens, they turned back, and commenced to swing on the back of the lurry. Unfor- tunately Ault got his leg ents'ngled in one of the wheels, and before the lurry could be stopped, he dragged round, until his body became wedged fast ' between the wheel and the spring. His cries attracted the attention of several people, and a man named Thomas Sankey, with the aid of a woman endeavoured to release the lad from his dangerous position. He was so tightly wedged however, that they were unable to do this, until a crow bxr was used to lift the body of the lorry, and fully ten minutes had slipped before the boy was released. He was taken home on the limy, and Dr. Valentine was sent for, who on arrival found that beyond a few bruises on the legs the lad was uninjured.. OPENING OF THE PITS AT IIAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>To celebrate the opening of the Princess pit, and Nos. 4 and 5 pits at Parr, for winding, belonging to Messrs. R. Evans and Co., a dinner was given by the firm at the Pied Bull Hotel, Newton, on Saturday night. About 90 persons, comprising managers, foremen, and underlookers at the various pits at Llaycitick, Ashton, and Golborne were present,. and partonk of the good things provided for their de- lectition by Mrs. Bond. Alter the cloth had been removed Mr. John Robinson, mining engineer, in the absence of Mr.T. D. Grimlte-Dray ton (managing director), presided,and he was supported by Messrs. E. J. George, secretary to the company ; Thomas Robinson (surface manager), John Jackson, J. Lat- ham, Laycock, Ferris, James, Soutliworth,Drs. Hay- ward and Dobb,and Islessrs.Pennington and Watson (contractors). The usual loyal toasts having been proposed and duly honoured, "The company" was given by the Chairman, and responded to by Mr. George. "The town and trade of llaydock " was proposed by Mr. Thomas Jackson, and replied to, by Ur. Matthews. Other toasts were submitted and honoured, and a number of songs were given, the evening being spent in a convivial wanner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>DEATH OF A VERY OLD RESIDENT.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A VERY OLD RESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Earles-to-w—n-ha; lost another of its oldest residents by the death of Mrs. Ellen Harrison, of 156 Earle- street, Earlestown, which took place on Saturday at the ripe old age of 75 years. Ile- husband pre- deceased her in 1884. Mrs. Harrison until about a fortnight ago has enjoyed fairly good health, when she was compelled to taketo her bed through an at- tack of influenza followed by pleurisy, and she suc- cumbed on Saturday, as stated. The deceased lady came to Earlestown from Ashton over half a cen- tury ago, and after her marriage she and her hus- band entered business, as shopkeepers, a vocation which Mrs. Harrison continued in until the time of her death, thus she may be really counted as the oldest shopkeeper in the town. She has also lived in the house in which she died, for 39 years. The funeral took place on Wednesday, at Winwick church yard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly session of the 'Hope of Earles- town lodge of Good 'remplars was held at the Co- operative Hall on Monday night. Bro. W. it. Dale presided and there was a fair attendance of mem- bers. A report of the open air demonstration in support of the local veto bill, was given by Bro. Ambrose, and adopted. Bros. Morris and Anderton gave an interesting account of a visit to the Good Samaritan lodge, Manchester, and tendered frater- nal greetings from the lodge which were cordially received. The officers for the ensuing quarter were elected and will be installed next week. The pro- gramme for the evening was"itapromptu speeches,' Bro. G. E. Warburton being responsible. A varied selection of subjects were discussed amongst others being, "can a man honestly defend cricket, and condemn football"" Are the churches doing their, duty in reference to the temperance question., "Are the juvenile temples utilised sufficiently in the promotion of temperance work " &amp;c.. The meeting proved most interesting and instructive.</p>
        <p>THE POLICE A ND BETTING AT EADLESTOWN. On Wednesday afternoon, Philip Graham, was brought before Mr. T.J. Gibes pie at the Town Hall, Newton, charged with betting in the street, at Larlestown. Ile was seen by Sergeant Longworth to receive money from different people, and the Sergeant with Inspector Kelly went to him and took him into custody. On searching him at the Police station, the police found a book upon him, showing that he had been betting all the week, and the different dates with the amounts received each day were entered in the book. When arrested he had that day made 25 bets in sums 'ranging from fit to 2s. 6d.„and had 295. 6d, in his possession. Ile admitted that he was employed by a notorious bet- ting man in the town, to to tips work, and he had been brought from Liverpool for the purpose. The magistrate thinking he was the dupe ot the other man, said he would deal leniently with him, and fined him 20s. including costs, as a rogue and vaga- bond, on condition that he left the town at once and said if lie was brought before him again, hi; would send him to prison without the option of a fine.</p>
