        <p>INEWTON-LE-WILLOW6 HIBITATiON OF TIIK PRIMROSE LEAGUE. • ANNUAL MaRTING. . The annual general meeting of the Newton-le- Willows Habitation No. 1,689 of the Primrose League,was held in the Town liall,Nowton,on Mon- day night, The Dame President,Mrs. Junes,presided, and she was supported by Messrs. C. B. F. Borron, C.C.,G. Clarke, and C. Wilson (secretary). There was only a moderate attendance of members. The Secretary read the minutes of the last annual meet- ing, witio were confirmed. In his ,report he stated that the a umber of members on the register was knights 20, dames, 10, associates 220, toad 250. Tfie dame president .and han. treasurer had resigned their respec- tive positious,owing to them leaving Ilene ighbourhood. He accorded thanks to the dame president tor the assis- tance she had rendered throughout the year, and to the wardens also, help which he trusted toe, iv,ited accord ;to his successor.—Mr. Borron, in the absence of Major : &amp;Awl, the hon, treasurer, wiz.) was aiU suff •rici; from the hecident he met with a week or two ago, pr,sented the Au/is:mai statement, in which he showed that they commenced the year with a balance of £lB Is. 4p., and finished with a balance of £l5 18s. 6d. This, tie said, was a very satisfactory state of affairs. The accounts were passed on the motion of i. t att erai 1 , seconded by Mr. Ritchie.—The dame president, said that owing to her leaving the neighbourhood very Phortly cite was unable to continue in the office of dame president. Her resignation had already been in the hands of the council for ...short time, tut it required confirmation at the general meeting of the habitation, and in bidding good- bye to them all, she felt her best !hanks were due to the members of the conned and not less to the warden who had accorded her such loyal and straightforward help daring the years she had held tile offiue. of darns presi- dent. It had been a great pleasure to her to work with so many wilting helpers, and alt'irtsgb she should no longer be dame president, she hopet to 'coatitrie an ordinary dame of the habitation. (applau.e.) wherever be she might happen to be, she ahould always feel a great interest in the habitation, having vv,,riced it up from the beginning, and she Japed under new auspices it might succeed better than it had done in past few-years. (Applause.)—qr. Borron said it was needless for him to say much after the an- nouncement Mrs. .Jones had made. She was not one of those who had occupied an ornamental position, as they had all seen what work she had done in the past. They had watched her from year to year hi king energetic and useful exertions to further the objects of the (Anne. The principles of Conservatism did not come up- like . mushrooms, they were as slow and gradual erowth,and he was Imre when the elections came on, they would see what the Primrose League had done, and they might congratulate themselves that the increase in the cambers at the last election had been 0110 of the, uneful efforts of the Primrose „League. He' wet glad to hear that Mrs. Jones would not- sever her connection with the Priinrcss League, and he. hoped to see bur name always enrolled -on tue list of the Newton Habitation, and he hooi'id in time she might resume that post which she had occupied with such honour. (Applause.) He moved that the resignation be accepted and that a cordial Vote of thanks be ten- dered her for her services. Mr. Clarke seeended and regretted very much that Mrs. Jones was leaving them. —Mr. Catterali supported the motion which was unani- mously accorded.—Mr. Borron. said that owing to the followine• resignations, viz: Mrs. Jones, 11,,v. EL Monk, Major and Mrs, eton, Mr. R. Hood-Wright, Mr. Gooch, he moved that a committee be formed to selects,. Ruling Councillor and other officials to fill the vacancies. Mr. .Catterall seconded and the motion ,was adopted.—Mr. Clarke suggested that the Committee be-called together at once to discuss the matter, and that ,a general meet- ing be called about a.month hence. 'file dame preeident thought the suggestion :a -good one, awl afror thatiking them for their attention, she aljoiirned the meeting.</p>
        <p>TUE VITAL STATISTICS OF ST. HELENS*</p>
        <p>eapnirrireeaselC:enodotr,riiith,nigogioir, noaorbt Ifte: Corporation :hes eoo nn healthlmedicalepa rel Wednesdayt officers oC;i°ttende tocolhfo tejAht tntte; twacelihoito‘ einhof tri(tfeD far.&gt; trot fortnightly aoitnmgr. nual horongh. The census returns showed. the popula- tiorbin April 1881, to be 57,403; April,.lB9l, 7.1,288. The estimated population on the 30. h June, 1892, on which the report :was based was 734240-34221 ma1e5i.35,019 females. That showed an increase-of I,9s2tpersons since the census of 1891,.and equal to a yearly Increase of 1,5C0 persona, or 2417 per cent. per annum. Few large towns were increasing at a greater rate than St. Wiens. The number of inhabited houses was 12,542. At the census period with 12,3fi1 houses, the number of per- sons per house was-5 77. It is now estimated that there are s•B2'per house,, and it is extremely probable that these numbers may yet be increased. I. December, 1892, any unoccupied house in the town proper want eagerly sought after, and in the better situations scores of applications were made for each new house. The . area of the borough is 8,558 acres. The birth rate for the whole of England and. WAles-for 1892 was. 30.5, while in St. Helens it was 39 7Y. During the year the number of births were 2,913 It was al ways very important to bear in mind the high birrh.rate which occurred in St.. Helens as that was so importann a factor in raising the death rate. It misht be stated , generally that nearly one-half of the children born died before they reached live years of age. Therefore, with such a large number Jf young lives, the deatli.ratesa St. Helens must be necessarily large. ' There has been registered during 1892;. within the boreugh, the deaths of 1,505 persons, but some.correc- odfied thinomthoeholyldepigahrtt ofmitaeifnrietimil Asqty. tions had to be made to that number. Eighty persons lliul i.e died at Winston Workhouse from St. Helens ; and three listens;t.l-19436o;r'so'gnhi; persons died at the hospitals in the town who came from outside the borough boundary. That raised the deaths to 1,523 The death is an unusually low rate for St. Helens, and only once during the last twenty years had the yearly death nate b . rawtehfeonr tithewvaesar39:slos 20 82 ). The table' sheoewn fence! niegssca('i;.see. sil r of death included fifty-nine from vio- There were twenty-two cases of smallpox in St. Helens during 1892, and it was stated that over 450 cases occurred within a radius of ten miles a St. Helens' including Warrington. In the twenty-two cases of smallpox one unvaccinated person died. These returns, it will be remembered, only refer to. the period ending December 31st last. o t 34 per thousand. from o at% r ithhge same The deaths from typhoid number twenty-five, equal to a rate the last four years was interesting. In 1889 there were 55 cases; in 1890, 150; in 1891, 185; and last year, 198. O • numbered wNotifications T Typhoid fever was one of the most important diseases from a sanitary point of view. It was an emisently preventible disease, and unfortunately St. Helens had the unenviable notoriety of having one of the largest disease Ideath rates in England from that disease.</p>
