      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>'THE EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>'THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1893.</p>
        <p>Tun Budget which was laid before The Badge. the House of Cornmons,on Monday</p>
        <p>evening, by Sir WM. HARCOURT, contained no scheme of the sensational order. We are not at present living in a time when the trade' of the country is increasing by "leaps and bounds," and when a CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER is I placed in the agreeable position of scarcely know- ing in what way to best manipulate a constantly expanding revenue. Sir Wm. Mumma had to discharge the unpleasing task of announcing a falling-off of the revenue and an increase of expen- diture. To meet the deficit thereby caused, he proposes to add to the income tax by one penny in the pound. An increase of taxation is never popular, especially amongst these upon whom the burden more directly falls, yet all the same there were not a few consolatory features about the statement of the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER. The decrease of receipts as coinpared with 1891-2 is £542,000, and of this the main part is due to the decrease of the revenue from spirits. That is a decrease which will not be lamented by any one who has the real welfare of the country at heart. Then, again, all the figures th;t are at the command of the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER , indicate that there is no permanent decline, but a gradual growth in the wealth and resources of the country. As Sir WILLIAM HARCOTJRT says, 1" the wealth of this country is increasing year by , year. You may find yourselves in temporary straits, but there is no occasion for apprehension or disquiet. The condition of its affairs is &amp;mid solid, and prosperous. The resources of this country are ample and they are always at com- mand." The nation has grown rich, and taxation, compared to the resources of all classes, is, we are also told, relatively light, and probably, in propor- tion to its wealth, this country is the most lightly taxed country in Europe. The growth of the income is a remarkable revelation of the prosperity of the pleases whose incomes are large enough to be subject to it. When Sir nonzaT PEEL first imposed the tax in 1842 its yield was about £770,000 to.the penny; now it is three times as much.ln 1889 when the tax was reduced to sixpence in the pound its yield was £12,700,000.1n 1892,0n1y three years later, it was £13,810,000, or £1,100,000 more than in 1889. The steady and vast growth of the income tax is, as sir- WILLIAM Haaoopar said,a conclusive answer to the pessimists—other- wise known as Protectionists—who are constantly telling us that we are being ruined by a vicious commercial system. If the system had been vicious there would not have been that rapid growth of national wealth which has been wit- nessed during the past fifty years. With regard to the increase of national expenditure between the years 1887-8 t.nd 1893-4 the naval and military services cost more by about £2,600,000; public education costs more by £3,400,000; grants and assignments to local authorities cost more by £4,200,000 ; and other civil charges cost more by £400,000. The yield of the post office and the telegraphs has been unsatis- factory. There has been an increase in the ex- penditure of the post office of £611,000, of which £486,000 is duo to an increase of staffs and salaries. The telegraphs have really shown an income which is £11.5,000- less than the actual working expenditure. According to the CHAN- =Lou of the EXCHEQUER the finances of the tele- graph department are going from bad to. wo)rSe and they are not likely to be improved by the Competition with the telephone. Taken as a whole the Budget is certainly not such a favour- able one as might have been wished for, but it is probably no worse than was expected. The fall- ing away of the revenue of the country which was observable in 1890 under the regime of Mr. GOSCHEN continues, and it is likely to be felt for some time to come yet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>; The Appoint- Mr. T. W. LEGIT, the member for me"t of the Newton Division, performed Magistrates. quite a prominent—an unusually prominent—part in tho House Of Commons on Tuesday evening. Ho moved the adjournment of the House in order to bring forward a vote of ' censure on Mr. BRYCE, the Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster. The irrepressible desiro of the Tories to censure Mr. BRYCE was due, practi- cally, to the fact that that gentleman holds an opinion that Liberals are as well qualified to dis- pense justice from the magisterial bench as Tories, and, what is more, has been daring enough to act up to his opinion. Mr. LECH may now conddle with himself upon having brought a decisive Parliamentary defeat upon the party with which he acts, and also upon being the means of eliciting many facts which testify to the glaring partizanship which has packed so many benches with Tories. Of late years the appointment of</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>FATRY EXTRAVAGANZA AT LATCHFORD. An amateur dramatic performance was given at the Parish Room, Latchford, on Thursday, the pro- ceeds being in aid of the Latchford Cricket Club. The performance consisted in fairy dances.&amp;c:,after which the performers took part very creditably in a play of which the parts were taken as follovvs King Piotimond, Miss L. Brom:theta ; Rose, Miss Sylvia Broadbent; Fairy Queen,MiFs Bleckly ;Dame Spriggins, Miss Miler; Fanny, liar daughter, Miss W. Broadbent ; Duke Falsino, the usurper, Miss Slater ; Fidelien, Mr. A. Willis; Fairies, the Misses Reynolds and Miss Ruth Owen ; Courtiers, Soldiers, Btc.4 the Misses IVillcocks,Marjorio Keynolds,Slater, Mortimer, Willis, and Nlessre. C. Reynolds,and Jack Slater. Accompanist,Mr.Broadbent. Miss L. Broad- bent made an excellent king, and in the fencing with Miss Slater,both performers acted very credit- ably. Miss Sylvia Broadbent, who took the most prominent part, was deservedly applauded for her realistic performance: The rest of the performers took their parts in an excellent manner, special notice being taken of Master Jack Slater, a little boy, who did not seem in any way abashed by the cheers of the audience. There was a large audi- ence.</p>
        <p>BE=MMI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1893.</p>
        <p>county magistrates in Lancashire has been, to all intents and purposes, in the hands of the Lord Lieutenant of the county, Lord SEFTON. In 1886 Lord Samoa-, who must be excused from ever having hem rampant in his Liberalism, became a Liberal Unionist. At that time the proportion of appointments was fifty-five Tories to forty-four Liberals, but so one-sided has been the system lof appointments since then that the proportion has now risen to eighty Tories and only twenty Liberals. There are thirty-two petty sessional divisions in the county, and out of these there is not a single Liberal magistrate in Seven. In the -division which returns Mr. ROBY to Parliament, in , which, clearly, there is at all events a majority of Liberal elec- tors, there is not a single magistrate of a Liberal way of thinking. Two or three years ago the Local Board, a body representative of the rate- payers, ventured to make a .recommendation to the Lord Lieutenant, but, we are told, not thel slightest notice was taken of this communication, while very shortly afterwards a gentleman tho I had been nominated by private influence was appointed, and he was, we need hardly add, a , staunch Tory. Mr. T. P. o.CoNtroa very effectively satirised the suddenly acquired virtue of the Tory party with regard to pplitical appoint- ments, in view of the fact that the whole bench of the county had practically been manned for years by the adherents of one party. He agreed with them that political opinions should not be a qualifica- tion for magistrates, but he contended at the same time, amidst the approving chaers of the Minis- terialists, that political opinions should not be a disqualification. The defence of Mr. BRYCZ, while judicious and courteous in tone with regard to those who had been affected by his recent action in taking the power of appointing magistrates out of the hands of the Lord Lieutenant, was conclu- sive ; and in the end the member for Newton was left to derive what satisfaction he could from the majority of seventy-four by which his motion was defeated.</p>
        <p>A FEW more cases like that of the A Duchess -1-% AA/WAGER DUCHESS of SUTHERLAND in Gaol. and Englishmen will have little cause to boast of their equality in the eyes of the law. The DUCHESS, as everyone now knows, was last week committed to gaal for six weeks for contempt of court. Her " contempt " was certainly of the grossest kind, and was shown by destroying an impounded document which might have had an important bearing on certain litigation now pend- ing. On the sentence being passed, the DUCHESS was not, as is usually the case, placed in ' durance at once, but was allowed to drive home in her carriage. She was then seized with an opportune illness which lasted a few days. Eventually she placed herself at the disposal of the legal functionaries. The interval had been taken full advantage of, to prepare her " cell " at Holloway gaol. it is said to be a roomy apartment twenty-five feet by fifteen, arid it has been furnished in a very comfortable aad elegant fashion. The chairs are covered in blue plirsh,there is an Oval table, a writing desk, a pier glass, and a wardrobe, and the luxurious carpet is underlaid with felt and ferns. There are, too, a profasion of books and -newspapers, and the DUCHESS is. allowed to use her own plate and her own wines. This is how a DucirEss " serves " her term of imprisonment. There are many poor people who, , because of their faults or misfortunes, have spent a term of enforced retirement in one of Her Majesty's gaols, but they will entirely fail to remember that they received any 'of the attention which aro freely accorded the Dowager DUCHESS ,of SUTHERLAND. Under the circumstances it is not surprising that the question of the Duchess's prison treatment was raised in Parliament on Thursday evening. In answer to tho interroga- tions that were addressed to him, the Hems SECRETARY explained that the Duchess is a "Mis- demeanant of the first division," and the Visiting Coinmittee were not breaking any rules in allow- ing her the privileges she is now enjoying. It is said that this answer has not given general satis- faction,and we do not wonder at that. The HomE SECRETARY'S interpretation of the prison rules is not questioned, bat it is quite clear that the rules have been So framed that the imprisonment of a "first division misdemeanant" who has plenty of money is a farce, so far as punishment goes. The poor misdemeanant must of necessity put up with . the cold unfurnished cell and gulp down the regu- lation prison fare, while all that the wealthy offender has to endure is an enforced confinement in luxurious surroundings, relieved by the visits of friends and relatives, and with edibles supplied by a fashionable confectioner.</p>
        <p>MANY clever inventors seem to An "Automatic" Rine, find in the designing of destruc- tive weapons an attractive sphere for the exercise of their talents. The latest piece of mechanism of this description seems to be emi- nently adapted for killing the greatest number with the greatest ease. It is an "automatic" rifle. All the soldier who is armed with this rifle needs to do when in action, is to place it to his shoulder, and pull,tho trigger as quickly as he can, so long as there is anything in front of him upon which powder and ball can be profitably utilised. Like the Maxim machine gun, these rifles perform by the force of their own recoil all the motions of loading. At a trial that has been made during the week, seven rounds were fired in two seconds, and every "bullet found its billet" in a space no bigger than a man's body. It is said that about one hundred and five shots could be discharged froth one rifle in a minute, and it is surmised, mid very reasonably it would seem, that that ought to stop any " rush " of the enemy. Even the most populous country with the most rigorous conscrip- tion will not be able to put soldiers into the field to repair the devastation caused by such a weapon. By the way,the inventor in this case is an English- man, Captain WOODGATE,WhO has been assisted by Mr. GRIFFITHS, a mechanical engineer.</p>
        <p>TAILORS' WAGES AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Mg/ tailors of -th-e-t7wa-;:e"-agltaii-n-g.E. an increased scale of wages, varying upon different work from 15 to 30 per cent. The demand has not been fully conceded and no work has been ,done at ono shop this week, the dispute there being as to one article, which means about ed. a week. _</p>
        <p>WIDNES FREE LIBRARY. During the month of Maxi 1413,724 versons visited the reading room, giving a daily avarage o! 518 ; against 14,252, and a daily average of 528 in the earresponding month last 'year. The volumea issued during the month numbered 5,216, a decrease of 331 in the lending depart- ment, and an increase of 61 in the reference depart- ment.</p>
        <p>A WELsit MINISTER FOR WIDNES. The Wesh Co ngreg4tional Church, Ifloor-lane, Whines, have given a call to Mr. Elias B. Jones, of Lianhairan, Carnarvonskire, a student a 114ngor College and Mr. Jones has accepted the invitation, lie will commence his duties early in May awl will he ordained a month later, probably by Dr. Herber Evans, principal of Bangor College, and ex-president of the Congregational Baton of England awl Wales. CHEMICAL NRW9 AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>It7is stated that a new chemtical works is to ha started on the Minh, in Ditton, in opposition to the United Alkali Company. There is ftaid .tn he amplo capital be- hind the undertaking, and Mr. Davidson former manager of Golding-Davis' works for the syndicate, i. 4 tnentioned as the manager of the new concern. Mr. W. S. Callan- der has been brought back to Widnes to take charge of Golding-Davis' wot ks and Mr. John Robinson has been promoted to the managetntut of the Runcora Soap Works. THREATENING A WORKS FOREMAN AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>John Stacey, of Bank-street, a foreman employed at Widnes Alkali Works, was summoned to Widnes Petty Sessions on Thursday for threatening his foretnan, Junes Williams, on the 20th inst. Mr. Knowles prosecuted. On the night in question the defendant left his work at. 9 45, and did not return until midnight, when he was drunk. The foreman told him to go home, as he wa., too drunk to do any work, but he refused to do so. and uttered grave threats against Williams. The defendant was bound over to keep the peace for six months. SUCCRSS OF A RUNCORN ORGANIST.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Bowyer, son of Mr. Isaac Bowyer, of Church-st., Runcorn, has been &amp;dented as organist at the South Bank Wesleyan Church, Middlesbrough, at a salary of £25 per annum, with liberty to take pupils. The organ at the chapel is a fine one. During his col- lege career, at Westminster ['raining College, Mr. Bowyer was chosen as accompanist: to his fellow stud:tnts in their examinations by Sir John, stainer. When in Run- corn he was a pupil of Mr. W. 11. Grine, the organist of st. Paul's Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>WIFE DESERTION AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at Widnes Petty Sessions, Edward Bilpby, a middle-aged man, of Ann-street, was summoned tor deserting his wife Dinah Bibby.—Mr. Knowles was for the complainant and Mr. Husband for the defendant. The parties have been married seven years. The defen- dar.t was em y ploed at the Liverpool Silver and Copper Works, earning.37s. 61 per week and the wife kept a , small sweet shop in Victoria-street. On the first inst. the defendant while in drink thrashed his wife and then left the house And had not since returned. The defence was that the defendant had been turned out. Re was willing to keep his wife in lodgings but he would not live at the shop with his wife's fainily.—The Bench ordered the defeneant to pay 7s. 6d. per week towards h:s wife's maintenance. FittoisiEs AT WIDNES. - -</p>
        <p>1 At the Widnes Police Court, on Monday, before the Mayor (Alderman F. it. Oos.age) and Mr. H. Wade Deacon, two boys named Jelin Thornoson and George Burro% s, both eleven years of age, and residing in Beaumont-street, were nharged with stealing a pair of boots front the shop door of William Williams, boot dealer, Waterloo-road, on Wednesday last. ff.di lads pleaded not guilty but they incriminated each other in their statements to the Bench. Thompson, who had been previously convicted. was ordered to receive six strokes with the birch rod, and Burrows four strokes.— A middle-aged man named Michael Kelly, ot Cruciley, WWI charged with stealing a belt from a lad named William Wilson, of Crow Wood Farm. on Thursday night last. The prisoner, who pleaded guilty, was bound wver to his own reco,gnisances. POOR RATES FOR TIM WIDNES DISTRICT. The presiding magistrates at the vi idnes Sessions, on Thursday (Messrs. J. Brock and F. A. Stapleton-Brether- ton), sanctioned the levying of the following rates, CM the application of Mr. E. Baxter i—Township of Much Woolton, rateable £20,81, a rare of is. 10d. in the pound, estimated to produce £1,907 13s. 6•!. ; Little Woolton, rateable value £15,798, a rare of is. 61. in the ' pound, estimated to produce £1,184 17a.9d. ; Halewood, rateable value £23,908, a rate of is. in tbe pound, esti- mated to pri..duce £1,195 Bs. ; lisle, rateable value £3.844. a rate of is. in the Wined, estimated to produce. £192 4i.; Speke, rateable value £11,284 10s., a rate of is. 4d..in the pound, estimated to produce £750 194.41; ITitrbuck, rateable value £5,968 10s., a rate of la. 24. in the pound, estimated to produce £348 Os. 11d. There were no arrears except the townships of Speke and Much Woolton.</p>
        <p>SMOCKING ASSAULT IN A WIDNES C/IPI,IICAL WORKS. At Widnes Petty Sessions, on Thursday, Edward Walsh, of Peel-st., was charged with assaulting Thomas Twigg, of 12 Wareing-at., on Thursday the 20th, Mr. 3. W. Knowles prosecuted. It appeared that both men were employed at Gaskell Deac in s TVorks, and had ao argument in the limehouse with regard to their work. The defendant struck Twigg a blow which sent him sprawling into one of the lime batches, which was so hot that a piece of paper could have been lighted In it. Two men named Ryan and O'Brien immediately pulled him from the lime, remarking that be was roasting. The defendant replied "I don't care a— -if he was roasting in hell." Twigg's arms and chest were bare and the Ain was peeled off.—Mr. J. Brock (chairman), said it was only by the merest chance that the man had not been knocked headlong into the: lime in wh;ch case he would have been maimed for life. As there was nothing; recorded against defendant they would give him the ~ption of a fine and they hoped it would be a warning to him for the rest of his life.—A flue of 20s. and costs 14s. 61 was imposed and paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ROBBERIES AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ROBBERIES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Yesterday at Runcorn Police Court, .Joseph Linfnrd, a canal loafer, was charged before Mr. John Brundrit and Mr. James Handley, with stealing a watch and chain be- longing to John Bury, master of the canal tit, Four Brothers. On Wednesday night Bury locked up his cabin, and on returning next morning he found that the padlock had been smashed off, and his watch taken I mom under the pillow of his bed On Thursday morning P.C. Moth apprehended the prisoner at the moment that he was offering the watch in plerig,e to William Hough- ton, of 69, Victoria-road. The prisoner who Alleged that the watch had been handed to him to pledge by anothr man was fined 40s. or in default a month's imprisonment. Samuel Hunt (13), TIIOIII3B White (11), and Peter White (0), were charged with having, on Thursday, stolen a silk handkerchief, worth 2s. lid., from the shop of Mr. Joseph Forsdick, draper and clothier, High-street. Thomas White pleaded guilty, and he divided the is. 31, which he had received for the handkerchief from Mr. March, pawnbroker, by giving Hunt, 7d., and his young brother a halfpenny. Peter White was discharged, and the others were each ordered to receive six strolics with ' the birch rod. ATPOINTIEENT OF A NEW VICAR OF_. WIDNES. •</p>
        <p>I The Rev. W. Hodgson, of Cla.ughton, Birkenhead, has I been offered the living of Sr. Mary's Parish Church, Widnes, and he has accepted the appointment. The new vicar is the son of the Rev. W. Hodgson, who was for 37 years vicar of Christ Church, Come, Lancashire. He le an M.A., of Mertnn College, Oxford, where be took a. first class in the Honour School of Theolivy. He , holds the Int. B.A. of London University. After taking his degree in 1887, Mr. Hodgson became curate of' Si. Peter-In-Bailey, Oxford, and in 1890 he was s,pp.ainteil by )anoa Hobson, vicar of Christ Church, Cistightnn, to the charge of the important church of St. Michael's Clauhgton, where he has ministered to crowded emigre- gations. During the past year the! iN has fsi.eri raised the sum of £BO3 for various purpos-s in the church. Mr. Hodgson is described as a 'Abort I Evangelist. in his views, and he will commence his duties 'at Wi,lnett as soon as possible. The Rev. G. H. St. Patrick Garrett the present vicar, who has been appointed vicar of St. Paul's Princes Park, will preach his farewell sermins to- morrow. The members of the men'A and women's Bible classes have presented Mrs. Garrett with a white marble drawing-room timepiece and Mr. Garrett with a black marble study timepiece and a study chair. ' VICTIMISING THE CHARITABLE AT RUNCORN. Before Mr. James Handley and Mr. G. L. Davies at Runcorn Police Court, on Wednesday morning, Rose Roberts, a woman of middle age, with a baby at the breast, was charged with having on the 2nd April and divers others days secured alms by in cans of false or fraudulent pretences. The prisoner pleaded guilty. From the evidence of Inspector Cooper, it appeared that for the past four weeks, the prisoner had visited a number of the better class people in the town and by plausable and pitiful tale had been given clothing, food, and Money. Her story was that her husband had died and left her with four children in a state of desti- tution. Mr. Cooke, the relieving officer had undertaken to bury her husband but she had not lived in the town Ilong enough to receive any other ad. She had given various addresses, and some of the ladies to whom she had applied had made up parcels of clothing and food and taken them to the addresses given. but had been unable to find the object of their benevolent intentions. Eventually the police discovered that she was co-habit- ing with a navvy named Jesse Fryer, in a state of filthy wretchedness at the house No. 9 Wellington-st., tenanted by a widow named Howard.—The prisoner who said she had been " brought to it " by the person with whom she lodged, was sentenced to 21 days hard labour. Her child was handed over to Alr.Fryer,who was stated to be the father.</p>
