      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>SUCCESS OF A RUNCORN MUSICIAN.</head>
        <p>SUCCESS OF A RUNCORN MUSICIAN.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to announce that Mr. William Coake, son of Mr. Thomas Cooke, of Church-place, Runcorn, has successfully passed the senior exatnination in piano- forte playing in connsction with the Associated Board of the Royal Academy of Music, and the Royal College of Music. Mr. Cooke was prepared tor this examination by Dr. J. C. Bridge, M.A., Mus. Doc. of Chester, under whom he has been studying for some time. JOSEPH Parrciuulis sale shop Is 51, Iflgh-st..his workshop 4, Bridge...L-4de. RAILWAY ACCIDI_DIT AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>_ On Sunday night on the 7.20 train from Liverpool to Manchester, the driver uncoupled his enaine from the train and took in a supply of water at the water tank. returning to the train the engine collided with 'such force that Uic passengers were thr6wn in all directions through the unexpected shock. Several persons received small injuries but a man, supposed to come from Buttermarket-street, Warrington, sustained a deep gash on the side of the head. The wound was washed and bandaged and the man proceeded on his journey. JOSEPH Parrcaann keeps spectacles to suit all eights Fran a shilling a pair.—Adet. THE JOINERe DISPUTE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>...- --- The masters with one exception, have granted the requests of the joiners of Runcorn, an increase in their rate of payment from 7id. to Bd. per hour. In accord- ance with instructions received from the chief Secretary of the Joiners and Carpenters' Association, the one who declines to concede the advance has received notice that the men will be withdrawn from his employment In due course, if a satisfactory answer is not forthcom- ing in the meantime. Jesspg'PRITCHARD has the beat stock of ladles' silver and geld watehes in the town.—Advt. THE ALLEGED INLIUNAI4 ASSALLT ON • SISTER AT V% IDNES.•</p>
        <p>At Widnes Petty SessiOntro—n-Thursday, Christopher Daly, of Caroline-street, was again brought up, charged with unlawfully wounding his sister Annie Daly on the 15th it. 'f be evidence was repeated showing that he bad broken a chair upon her head, had kicked her and then jumped upon her body until she was davd. Dr. Allen said the woman's life was in danger for some dace, through the inhuman conduct to which she had been outlet:Wl—The prisoner was committed to the Assizes for trial. PSITCHARD'S have now stood the test for 50 years, andquality aid Arst-clase workmanship &amp;reedit guaranteed.—Advt.</p>
        <p>\ CRUELTY TO A HOUSE AT BOLD. On Thursday, at Widnes Petty Sessions, Patrick Mc- Cann, carter, was charged with cruelty to a horse, by working it while in an unfit state, and Mr. J. R. Whal- ley, firmer, was summoned for allowing the animal to be worked. It was proved that the horse was in a very poor condition and suffering from a raw wound on the I shoulder. It was stated for the defence that the animal had nbt been worked for ten days previous to the date of the alleged offence and there had been no intention to act contrary to the law.—The Bench di3missecl the summons on payment cf coats and up-in Mr. Whalley promising not to work the horse until it was completely well. te PRITCHARD'S for the best live guinea watch a work- ingman can have.-4dvt. PERFORMANCE OVA NEW CANTATA AT RUNCORN. On Monday evening next the members of the singing classes conducted by Mr. John Ifolford, L.T.S.C., will perform Dr. G. F. Roots new cantata :—" The deliver- ance of England." It ha a been well said that the name of Dr. Root is a sufficient guarantee for the worth of a musical competition, and as the public of Runcorn have had ample opportunity of judging of the merit of the performances given by the members of these singing classes it may be taken for granted that those who attend this performance will enjoy a rich musical treat. An interestina feature wia be the presentation of the c?rtilicates gained by me Tilrrs of the classes in the various stages of the theory and practice of vocal music during the eleventh soadon of these classes which has just terminated. •</p>
        <p>JOSEPI PRITCUARD is prepared to wind and keep ii repair. private clocks from 10a. per unnurn.—Advt. A WIDNES MOTHER RECEIVES STOLEN GOODS.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at the Whines Police Court, before the Mayor (Alderman F. H. Gossage) and Mr. H. Wade Deacon, two boys, nine years of age, named Hugh Jones and Frederick Gilbert, of Cornwall-street, were charged with stealing A pair of boots from the boot ship of Alfred Turner, Waterloo-road, on the 10th inst. Jane Jones, mother of the first-named lad, was charged with receiv- ing the boots well knowing them t 3 have been stolen. Superintendent Keighley withdrew the charge against the boys arid the evidence given against the woman was that her lad came home and told his mother that Gilbert had stolen the hoots, but being too small for him he had given them to him (Jones). Mrs. Jones put the boots on a shelf, and some days later attempted to sell them to Mrs. Fulton, a neighbour. The prisoner was fined £l, with the alternative of fourteen days' imprisonment with hard labour.</p>
        <p>If you want a lucky wedding ring go to 51, Itlom-ov.,whore you will geta. present with it.—ildrt.</p>
        <p>- SONS OF TEMPERANCE' RPNCORN. At the usual meeting of the above society on Satur- day evening, under the presidency of Mr. Thomas J. Holt, an address was given by Mr. Daniel IliFbro wn on " Character "to an appreciative audience. 1i said that their character was what they really were, and reputa- tion was the estimate in which they were hell by their fenr.wmen. He proceeded to show the importance of building up character. FO that they might live wisely and well, and thus fulfil the great mission of life. fle said that to-day they needed more moral courage in the , conflict of life. People talked glibly about what a grand thing it was to die for one's country, but he held that it was far nobler to live. There was more true heroism in %the battle of everyday life than to be in the battlefield once or twice in a lifetime and stand before the cannon's month, and clmcluded by saying that the possession of high moral qualities were beyond price. The address was succeeded by a discussion in which Messrs. David Smith, Samuel M. Temperler, Thomas Whyte, and Edward Key tkok part. A vote of thanks to Mr. Bis- brown was accorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The .Commissioners of Runcorn have attached their seal to a general district rate of 2s. 10d. in the .e. Tnis is an increase of sixpence upon the previ- ous year. Small-pox is said to be the chief Cause of the increase, and unfortunately that dread scourge keeps with us. There have been two fresh cases ti.is week.</p>
        <p>The Committee who have had in band the pur- chase of the Runcorn Waterworks had a meeting on iVednesday night, and f heard that it is probable that powers to borrow .£30,090 will be obtained..</p>
        <p>The family of the late Mr. Thomal Norman have placed a splendid memorial tablet in St. Paul's wesleyau Chapel in commemoration of their father. The tablet contains an admirable medallion portrait of the deceased gentleman.</p>
        <p>Our Radical parson in the Northwich Division. the Rev. Francis Minton, has been giving a good word for the publicans. Speaking at the opening of a workmen's Institute, at Middlewich, Mr. Min- ton said as he endeavoured to act with scrupulous fairness, be would call attention to the fact that the publicans in the past had been the only ones as a body who bad made provision for the social life of the working classes. That audience might say it had been a matter of their own interests. It did not do to probe too.deeply into motives in the past. It was a happy thing that the interests of the workingmen had been left to the publican rather than to the cold and damnable neglect of society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WIDNES DIVISION LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WIDNES DIVISION LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of this association was held last night, for the purpose of electing the district and executive councils. Mr. E. Carey presided. Mr. 11. S. Tim mis wrote expressing regret at his inability to be pro-e.nt.</p>
        <p>The dhairmaa, in his opening address, referred to the satisfactory progress the Liberal party had made in the country during the past two, or three years. and to the fact that they had sent a majority to the House of Commons. It had been predicted that as soon as Mr. Glad-tone introduced his Home Rule Bill his majority would dwindle away, but that Bill had passed a second reading by a full Government majority of 43. They had proved that the Irish Nationalists the Parnellites, and all the various parties of Which the great Liberal party I was composed, were united on the great object of better government of Ireland. (Applause.) A council of 208 members was chosen, and the presentatives to the executive were elected as follows: —Messrs. W. Miller, D. O'Neill, E. Carer, Stuart Deacon, Joseph Morrison, H. Wade Deacon. J. Randle, F. J. Pooh., J. W. W.ring. G. Warner, G. Davies, Wm. Hodgkinson, E. K. Muspratt, T. Cozier, S. Owens, F. Lawrinson, J. Midwooci, T. J. Peters, J. W. Taylor, F. B. Pinkhurst, Dr. O'Keefe, W. W. Goßßale. il• S. Timmis, J. D Atkin, S. Quinn, T. Snape, F. Hartland,' an" Dr. Donnolley. Mr. H. Wade Deacon moved That this meeting of Whines Liberals heartily congratulates Her Majesty's Government upon its majority at the second reading of the Bony Rule Rill, and expresses its earuest desire that this and • its other principal measures inay speedily receive the assent of both Houses of Parliament: • Mr. Hartland seconded the motion wkich was sup- ported nv Dr. Thomas, of Liverpool, and carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>RUNCORN SEAMEN'S INSTfrUTE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SEAMEN'S INSTfrUTE.</p>
        <p>A three days' bazaar was opened in the Institute I on Wednesday afternoon, with the object of clear- ing off the debt of .£5OO which at present burdens' the building. It is claimed that since the Institute has been opened it has been a great boon to the , sailors who have attended for reading and writing and innocent enjoyment. There were a number of well filled stalls admirably arranged and the room pre- sented a bright appearance. The list of stalls and atten- dants WAS as follows :—The Mission stall : Mrs. Shaw, Mrs. Allen, Mrs. Mack, Mrs. Waterworth, Mrs. R. Shaw, Mrs. W. Shaw, jun , Miss Leathwood, Miss Wardle, Miss A. Randles. Friends' Stall : Mrs.R. Abel, Mrs. J. El srsfisld, Misses Smith, Miss Abel, Miss M. J. fforsefield. Runcorn and Balton Stall: Mrs. W. Collier, Mrs. Maclennan, Mrs. Flumphreys, Miss John- ston. Weston Point stall : Mrs. Pendlebury, Miss Gar- ner, Miss Maclean, Miss Clarke, Miss Grounds, Miss limn, Miss Dtiebavin, Miss Timperley. Fruit and Sweet stall: Mrs. Martin, Mrs. Tims-iins. Toy Stall : Miss Sutton, Miss Moss, Miss Hewitt. Fish Pond : Miss Johnston,M:ss C. Smith. Refreshment Stall Mrs.Dyltes, Miss Bate, Mrs. Defforte, Miss Bowyer, Misses flampson, After a few remarks from the Rev. W. Woosnarn, chaplain to the Mersey mission, and Mr. W. Shaw, 1 missionary, Mr. N. Afar:Vicar, of Liverpool, opened the bazaar, remarking that all present would heartily join with him in wishing that the bazaar would be a success. There was no society of which Liverpool had more reason to he proud than the Mersey Mission to sea- men ; its growth during the last few years had been marvellous. That institute had proved a boon to sailors and boat people during the 18 months it had been open, and its scope of usefulness would be much widened when the Manchester Ship Canal was opened.—On the motion of the Rev. Canon Wood, seconded by Mr. S. Taylor., a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Mac Vicar, and the sate commenced.—ln the evening there was a musical entertainrsent given by Widnes Subscription Band, and Messrs. Br inch, R. Morris, C. Chanties, Mr. add Mrs. Grounds, and Masters A. and B. Timmins,and Miss E. Ainsworth.—Thercr was also a speaking bee. On Thursday afternoon, Mr.y. Wiswell, chairman of Runcorn Board of Commissioners performed the open- ing deremony. In the course of his speech, Mr. Wiswall expressed sympathy With the sailors and the efforts made on their behalf, and printed out the importance of that Institute. There was no doubt that the Institute would be of great benefit to the town when the Ship Canal was opened. He was glad to inform them that water would be let into the docks almost iminediately, arid that they would be re-opened during May. The other portion of the Ship Canal had to be completed bsfore the whole length was csnnected with the port, but that was within the near future, and might be accomplished during the present year. When the canal was brought to completion it would be a grand thing for that district and time country generally,—On the motion nf the Rev. Alan Williams, seconded by Mr. Jonathan tiorseneld, a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Wiswatl. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>INQUESTS AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>INQUESTS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Yesterday Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held imposts at VVi.ines.</p>
        <p>On the body of Noah Lamb, 24 years of age, labourer, of 46, Viaduct-st., who committed suicide by haogimr on Wednesday morning. The deceased got up at half- past six to go to his work as usual and an hour later he ,Aas found iu the water closet at the back of the house suspenced from the rafter by a clothes line.—P.C. Cr:inshaw cut the body down, which was warm at the time, but life was extinct. The clothes line was wrapped round the man's neck fivefold and fastened by a run- ning noose. His toes touched the ground, but his knees were drawn up, showing that the deceased had acted with great determination. A brother stated that the deceased had been strange in his manner since the death ot his father and Innther.—The jury returned a verdict of sukido while of uasound hid.</p>
        <p>..... . On the bodyof Thomas Wald), aged 14, a plumber's blower, of 7 Milton-a. Mr. G. W. Alien (Oppenheim and Malkin) represented the United Alkali Co. The deceased was employed at Mathieson'a Works, and on Tuesday afternoon ;ibout half-past three he %vas in charge of the bellows at the top of No. 4 Vitriol chamber. The bellows were connected by a tube to a machine for the purpose of blowing air down to the plumbers below. For some unexplained reason and in the face of direct orders to the contrary, the lad appears to have left his bellows, and climbed to the top of the draught tunnel for the purposd of getting to the other side. Ile carried Ile bellows with him and was in the act of lowering them down when he overbalanced him- self and fell to the roof of die salt cake shed, a distance of 26 feet. He received such serious injuries that death resulted in an hour. A verdict of accidental death was returned. On the body of an infant named Mary Ellen Beech, daughter of Ralph and Mary beech. of 38, Market-3r., who died from suffocation on Sunday. It appeared that the Beech family only occupied the kitchen aid one room of the house, and 0/10 bed held the father, mother, infant, a girl aged ten years, and a boy aged eight years. On Saturdi,y night . father and mother were both drunk, and the former quarrelled with his wife's brother. At tw,i o'clock on on Sunday morning I..spector Pattinsen and P.C. Mea- sen,ger went into the place because of the great uproar ' and found the two men stripped and fighting. At half- ' past two the mother put the child to bed, and at three OA and her husband retired to rest. At 'nine o'clock • when they awoke, the chill was lying there dead.—Dr. O'Keeffe said the baby had been suffocated. _The jury returned a verdict that the child had been accidentally suffocated, but asked the earner to censure the parents.— the Coroner commented strongly upon the parents and three children sleeping in the same bed,and said if the parents spent a little more of their, money on their children and A little less on liquor they might be able:o provide sufficient accommodation. It Was not enrprising that as both parents were drunk the ciilh was dead. He expressed regret that the law did not allow him to purlieu them in st me form. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>There seems to be a misconception amongst the poorer classes in relation to the free ed•icat ion acts. There was an instance presented at the Borough Court, on Thursday, when a woman in answer to the charge of neglecting to send her child regularly to school,replied that the girl had been sent home for her school fees, and had been kept at home because they were unable to pay them. Alderman Chand- ley wee good enough to tell her that if she applied for free education at the school ahe would obtain it, advice which she will no doubt take advantage of. The wonder is, that parents may yet be found ignorant of the fact., when they find such a diffi- culty in providing the schoolpence for their child- ren.</p>
        <p>•There is an excellent institution in existence at Messrs. Cros field's soap Works. During the winter, the employ6s had social evenings, the programme consisting of songs, speeches, &amp;c., and Mr. Arthur Crosfield frequently took part in them. Now that summer is coming, social evenings go out of fashion, and picnics become the rule. Accordingly last Saturday, the first picnic of the season was held, when a large number of the emplcq Os with their wives and sweethearts went by 'bus to Frodshana, Social evenings and works picnics help to unite the workmen more closely together, and when the masters also take part, the, good effects are still greater. In these days of" Capital versus labour such institutions as these are very useful.</p>
        <p>Can anyone say when it is intended to have brass band performances in the park this year? We have had Fix weeks of the most beautiful weather, and yet there has not been one single band to test the new bandstand, and provide for the public the de- lectation which they have eagerly waited for during the past few weeks. lam told that the bands have agreed that it will be early enough to commence performances in June, but surely this cannot be the case. At any rate, I hope if it be title, the matter will be reconsidered. I sin aware that the bandsmen of the town do not receive much financial encourag3ment to under- take a long sea.on. The KIM voted to them by the Corporation is on a meagre scale, and if it were doubled nobody would consider the allowance an extravagant one, considering the enjoyment that is derived b$ large crowds from the band perform- ances.</p>
        <p>"Do tortoises kiss?" is a—question which hasoften been asked lately by amateur naturalists who fte- quent the Park. There are two of these shelly crea- tures in the aviary, and they have often been noticed to place their head together, mouth to mouth, in a manner which seems to indicate that they have a human fashion of demon-, strating their affection. Or, are their osculatory performances simply due to an exerciie of the, faculty .of imitation? I have heard it suggested that the observant little reptiles are simply in a crude and unsophisticated way copying the actions of indiscreet lovers who occasionally frecoent this part of the park.</p>
        <p>Among the collection ot pictures, left by the late Mr. W. Barlow, and which are 4.0 be exhibited in the Art Gallery, are several valvuble‘ originals from the brushes of Mr. Luke: Fildes and Mr. A. Wood. Aa you mentioned last week, the Museum Committee are likely to purchsse some of Mr. Barlow's pictures and it is certainly very desirable that they should acquire some of the pictures of the artistes named seeing that both of them received their training at the old Warrington School of Art. At present,. singular to say, there are •.no paintings from either of them at present in our Art Gallery.</p>
        <p>• A visit to Warrington Parish Church last Wednes- day enabled one to bear the well timed remark's of Chancellor Espin concerning the turning of our churchyards into what really seem, as he said, to resemble forcing beds in some nursery garden, by the introd tie' ion of the numerous glass bell-shaped. coverings which are used to protect the imitation, wreaths and other articles of foreign manufaeture- which it has unfortunately become customary to place on the graves in churchyards and cemeteries. Undoubtedly one of the most unpleasant impres- sions left on the mind of an Englishman after visit- ing certain Continental churches and burial grounds, is the preponderance of so much that is sham, not to say tawdry, not only in the form of imitation flowers in the churches, but in these identical wreaths which are so common abroad, and which, however good they may -cie for the trade 'of the undertaker, are essentially un- English and most undesirable. If therefore with- out wounding the feelings of those who desire to commemorate their loved ones, a guiding influence. can di-creetly be used to discourage and make im- possible the introduction of these foreign importa- tions into our grave yards it will be a step in the right direction. The simple bunch or wreath of evergreen or self-gathered flowers is so much more in harmony with the hitherto abiding charm of the quiet resting places of the dead, while these manu- factured shams, however excellent in workmanship they may be—made of china or enamelled metal— have a decided smack of the xixth century "ready ; made to order"—" save you the trouble of doing it yourself"--way of doing things. PA 4 r MERLIN. eoo. op 'it j;••••. MEMORIAL TO THE LITE THOMAtif €4, 4•-, NORUAN, OF RUNCORN. sirt4,e 'roq 0,4 th tzs • 4'0,4e eet t6t",ve (Ge, ),6pr0r4,(4 '4•4? • (14.</p>
        <p>Yesterday there was erected in' St. Paul's lean Chapel, Runcorn, by the family of the Mr Thomas Norman, an imposing tablet in to the memory of that gentleman. The which bears a medallion portrait of Mr. Norinarr,„% tains tne follo Wing inscription :—ln loving mei-2V' Thomas Norman, of Linden Lodge, nigher ' who died in 7,,rest peace October 19th, 1885, years. His active life was devoted to the welt family, the Methodist Church, and the town oP Life's woric well done Life's crown well won "4 to *I. Rest in Christ. ' 1/6 •')' Av,l' 41. tfie F RODSHIM. ,•ts• to, BELLE MOS= PLEASURE GROUNDS., Tuesday. evening next, the Frodsham Farmers Atis Compsar Limited will offer for sale at the Bearspkaw El,tel, the freehold, fully licensed picnic and pleasure grounds, situ- ate on Overton thus, Frodsham. knoxrd as Belle Monte. The grournis contain about six mord of hill and culti- vated land, orchards, and pleasure grounds, together with a dwelling house, tea, refreshment, and dancing rooms and other erections. The property is situate in one of the most picturesque and beautiful parts of Cheshire. For upwards of twenty years the grounds have been successfully carried out by the late Mr. James Rigby. Messrs. Lins,ker and Linaker are the solicitors in the matter.</p>
