      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Judge. Wynne Ffoulkes.</p>
        <p>CLAIM FOR CONFECTIONARY.—A claim of £3 o.a. I.d was made by Mr. R. A. Naylor, confectioner, Queen-at., Warrington, against William Gill, iron worker, form-rly the keeper of a confectioner's shop in Tanner's-lane.— , Mr. A. Browne appeared for the plaintiff. The claim was made for two,supplies of confectionary received by the defendant from the plaintiff.—Defendant, who ad- mitted the debt had been contracted, said his wife had contracted it without his knowledge or consent.—ths Donour considered th it as the shop was for the mutual benefit cif the defendant and his wife, the defendant should be held responsible for the debt and accordingly gave a verdict for the plaintiff.</p>
        <p>DR. E. HERBER EVANS, OF CARNARVON.</p>
        <p>This celebrated Welsh Congregational minister is to preach at the Sunday school anniversary of Wveliffe Church Jo-morrow (Sunday). It is, we believe, his firt visit to Warrington, and therefore' he is known as yet in our midst only by his great fame as a preacher of the Gospel, and:3y his earnest advocacy in his beloved principality of all policies which tend to the upraising of the people, and their deliYeratice from everything which burdens and oppresses them. In Wales, DrEvans wields an un- rivalled influence, not alone as a man of command- ing eloquence, but a* a, Christian patriot and statesman. In England he is well and wi iely known among Congregationalists, having tilled with conspicuous success the chair of the Congregational Union of England and Wades for 1892. Ife, now occupies the high position of Principal of Bangor Thealgical College. He is a prince of preachers, and all who go to Wycliffo on Sunday next. to hear him. will be sure to be de!ighted and profited. CI: n in Warrington. and for flock to hear him, their csooonta‘livreseoci,iacslt:asttitioli rifl7il;3B(rilletmeileiti being that he will, for them, be at the disadvantage ot preaching in Eng- lish, and not have the treelom of speaking in the jangumge of Wales, which is supposed, by Welsh- men at any rate, to have been the language of the Garden of Eden.</p>
      </div>
