      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Ekma STRALMRS SMARTLY CAMMIT.</p>
        <p>George Yend and Henry Thomas were brought before the borough magistrates on Saturday, in order that they might be handed over to the Birmingham police, on a charge of stealing a horse and trap from Birmingham on the 18th inst. Inspector Spinks said thatin consequence of information he received from Constable Clark he went in plain clothes to the Patten Arms Hotel, and there saw a pony and dogcart which had been offered for sale in the town. Be got a description of the men and placed Sergeant Smith and Constable Clark in the, stable. Yend was the first to appear, who came in at the side Joor in Museum-street, when he, thinking thei In- spector, Mr. Connor, of Sankey-street, offered to sell the pony and dogcart for £23. After questioning him he arrested him, and while so doing the other prisonercame up, and seeing what had happened, instantly ran away, followed by Sergeant Smith and P.C. Clark. After an exciting chase round the iron bridge, Arpley, and past Walton Arches, ha was caught at Kenyon's Spelter Works by Constable Clark. Mr. E. Connor, Sankey- street, said the prisoner came to him on Friday and offered to sell the horse and trap for-E2B. Their answers not being satisfactory as to where they came from, and their reasons for selling it, he told them he would corrie down and see it. Be afterwards told Core stable Clark of his suspicions. Mr. Roebridge, of Bir- mingham, said the prisoners came to hire the horse'and trap on the Tuesday previous for the day, for which they paid him 10s. The next morning he gave a description of the horse and trap, and the prisoners, at the Birming- ham Police Station. Inspector Monk, Birrainltham, said 'Vend had been convicted for house breaking previously. He made an application for them to be handed over to the Birmingham police. The application was granted. On Monday at Birmingham Police Court the prisoners were committed to the sessions. THEFT OF A WATCH.</p>
        <p>Robert Smith was charged at the Borough Court, on Monday, with offering a watch for pledge on the 21st inst., without giving a satisfactory account of how it came into his possession. The Chief Constable said that prisoner had been remanded from Saturday, and the ,wner of the watch had been found to be Frederick Jas. ,ice, engine driver on the Ship Canal at Parting,fton, a saw the watch safe in his waistcoat poclaet on %37. Defendant was a lahrsurer occasionally em- 'n the Ship Canal. The right number of the ,ad been given by Joie° before he saw the watch. !price given by Joice,prisoner was handed over .asiaire police. BEER AND BOOTS.</p>
        <p>PetTiiestsin-ns,-Orn-fuesday, Geo.Bdwers ith stealing on the 23rd March. a pair of 6d., the property of Charles Heaton,the ry'Arms, Grappenhall. From the evi- that the prisoner went in the back I use to the kitchen, when he had I were afterwards missed,and it was I t had sold them in Warrington.— ' ,nd dealer, Mersey-street, War- came into his shop with the I m 51. 6J.—P.C. Millinaton ith Mr. Heaton to Leslie's I a him a description of the n said he had never bought •eks past. On looking round overhead,and said," These Imitted buying them from As sent to gaol for one n) said they considered isfactory, and if it was üble.—Superintendent night not receive his mduct had been any- lice. Such conduct • . The Bench dis- HOUSE.</p>
        <p>e Borough Court, lodging-house of e defendant with intiff for about Inaghan was in he called him awnticket on for is. 6d. at reet, in the olice station house, and y about it. 81mi it be- )ledged a issed the to her. er bus- ithout is the is as ,uds ere</p>
        <p>three friends. He asked Mr. Walsh for three glasses of beer,and Mr. Walsh served him and charged him 41d for it. Witness asked if that was the best beer, and several other things were said about the beer, when Walsh said he was nr.t going to have any bother and he must clear off the premises. Walsh came across the bar, catight ' hold of him by the coat, and threw him in the back yard. He then pitched him against the paving stones. Witness then'went again for the beer he had paid for, and Mrs. Walsh gave it to him. After evidence by Joseph Cortis and Michael Brennan, Mr. Browne said that his case was that Blake came into the house and en being served with the beer, he held it up and said it was bad beer. He made a disturbance, and Mr. Walsh told him to leave the house, hut he refused, and said he would have Mr. Walsh out of the place in three months. Mr. Walsh then put him out, and Blake struck him in the face. Mr. Walsh denied committing the assault complained of by Blake. Mr. Walsh and Mr. Greaves each gave evidence, after which the magistrates dis- missed each case, ordering each party to pay their own costs.</p>
