      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Mr. John Burgess (in the chair) and Mr. L. Voisey. SLEEPING Our.—William Leach was charged with sleeping on the premises of Mr. Kenworthy, timber merchant, on Friday night. Mr. Kenworthy said the prisoner had pulled down the damper of the boiler evidently for the purpose of making himself warm, which had delayed work that morning for about an hour. Inspector Spinks said he found the prisoner in the fire- hole. He was perfectly sober. As it was the prisoner's first offence, and Mr. Taylor (P.C.151.) offered to pay his lodging money until be got work he was discharged. A DRUNKEN DRIVER.—John Baguley was charged with being drunk whilst in charge of a horse and cart on the 25th March, in Sankey-street. The prisoner drove into a hansom cab and damaged it a little. He was sent to gaol for 14 days hard labour. MONDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Francomb, and Messrs. F. Monks and S. Wright. FRAUDULENT RE-EIrLIST3II3NT.—SAIIMeI Hirst WAS charged with fraudulently re-enlisting in the Third Manchester Militia in December last. although belonging to the Fourth Lancashire Regiment. Prisoner was re- manded. On Thursday, the prisoner was again brought up, and sent to prison for one month:with hard labour. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Joseph Warburton, Win- wick•street, on Saturday, fined 5s and costs or seven days ; James Smith, Winwick-streei, on Sunday; ss. in- cluding costs or seven days; John Kelly, in the Market Place on the 15th inst ,sa. and costs or seven days. ' _ .</p>
        <p>- CHIMNEY orkFzaz.—For this offence John Atkins was fined is.</p>
        <p>TRANSFERS OF LICENSES.—The following transfers were applied for and granted :—Frank Neild to the Ring o' Bells, Church-street, Joseph Henry James to the Leigh Arms, Mersey-street, Elecry Charles Bennett to a beer house in Hall-street, John Armstrong, to a beer house in Boyle-street. ROGUES AND VADABONDS.—John Whittaker was charged with being a rogue and vagabond and with slcepisag on the premises of the Golden Grove Inn on Sunday morn- ing. Prisoner was fined ss. including costs or seven days.—Samuel [Wise was charged with being a rogue and vagabond and with being in the enclosed premises of the Warringten Union Workhouse, on Sunday morn- ing, with intent to commit a felony. Mr. Curry, Union janitor, said the prisoner had been in the Union for two years previous to the present month, when he left to look for work. On Sunday morning at about two o'clock, he climbed over the wall, and came to the re- ceiving ward, where the man in charge let him in. Alderman Francemb "He evidently liked it so well during the two years, that he wanted to come again." (Laughter.' The magistrates dismissed the case on his promising to look for work.</p>
        <p>TIIESDAL—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), Mr. E. J. Guest and Dr. Starkey Smith. HE MISSED TM&amp; 'Bus.—Thomas Daley was charged with sleeping in a hay shed in Lower Wash-lane on Monday night. P.C. Delahav gave evidence after which prisoner stated that he missed the 'bus to get into Warrington, and that was the reason for going into the hay shed to sleep.—Prisoner was dismissed on promising nut to iepeat the offence. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (in the chair) Mr. J. Geddes and Councillor J.H.Fleming.</p>
        <p>. _ CHARGE OF INDECENT fissAuzr,—John Sil;erwood was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Butter- market-st., and also with indecently assaulting Sarah Wilson, a married woman, at Gallop's lodzing-house, at 6.30 on the previous night. Sergeant Taylor said he received information as to the assault from Sarah Wil- son and met the prisoner, drunk in Buttermarket-st., abd he charged him with the offence, when he said it was not true. Sarah Wilson gave evidence as to the assault which was ccrroborated by Elizabeth Butler. The defendant called three witnesses who contended that no assault had been committed. The magistrates con- sidered the evidence of a confliceing nature and dismissed prisoner on the charge of assault, but fined him ss. and costs for being drunk.</p>
        <p>Tnunstay.:7l3efore_Alderman Chandley. (in the ch.?),</p>
        <p>Alderman Sutton and Captain Ridgway. SCHOOL CASES.-Mr. Riley prosecuted in the following cases, for not sending their children regularly to school'. William Leyland, Bowcock-street, ss; John Coyne, labourer, Bank Quay, 2s ed ; Thomas Cummings, labourer, Cockhedge. 2s 6d ; W,n. Finch, hawker, Bew- sey-road, sa; John Jackson, Friar's Green, 24 ed ; Mary Ann Meddlecot, allowed the Bench to give her son six strokes with the birch rod as he had played truant since the summons was sent. Edward Horan], Latchford promised to send his daughter to an elementary school, and the case was adjourned for a fortnight. Edward Stacey, forgeman, Bewsey-road, 20 ed.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Wright (chairman), Dr. Gornall, Councillor Roberts and Mr. William Pier- point. A STRANGE COITPLE.—Ann Lewis, 50, Eldon-street, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Eldon- street on Ihursday. P.C. Baxendale said that prisoner was creating a disturbance on the previous morning, and he brought her down to the police station. On be- ing searched a razor and a bottle of laudanum was found in her possession, Joseph Birchall in corroborating the officer's evidence said that he lived with prisoner. Mr. Hunt asked why he did not marry her, but he said that she was ft awful," and he knew better than do that. Prisoner said that she had got the laudanum for her 1 tooth, and the razor to cut her corns. Alderman Wright said that being in possession of these articles I looked very suspicious and prisoner and Birchall were 1 living a dig :raceful life. Prisoner would be fined ss. and costs or seven days. . .</p>
        <p>MAINTENANCB ORDER.-Wm Hy. Nash, Derby-st., Earlestown was ordered to pay 2s. per week towards the maintenance of his father. •</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO HORSES.—George Bowers, was charged with cruelty to a horse by working it while in an unfit state, on the 19th inst., and Wm. Hy. Palmer was charged with causing the same to be worked. Inspector Dowty said that on the 19th inst., he saw Bowers going towards the Central Station with a horse which was very lame. Witness followed, and examined the horse at the station. He found its fetlock in a bad state, and the frog was almost eaten away, and the'animal was stiffer- ing from great pain. Bowers said that he had been sent by Palmer who is the horse keeper at Connors. Palmer had since tried to settle the matter. Palmer said that no blame whatever was attached to Bowers as he had gone out with the horse by his (Palmer's) orders. The horse would not have been sent out if it had been known how bad it was. The magistrates fined Palmer 203. including costs and dismissed the case against Bowers.— Wm. Mather, Mal dealer,. Foundry-at, was charged with committing a similar offence on the 20th inst. P.C. Turner, anct Inspector Dowty proved the case, and che defendant was fined 10s. including costs.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Before Dr. Steele and Mr. P. Speakman.</p>
        <p>MISCELLANEOVS.—EIIen Langford, &amp;ion-street, was ined ss. and costs for using obscene ianguage.—Ellen 'arker, James Robinson and Edward Lanette,. were each</p>
        <p>_ ied 53. and costs for drunkenness. For fighting with nan named Mullen in Mr. Jonathan Hossetield's field, Sunday 26th March, Baker Hunt was bound over to the peace and pay 14s. 4d. costs.—Ellen Coffey, a Maracter, was sentenced to a month's hard labour decent conduct. ' ALLEGED WIFE DESERTlON.—Frederick Ellison, mith, Stretford, was summoned for deserting his d family at Halton. The case had been partially t the previous sessions ; it had again to be d because the magistrates had important ,nts to fulfil. May 10th was the day fixed for EUPTED SUICIDE.—Efenry Massey, aged 16, brought up on a charge of attempting to lidos by taking poison. He had been an workhouse for a week and Superintendent Ihe might be now discharged with promis- at the offence—The Beach discharged the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ISBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>ISBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. C. Farr, C.C., (in the chair), 7 Neild, W. Long, and H. Talbot. 3IIDEULY.—Martin Higgins, labourer, •I Morris Roe, labourer, Stockham, including costs for being drunk and Brook on the 25th March. "arnes Forster, 17, Allen-street, War- ined is. including costs for riding on the night of March 4th nt N. Hutchins, Lindhurst, Altrin- le on the footpath at Newton on including costa. , ;mtg.—Timothy Brannon, 10, was charged with allowing at Grappenhall on the 23rd name of the owner engraved led is. and costs. Albert for allowing a dog to be at lth April was fined a like • shout a license on thc- 22nd r, Cross-lane, Latchford</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDA V, APRIL 29, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDA V, APRIL 29, 1893.</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTON TREspassEn.--Timothy Brannon, 10, Hall-street, Warrington, was charged with trespassing in pursuit of game on the premises of Mr. J. C. Parr on the 23rd March. P.C. Millington said that at 7.40 p.m. on the day in question he saw the defendant with three greyhounds on the land of Mr. J. Charlton Parr. Os seeing the constable he commenced running and hurry- ing the dogs on, when the constable gave chase and caught him. The defendant said he only owned one dog, the ot era belonged to another person and he refused to come and give evidence because he might get summoned. He was fined ss. including costs. SLEEPY Detyzes.—Samuel Johnson, Latchford, was fined 53. including costs for being asleep whilst in charge of a lurry and two horses at Moore on the 13th March. Thomas Topham, of Warrington, was charged with being asleep at Walton Superior, in charge of a horse and cart belonging to Mr. Newton, contractor, Warring- ton. P.C. Shetwell said he met the defendant on the day in question and stopped the horse. He asked him for his name and address, when he said his name was I Williams, and lived at Newton, whereas his name was Topham, and he lived at Cross-street, off Orford-lane, Warrington. He (the constable) had great difficulty in finding him. The magistrates thought it a bad case, and as a warning te others, fined him 103. and costs.</p>
        <p>SALFORD "A" v. mons "A."—At Widnes, on Satur- day, in beautiful weather. Seither aide was fully repre- sented. After a pleasant game, in which the visitors quite outclassed the home team, the result was a win for Salford by two goals and one try to one try.</p>
        <p>"Mr. J. Woodcock, 177 Scotland Road, says that St. Jacobs Oil is the most popular remedy in the trade." a</p>
        <p>4 "Air. John— Girvan, 289 and 345 Scotland Road, never yet heard a customer say that St. Jacobs Oil would not do all the proprietors claim for it." " Mr. W. Willi..mvon, proprietor, North Apothe- caries Hail, 39r Scotland Road, says he is simply as- tounded at the enormous sale which St. Jacobs Oil has, and which increases every month."</p>
        <p>" Th,ma; IVhite, Stanley Pharmacy, 585 Stanley Road, says that for rheumatism, neuralgia, and all bodily pains where an outward application is required, St. Jacobs Oil is undoubtedly a certain specific." 13w/den 6-4 Corntany, Kirkdale, 399 Stanley* Road, say that they are personally acquainted with many ens-s where the use of St. Jacobs Oil has cured people who have been given up by local medical men to die. The case of Mr. 'William Buchanan, the Cunard s.s. Company's Engineer, is a sample. Mrs. Buchinan first bought the Oil from this shop when she did not expect her huLband to live an hour. He had been given up to die by six of the best medical men in Liverpool. His case was intense neuralgia in the head, which was cured by the contents of less than three bot- tles of the Oil. That was more than three years ago, and he has been a well man ever since."</p>
        <p>"a" Mr. J. C. Caley, Post Office, 453 Stanley Road, says that he sells hundreds of bottles of St. Jacobs Oil every month: all his customers say it is grand stuff for bodily pains, including neuralgia and rheumatism."</p>
        <p>tere_a representative of</p>
        <p>the Charles A. Vo,geler Company, the well known proprietors of St. Jacobs Oil. The reporter, learning that the Company's representative was down from London for the sole pur- pose of verifying certain written statements which they had received in London from Liverpool, askycl permis- sion to accompany him on his round of investigation, which was cheerfully granted, and the following result was sent off the same evening to the Manchester paper indicated and published the following morning :—" A carriage being called, the reporter and the Company's representative drove first to the house of Miss C. A. J. Vernon, 8 Homer Street, Toxteth Park, who it appears had been unsuccessfully treated for more than a year by six different doctors for intense neuralgic pains in the head. Miss Vernon had been given up by her family as a hopeless invalid, she had suffered the most intense agony, was unable to sleep, and no change of medicine seemed to have any effect whatever upon her. In this extremity she procured St. Jacobs Oil, and per- sonally stated to us that after the second application the pain was eased to that extent that she could sleep, and said that although the contents of the first bottle were not yet finished, the pain had entirely disappeared, and she felt she had a new lease of life. Miss Vernon's father and herself seemed full of gratitude to the pro-. prietors.of St. Jacobs Oil. "Mr. James Mcßeath, 30 Clark Street, Toxteth Park, we found had suffered for years from rheumatism in the hands to that extent that they had become almost use- less. He was unable to obtain any relief until he used St. Jacobs Oil, when the pain disappeared and his hands assumed their normal shape and condition, and although this was more than a year ago there were no indicauces of a return of the trouble.</p>
        <p>at. Jacobs Oil an her chemist, Mr. Cluett. 95 St. James' Street, the application of which eased - the pain. Procuring two more bottles from the same source, she ap- plied the contents, with the result that the pain had entirely disap- peared ; she could walla as well as ever, is in and considers.So the greatest rheumatic' the world. - ler further to verify Mrs. S statement, we drove round Cluett's place of Lusbless, heartily endorsed the above, stated that Mrs. Watson's case s only one of the many cases daich had come under his Mune- observation, where rre*t mira. culous cures bad been wrought by the use of St. Jacobs Oil. "The above are only. a few of the many people</p>
        <p>. . whom the Vogeler Com- pany's representative and If called upon, all of whom from personal ex- perience spoke in the highest praise of .'St. Jacobs Oil as a pain reliever. In many cases the almott instant relief and permanent cure effected were simply marvel- lous. It appears that this popular remedy is used literally speaking by everybody in Liverpool. the name of St. Jacobs Oil seems to be a household word there.</p>
        <p>"It appears that all of the great steamship companies. such as the Cunard, White Star, Inman, and National Lines, never consider their ships ready for sea without a liberal supply of the Oil on board. We-learned from the officials of the North Western Railway Company that St. Jacobs Oil is largely used among the ...A, of that popular line. At Crewe, where the Company's extensive works are located, it is said that thousands of bottles of *. Jacobs Oil are used among :Mc work. people every year. and some of the leadim; :11,•tehant5 in Crewe state that they would no more think of being out of •St. Jacobs Oil than they would of beial out of tea and sugar. We were informed that • all along the line of the London and North Western Railway, the Oil in yellow wrappers was used on the Company's horses for lameness, sprains, &amp;c., with the best possible results.</p>
        <p>"We leamed from the offieials of the Midland RA way and Lancashire and Yorkshire that large quantities of the OH are used annually by the empioyes of these lines.</p>
        <p>" All of the chemists and druggists whom we called upon invariably stated that St. Jacobs Oil was the most popular and had the largest sale of any Proprietary Medicine, and ten times the sale of all other liniments and embrocations combined."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the head office, Bewsey-street. Warrington, not later than ten o'clock on Wednesday monaing.</p>
        <p>CRICICETBRS. Lawn Tennisers. and Golfers are reminded that GEO. G. BUSS Y &amp; Co. mannfacturs high-olass requisites. Their new catalogue is obtainable from Local Dealers, or the Sports Manufactory, Peckham Eye, S.E. Trade 4E-Eff&lt;4 Mark.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON JUNIOR CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH V. WALSER'S BREWERY.—These teams met on the ground of the first-named club on Saturday, before a large number of spectators. Special interest attached to the match, for the Churches had only to win to establish themselves champions for the present year, which is the first of the competition. After the form shewn by the Parish the previous Saturday against Stockton Heath, their victory in the present engagement was looked upon as assured, and the expectations were fully justified. Half-time arrived with the homesters leading by 13 points to nil, and with this substantial majority they rested content, formn the second half they eased up, satisfying themselves with preventing their opponents from scoring. Final score :—Parish Church, 1-4-3; Brewery, 0-0-2, Referee, Mr. J. T. Bate, W.F.C.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN V. WIGAN.-It was unfortunate for Run- corn they were not able to send a representative team to Wigan on Saturday, several " A" teamers being in- cluded in the contingent. The home team were assisted by Sudlow, of St. Helens. In the opening half the " myrtlegreens "showed up very well, the forwards in particular being smart, and they had the best of the game. It was just on the interval" though, before a score was made, J. Faulkner running cleverly round the scruminage,and grounding near the posts. Nothing, however, accrued horn the place kick, the ball being charged down. On change of ends the play veered round also. Runcorn being forced to act on the defen- sive. This they did successfully tor a time, but at length, from a pass by Halliwell, Whitehead ran in a splendid try. Not long afterwards, in a capital dribble headed by Unsworth, Arkwright headed another, but, as neither point as improved, Wigan retired victorious by two tries to one. The game was a fairly interesting I one, but the heat told a tale on the players. The win- ners were best represented by Unsworth, Barnwell, Whitehead and Trow, and on the other side the most noticeable were Butterworth, Crompton, Houghton, and Faulkner.</p>
        <p>Ruwcotor v. Wrotar "A " Tzems.—Played at Runcorn, on gaturclay. Neither team was fully represented, al- though Wigan had the assistance of Potts and Lally, of the Warrington Club. During the first portion the game was very evenly contested, both sides missing several good chances. Webbe eventually scored a good try from a line out, just before the interval, which Warder failed to improve. The second half was greatly in favour of Runcorn, who pressed continually, but it was some time before they scored, Warder running in a couple of bril- liant tries both of which Dunbavand improved. The game ended in a victory for Runcorn by 2-1-2 to Wigan nil.</p>
        <p>Wins v. SAM:AM.—This match' was played at Salford. There were about 1,000 spectators. Widnes were without Brennan, Hogan, Gardorr, and Whelan, but Salford were strongly represented. Drummond kicked off for Widnes against the sun. Smith scored a fine try for Salford, but no goal resulted. Midfield was then the scene of hostilities for some time. Clarke made a fine run on the other wing, hut Miles proved a stumbl- ing block, and forced a minor. Barber conceded a similar point at the other end. Walsh received an in- jury, and had to leave the field. Hughes had exceed- ingly hard lines when the Salford lines were at his mercy, and Widnes continued to hold the upper hand, but the Salford defence was perfect. Half-time score ; —Salford 1 try, Widnes nil. Mills scored, but Manwar- ing failed to convert. Wynne glover for Widnes,Drum- mond missing with the kick. Final result :—Salford 2. tries, Widnes 1 try. . _ . .</p>
        <p>- LEIGH V. ST. HELENS RECREATION.—PIayed at Leigh, on Tuesday, this being the return match. Wilding and Fitzgerald, of Cardiff, both appeared in the Leigh team, whilst the Recs. were assisted by several Aspull men. In the first half, the Leigh backs mulled fearfully, and Sed- dowand Pilkington each scored goals, and Wilson,Fildes, arid Molyneux scored tries, one goal resulting. Leigh played fourteen men, Wilding being hurt early on. After changing ends, Briers scored for St. Helens, and then Leigh pressed severely right to the finish, but could only score numerous minors, although Fitzgerald almost dropped a goal. Result :—Recs. 3 goal 3 tries, Leigh nil.</p>
        <p>Aspum. v. ST. HELENS RECS.—Played at Aspull, be- fore a moderate crowd. Aspull kicked off, Pilkington secured and ran in a gooa try, Seddon kicking a beauti- ful goal from a difficult place. A series of scrimmages ensued, and from a kick across the field Lockett secured and tried a drop, which fell short. Hogan eventually secured, and scored an unimproved try. Half-time score :—St. Helens Recs. 1 goal, Asptill 1 try. Resum- ing, the Recs., playing dowehill, adopted aggressive tactics but Aspull defended well, and gradually worked the ball up the field, the play being located in the visit- ors' half for some time. Sharrock dropped a beautiful goal, and Pilkington gained his second try. Seddon con- verting. Final result :—St. Helens Recs. 2goals, Aspull 1 goal (dropped) 1 try. &amp;woks v. ST. HELENS.—These teams met at Run- corn on Tuesday night, tor the benefit of the volunteer band. In the first half, Runcorn had much the best of the game' and Davies and aoughton scored. Crompton converted one of the tries. St. Helens played up better in the second portion, with brilliant passing on both sides. Whitehouse 'dropped a goal, and Cross scored a try for St. Helens. Crompton scored for Runcorn just on time. Result :—Runcorn 9 points, St. Helens 8 points.</p>
        <p>_ WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH V. REST OF LEAGUE.— Parish Church, the champions of the Junior Footbal League, met a team made up of members of the other, teams of the league on Thursday night, on the Warring- ton football ground, Wilderspool Causeway, before a large number of spectators. Bennett scored for the district about five minutes from the start, but Carey got over for the church, and the try was augmented. Half- time score was Parish Church one goal, Rest of League one try. In the last half, Mason dropped a goal and the Parish Church retired winners by two goals (one drop- ped), to one try, or nine points to two.</p>
        <p>BIRKENHEAD WANDERERS V. RITI.CORN--These teams met at Birkenhead on Thursday evening,in fine weather, before a fairly good attendance of spectators. Even play followed the kick off, until Runcorn broke away, and from a good pass by Clark, Crompton ran in a fine try, the same player converting. After this Runcorn pressed the home team. Runcorn's passing was very fine, but they were unsuccessful in their final efforts to score, a minor being the result. J.Hughes was next conspicuous with some good defensive play, passing by Hurst, Kelly, and W. Hughes, enabling the latter to score a good try, Kelly doing the needful. The visitors by splendid pas- sing attacked ths home fort, Crompton once more cros- sing the coveted line and converting, Nothing further occurred up to the interval, Runcorn then leading by '2 goals to 1. After restarting the Runcorn men took up the running, but a free kick to the home team removed the danger. Runcorn again returned, and Houghton had a drop at goal, the ball going wide. J. Hughes was next cheered for some good play,sending the oval to the centre. A further rush by the home side took the ball to the visitors' line, where they experienced hard luck in not scoring. Scrimmaging in the visitors' quarters was indulged in, but J. Hughes removed the danger, and midfield play ensued. A strong rush by the Wanderers was almost productive of a score, but twice Houtbton saved grandly. Runcorn were prominent with good passing tactics,and Crompton secured his third try after a good run, Warder placin successfully. From now till the finish, the visitors had' the best of the 'game, and wound up with a victory of 3 goals to 1. WIDNES FIRST TEAM'S RECORD. Widnea. Widnes. G.T PT9. G.T.PTS G.T.PT9. 010...0 Salford 1 1...7 0 0 0 St. Helens... 1 0... 5 1 0...5 Blackley 3 0 14 2 2... 13 B'head Wand 0 1... 2 1 1...7 Millom O2 4 4 2... 23 Dukinfield ... 0 0 0 0 0...0 Mossley 1 3 .9 0 6... 12 Blackley 0 0... 0 0 0...0 Tyldesicy ...... 1 0...3 0 2... 4 Radcliffe 0 0...0 liuneorn ......... 1 0...5 1 0 4 Crompton 00.0 Barton 00.0 10... 5 Mill.nn I 5.15 1 1...6 Barrow ......... 1 0...5 0 1... 2 Warrington 0 0... 0 0 2...1 Roc/10435L CI 0 3 0 1 2 Moseley 1 0...5 Dukinfield .. .. 1 1...5 0 0... 0 Warrington 1 0... 5 0 0...0 B'head Wand. 1 0...5 00... 2 B'head Wand 00... 0 1 1...7 Tuebrook ...... 0 0...0 11... 7 Tuebrook 0 0... 0 0 S.. 0 Crompton 0 2.. 4 21. 12 Leigh 10.5 0 2...4 Barton 0 0.0 0 0 0 Runcorn 0 0.0 Leigh 2 0...8 0 1... 2 -t..lielens 1 2... 9 2 2 14 Radcliffe 0 0...0 0 1... 2 Salford During the Season Widnes have ;von fifteen matches,! lost fifteen and drawn two. They have scored 18 goals and 27 tries, 140 points to their opponents 19 goals, and 21 tries 127 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ASSOCIATION. WARRINGTON MEDAL' COMPETITION. FINAL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. WARRINGTON MEDAL' COMPETITION. FINAL.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHINS' v. WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>These teams met on Saturday on the Cricket Field at Arpley to play the final match of this competitiox. There was a" gate" of about 4,000 persons. Warring- ton won the toss and defended the west gal, the cross wind, however, giving but alight assistance to either side. Mr. Finlayson kicked off, and the Saints were immediately on the offensive by means of Clayton. After a few minutes play Plicenix scored from a scrimmage. Immediately after the re-start, Warrington had a clear run for the Saints' goal, but Radford shot wide. For some time Warrington were dangerous, Marshall, Cook. and Colley defending well, St. Elphin's now played up and Higginbottom scored a goal which was disputed by the Warrington team, and they refused to play for some time. After about five minutes' stoppage, the game was resumed, and a fine shot was sent in, Marshall fisting out, but to the surprise of the spectators, a goal was allowed. The Saints gainad two fruitless corners, but at length Clayton scored a fine goal from almost the half way line. Warrington now pressed, and Marshall saved with almost all the opposing forwards upon him, but they would not be denied, and Davies scored the best goal of the afternoon, just before half-time when the scores were St. Elphin's three goals,Warrington two goals.—Hatton was prominent he two shots soon after the interval,which Bannon saved. The greater portion of this half was in favour of St.Elphins,as Cook was in fine form and time after time stopped the rushes of the War- rington forwards. An exciting scrimmage took place before the Warrington goal,B nnon st'bpping shots from Tickle,liatton and Scarisnrick. The Saints kept up the at- tack,and at length, Mather mulled the ball in front of his goal,and Hatton,rushing up sent it through. Play slowed down after this, and Mather and Cook on each side defended well and the final result read St. Elphins' four goals, Warrington two goals.—After the game, Mr. Finlayson presented the medals to the members of "The Champion Association Team of Warrington." The Rev. T. W. Cook replied on behalf of the team, and three cheers were given for the St. Elphin's team, Rev. T. W. Cook and Mr. Finlaysorn</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHINS T. WARRINGTON UNITED.—The Association champions met Warrington United on Thursday night at Dalton Bank, before 700 spectators. Connor for the United was early prominent with a good shot which just went outside. Visits from one goal to the other, marked the progress of the first half, and the interval arrived with a clean sheet. In the second half, the United did most of the pressing, and frem a well placed eintre, Fennelly put the bill smartly pait Bates and the g ime ended with the result, Warrington United, 008 goal, Bt. Elphins nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>MARRIED v. SINGLE.</p>
        <p>In aid of the fund being raised towards the institution of a gymnasium for Earlestown and Newton, a cricket match was played on the 'Newton Cricket ground, on Saturday afternoon, between teams selected from the Earlestown and Newton clubs, the married members be- ing opposed to the single members. The weather was purely cricket weather,, and there was a fair number of spectators present. Mr. W. H. Hindley captained the benedicts and Mr. C. W. Emmett the bachelors, and the latter winning the toss, went to the wickets first, Hus- band and Cook facing the bowling of Cooke and Lawton. Both batsmen began early t 9 score, but Husband played in a most careful manner, never giving a chance. Cook contributed 15, which included four boundaries, before ha was dismissed by Barker. Alderson partnered Hus- band, and drove one to the boundary, his partner also hitting out freely. Alderson exhibited good style, and after adding 14 to the score tie was caught and bowled by Hindley, who had relieved Lawton of the leather. Miller replaced Barker at the other end,and rurton filled the vacancy caused by Alderson. The change in the bowling was not without effect, and Turton was dis- missed for 5. Hopkins replaced him, and Lister, who relieved Miller c and b Hopkins far 5, the score standing 90 for 4 wickets. Glover joined Husband, the latter getting one away to the boundary for 3 off La-vton,who bad relieved Hindley. Glover gave a chance, which Emmett failed to negotiate. Husband next played one to Hindley,which he smartly fielded,the batsman having a narrow shave,Lawton soon after captured Glover,who had only added to the score, the score being 98 for 5 wickets. Emmett filled the vacancy, and at once com- menced to hit out, but he failed to increase the score largely, his contribution being 2, and he was dismissed by playing the ball on to his wicket. Husband continued to keep his wickets intact, notwithstanding the frequent change of bowlers, and his clean hitting was a treat, boundaries coming frequently. Barr joined Husband, hut he was clean bowled by Lawton without scoring. Brierley replaced him, and Husband placed one nicely to the boundary and the next ball he drove to the pavillion. Brierley next gave Nokeoi a chance which he missed. He skied the next delivery, which Lister failed to hold. After a couple of drives for 2 Barker took the ball from Lister, the score standing at 120. Brierley drove his fist delivery to the boundary, and Husband followed suit. Lawton, however, quickly cut short Brierley's career,after adding 15 to the total,B wickets being down for 139. Clare filled the vacancy caused by Brierley,but Lawton dismissed him with the second ball for a duck. Carson replaced him, but after a single and a boundary Barker sent him, back to the pavillion. Mills joined Husband, who was still playing carefully, and at 149 for 10 wickets, the innings was declared closed. After the usual interval the married men went to the wickets, F. Emmett and W. H. Notes taking first place, while Cooke and Clare trundled the leather. After scoring a single. Clare sent Nokes to the right about, and Miller joined Emmett. At the second delivery by Clare, Miller had a narrow shave, but he afterwards played in his usual vigorous style, boundaries coming freely. Emmett compiled 3 and was then clean bowled by Cooke. Lawton partnered Miller, and as the score was gradually increasing a change of bowlers was effected. Hopkins and Alderson .going on. After adding 29, Miller was taken at long field by Husband off Alderson. Lister joined Lawton, the latter being dis- missed by Alderson for 15, Lister was partnered by Dudgeon, but the latter .was removed without scoring being caught by Mills off Alderson. Mills relieved Hop- kins of the leather and Barker joined Lister, and before a separation was effected the score was run up to 91, 7 wickets being down. Barker being caught by Clare off Alderson. Lister made a serviceable contribution of -13, his dismissal being brought about by Husband off Mills". With the exception of Sanders who carried his bat through for 14, none of the others made any appre- ciable addition to the score, four players only contribut- ing 3, and the innings closed for 108. Alderson ex- hibited excellent form both with bat and ball, and gives promise of being a useful member to the Earlestown club. The individual scores" are as under :</p>
        <p>SiseLE.</p>
