      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WESTON CRICKET CLUB.</head>
        <p>WESTON CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>Cricket commenced at Weston. on Saturday, under most favourable circumstances, when an opening game between the married and single members of the club was played, the married team being assisted by the Rev. G, H. Kingston, the president of the club. Several new engagements appear on the fixture card this gear, to- gether with a strong list of vice-presidents, and there is every prospect of the club having a prosperous season, but whether it is a successful one remairs to be seen. The club, which is one of the oldest in the neighbour- hood, this year completes the 25th year of its existence, and the membership is now larger than at any other period. Mr. James Tudor, who, last year succeeded the late Barry Dean, again captains the first team, and Mr. D. Somerville has undertaken he duties of secretary to the club. After a very pleasant and enjoyable game, the match On Saturday resulted in a draw in favour of "single " team. The score was as follows</p>
        <p>- SINGLE. MARRIED. Peen ck run out It Limp b Tudor • 0 Pye bJ Tudor jun. P Brookes b Tudor .1 Lightfoot c Hope b Hope lbw b J. Tudor jun 6 Pr ce 0 Bev. G H Kingston lbw G 0 Jones b Price It J3leakley b Kingston Owen c end b Hope 2 3 Tudor lbw Bleakley Pondlebury c Tudor b Devaston b Bleaklev 2 Kingston J Tudor jun. b Tudor l4 Hughes e Bankes b Light- Ban kes c Pendlebury b foot Tudor G A Tudor b Kingston ... 1 Lloyd c Owen b Lightfoot 0 West o Kirkham b Kings- Norton ton Cottrill run out. Hsugh not out 0 Byes l6 Byes ll</p>
        <p>Total —9l</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>The St Helens magistrates on Monday heard sum- monses against 114 residents in St. Helens for non- payment of rates. This, we anderstand, is the largest number of rate summonses ever brought before the court, and is an index to the state of trade in the town. KAI'S COMPOUND. for Coughs and Colds. Asthma and Bronchitis are relieved by it, 90, 130. &amp;c.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WARRINGTON GARRICK 806IETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GARRICK 806IETY.</p>
        <p>THE "ORANGE GIRL."</p>
        <p>It is about two years since the only amateur theatrical company, Warrington now possesses— the Garrick Society—was formed. But since then it has worked hard for, and certainly achieved, a footing in thA town which would seem only obtain- able after a longer existence. Several difficult , pieces have been creditably submitted by the members„' both in Warrington and St. Helens and the benefisial ' effect of their experience ,upon the histrionic capabilities of the members, has been demonstrated in an obvious manner, in the performance of the" Orange Girl," at the Theatre Royal, during the present week. The patronage of the Mayor, and the Borough member, hail been obtained, and there were fairly large attendances each evening. In the short, though pathetic prologue, in which the " die is cast," concerning the future lives of the character, was performed in a very creditable manner, although it contained, as did the piece through- out, just a few little shortcomings which amateurs, except of long experience, cannot be expected to be entirely without. The part of the hero, John Fryer, could not have been played with a much more breezy or freer manner, than it was by Mr. John Jones, who by the way also rendered yeoman service by taking in hand the stage manavement, the arduous duties of which he executed with efficiency. The " bad bold villain," Sir Peregrine Beamish,was left to Mr.H.Claff for delineation, and Mr. Claff did not fail in giving his part the re- quired interpretations and the movements which are required to make up a realistic Sir P. Bea mish. Next wo have Uriah Underwood, "the blighter of the hero's happiness." The treatment of this character by Mr. H. ' Barn i was characterised by considerable feeling and ' "go." Then there comes Col. Alford, Governor of the Prison, who is very correctly, though quietly, imper- sonated by Mr. Brassington. Mr. F. Worrall's "Pepper , Frost" is probably atwnit the hardest character to deal with as it reqnires considerable personal originality from the actor. Although occasionally displaying a little crudeness, Mr. Worrall succeeded generally in getting through his work in a mariner which called forth the approbation .of the audience. The title role "The Orange Girl" was left to the care of Miss K. M. Neville, a professional, and her performance was undoubtedly stamped with a superiority which helped very consider- ably to make the success of the piece. As Sallie Jesup Miss Amy Dale was very clever, and Mrs. Minaham, as Ellie performed well. Other characters in the piece. who are deserving of praise are Mr. C. Grayson (Gre- gory Dvngell), Mr. Heine (doctor), Mr., W. Webster (a warder). Mr. J. Heyland (ticket-of-leave Man), Mr. T. Rowell (Joe Randall). On Tuesday night Miss Winnie Coop and Miss Bessie Coop and Mr. George Taylor, gave a musical entertainment. The scenery was painted by Mr. J. W. Snape.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>JAMES HUNT OF WARRINGTON, AND</head>
        <p>JAMES HUNT OF WARRINGTON, AND</p>
        <p>HIS DOG.</p>
        <p>THEY PERFORM BEFORE; BARON ROTHSCHILD.</p>
        <p>James Hunt, of Warrington, and his dog left here not long ago as a clever, though not very well- known.couple. But they now seem to be ascending the ladder of a lucrative celebrity; and anyone who has seen them perform cannot but admit that they deserve all success. Hunt describes himself as the champion trick forward and backward jumper of the world, and champion over all self-styled champions, while his'dog is described as the champion over all champions of the world, being open to jump any dog in the world from £lBO to £5OO aside. Hunt's jump- ing feats are innumerable, and show that he is a man of unlimited resources so far as this branch of athletics goes. He recently jumped over an elephant at the Royal Aquarium, London. He can jump off the bottom of a "tot "glass lin. wide over two chairs ; off a glass of water over five chairs without spilling the water ; on to 2 children's faces lying on the ground 10ft. distant and off again without hurting him, Ire. Amongst the feats performed by Runt's dog is jumping 'over 18 chairs at one jump; over 5 horses at one jump; over a gate 81t. high ; over a gate 81t. high and 12 chairs at one jump; through fired rings and gates. dm. Hunt and the deg are at present performing at the Empire, London and the other night were called upon to give a special per- formance before Baron Rothschild. Afterwards the Baron sent for Hunt, and made inquiries as to how the dog was trained whether by kindness or cruelty. Hunt informed the Baron that the dog was trained by him and his father, and it had been accomplished by kindness, goOd feeding, and holding him to one purpose. The dog shook hands with the Baron, who made Hunt a handsome present. Hunt and his dog have been invited to perform at the Hippodrome at Paris at £3O a week, but an engagement has not yet been entered into. He has already performed at the Alhambra, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAM EN r.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAM EN r.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House et Commons, on Monday, Mr. CAMPBELL- I BANNTennrew informed Major Rasch that the extent of the increase of special pensions for Crimean and Mutiny ser- vices was not yet finally settled. Mr. Camp bell-Ban- nerman informed General Hushes that the question of distributing long-service badges to Volunteers under commission rank was under consideration.</p>
        <p>Sir E. Gas, replying to Colonel Howard Vincent, said no intelligence had been re ceived to the effect that Pre- sident Cleveland proposed to abrogate the twelve treaties cot:minded by the United Sta tea with foreign States, and providing for the admission of American goods duty free, or at a reduction of 25 per cent, of the duties levied on British goods in reciprocity for admission to the American markets.</p>
        <p>The CHANCELLOR cf the ExcErtumuz made his annual Budget statement. He said the expenditure for the past year had exceeded the estimate by £122,000, while the revenue had been £58,000 less than the poilmate. The receipts from foreign spirits had fallen £337,000 and those from hems spirits £450,000, but tobacco had yielded £182,000 more. Beer remained almost the same. The income tax had increased by £70,000. Thq entire revenue fel. the year was £90,395,000 and the expendi- ture £90,375,000 leaving a balance of £20000; but in addition to the revenue upwards of £2,000,000 was borrowed during the year. The reduction of the National Debt proper during the year had been £8,832 000. The total estimated expenditure for the year 1893-4 was £91,484,000, an excess of £1,089,000 over the expenditure of 1892-3, due chiefly to education and post-office requirements. The estimated total re- venue for 1893-4 on the existing basis was £89,290,000, so that £1,574,000 had to be provided for. Comment- ing on the growth of the national expenditure, the right hon. gentleman said there was nw economical party I n the country now. Financial economy seemed to have gone.the way of political economy. As to the means of meeting the deficiency, he proposed to increase the in- come tax one penny.</p>
        <p>. , The Railway Servants (Hours of Labour) Bill as amended was considered. Sir „T. Goren, moved a new clause declaring that hours exceeding eight for signal- men and ten for other servants should he deemed prima- facie excessive. The Government opposed the motion, which on a division WdS rejected by 257 to 71. The debate was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday ovening,several questions were put to the Home Secretary in reference to the Hull labour dispute. Mr. ASQUITH said the local authorities were of opinion that the military force was still necessary, and in these circumstances it would not Ibe withdrawn. He had authorised the despatch that morning of twenty-five mounted men of the Metropolitan, ' Police to assist in the preservation of order. Mr. G. W. RUSSELL informed Mr. S. Smith that the Secretary for India had not heard of any intention onl the part of the Government of India to propose a reim- position ot the cotton duties. Mr. BRYCE, replying to questions put by Mr. Forwood and other members, made a statement on the subject of the appointment of magistrates for Lancashire boroughs. Subsequently Mr. T. W. LEou moved the adjournment of the House in order to call attention to the action of the Chancellor of the Duchy in regard to the appoint- ment of county and borough magistrates. Mr.,LEOR'S censure of Mr. Bryce was supported by Mr. [UNHURT, Mr. CURZON, Mr. MOWBRAY, Sir H. JAMES, and Mr. CHAPLIN, while Mr.ROBY and MT. T. P. O'CONNOR spoke in vindication of the right hon. gentleman. Mr. BRYCE explained the course he had adopted since he came into office, and declared that no,person desired less than he did that appointments to the bench should be political. On a division Mr. Legh's motion was defeated by 260 to 186—a Government majority of 74. The debate on the Employers' Liability Bill was re- sumed, and the bill passed its second reading.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday the debate on the motion for the second reading of the Registration of Electors Act Amendment Bill was resumed. Sir IL Paget moved an amendment that the expense of regis- tration should not be raised unjustly by imposing an ad-, ditional charge on occupiers of houses and lands only. Mr. Haubury seconded the amendment. The debate was continued by many members. Exception was taken to the period of qualification, and it was urged that the Government should have brought in a more Radical measure of reform. Mr. Fowler considered the criticisms on the bill, though contradictory, were on the whole fayoqrable to it. Ile pointed nut that the object of the measure was registration and not enfranchise- ment, maintained in answer to the amendment that there had been a steady fall in rural rates during the prestrit century, held that the machinery of the bill was cheap, easy, and fair, and said the Government were quite willing to refer the measure to a Committee of the whole House. The amendment was negatived without a division, said the bill read a second time,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday evening Mi. ASQUITH, in answer to inquiries, made a statement as to the prison life of the Dowager Duchess of 8-UTHERLAND, and said he had no reason to believe that the Visiting Committee of the gaol had allowed her larger privileges than were usual in such cases.</p>
        <p>The right hon. gentleman informed Sir J. LUBBOCK that he was not in a position to pledge the Government to undertake legislation this session in favour of shorten- ing the hours of labour in shops. Replying to another question, he said the subject of laying restrictions on the use of revolvers was engaging hi., careful attention.</p>
        <p>Mr. BRYCE refused to comply with a demand of Sir F, MILNER'S that he would furnish the names of the magis- trates who " had refused to take •declarations front members of the Hladstonian party."' in Committee of Ways ad Means the Crrasect,Lon of the EXCHEQUER moved a resolution providing for an increase ot a penny in the pound in the income tar. Two amendments having been made and withdrawn, the Budget resolutions were agreed to. 'The House then proceeded to consider the Riilway Servants' flours of Labour Bill as amended, and the bill passed its third reading.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING MINI N G FATALITY AT GOLBO I tNE.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, Mr. Brighouse, County coroner, held an inquest at the Manor Arms Hotel. Go!borne, re- specting the case of James Allen, who met with a shock iiig death about noon on Saturday last, at the Brya Colliery, belonging to Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., Limited. Wm. Nicholson, fireman at the pit, said he came up in the cage with the deceased about half- past one on the day in question. Two other men ac- companied them. The cage was wound up to the top. The catches had been fastened up, and the cage was suspended on the rope only. Witness stepped out first, and was followed by deceased ; , the other two men got out on the other side. Witness turned round and saw the deceased re-entering the cage. He was stooping down, and was in the act of coming out again, when the cage Atarted to descend, wedging the body of the deceased between the top of the cage and the landing place.—James Banks, the banksman, explained that when the cage came up at the time the accident Occurred he was doing his ordinary labour fifty or sixty yardS away from the shaft, when he noticed from the sound of the pulleys that men were being wound up. He made all haste to get to the catches. Deceased wag stooping down to get his tools out ot the cage w hen ot started. Witness and another man shouted to the engineman to stop. The catches were used for the remainder of the men who came up.—James Potter said he was in charge of the engine when the accident occur- - red. He saw the four men leave the cage, and concluded they were safe. On receiving the signal from beloW that other men were waiting to come up, he started to lower the top cage, and stopped the engines immediately he heard the shout.—The pry returned a verdict of : "Accidental death," adding that they were of opinion. that if the browman had been on duty on the pit bank, the accident would not have occurred. The inquirr lasted three hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CRAZY POLITICIAN.</head>
        <p>A CRAZY POLITICIAN.</p>
        <p>THREATENING TO SHOOT MR. GLADSTONE., William Henry Townsend, a Sheffield manufacturer' Son, was brought before Sir John Bridge at Bow-st. Thursday, charged with firing a revolver to the common danger. On the previous night he was arrested in the Horse Guards tarade, where he told &amp;policeman that he intended to shoot the Premier, and immediately after- wards fired two shots at Mr. Gladstone's residence in Carlton House Terrace, the bullets striking the, wall. The prisoner said, "I did not tire wantonly. It was an accident." In his pocket was found a memorandum" book containing erratic comments on Home Rule and threats against the Prime Minister. The man was re- manded. A Sheffield correspondent writes that the prisoner left that town abruptly, that politics. had be- come a craze with him, and that he could talka nothing else.</p>
        <p>MR, GLADSTONE AND THE MIN'ERS)</p>
        <p>EIGHT HOURS DAY.,</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone has consented to receive on Monday' next a deputation from the Milling' ABSOCirttiOn of Great Britain on the question of eight hours a day in mines, who will urge that there should be no eornpulsQry legis- lation on this subjeot.</p>
        <p>L1N1.511 CATHARTICS/2kt PILLS. agreeably aperient, 90..1314 28. 9(1. 0t all Chemlate,—Advt.</p>
      </div>
