      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>BURIAL BOARD. ELECTION AT HALTON.</p>
        <p>The election of two members of Halton Burial Board took place on Saturday, and aroused consid- eraule interest and excitement. At the vestry meeting,Messrs. John Barrow and George Leath- , wood,the retiring merakers who are Nonconformists received a majority of votes, but a poll was de- manded on behalf of Mr. John Samuel Jeacock, and Alfred Fryer, who were nominated by the Church party. The election was of the moat primitive description. The Vicar, the Rev. G, D. Wray, was the returning officer, and each voter had 0 publicly announce to him ;low he wished to vote, and the vicar then made the record in the poll book. Mr. Woodier assistant overseer, checked the voters by the rate book. Much feeling was imported into the contest, and both sides strove strenuously to secure the victory, convey- ances being freely used. Shortly after six o'clock the result wee declared as follows—Mr. Jeacock, 140, Mr. Fryer, 139, Mr. Barrow, 107, Mr. Leathwood, 103. The victory for the Church was signalled by the ringiag of the school bell. On the motion oi Mr. Jeacock seconded by Mr. Job Hancock, a vote of thanks was accorded to the presiding officers. The poll book was sealed up, and after being retained by the vicar for a month will be destroyed--The successful candidates and a number of their friends afterwards met in the library at the school and Mr.Jeacock said the cause of the election was,they all knew,from it being found impossible to come to a reason- able arrangement with their friends on the ether side, who were usurping one seat after another, and in a certain way had threatened to take the whole of them, Although the Church party had been a quiet people, this usurpation called forth an effort to see if they could not right things. The result had bean successful, and he was sure it would re-act for the welfare of the Church in many ways. They loved their Church. He was per- fectly persuaded that when the English Church was at- tacked, it would call forth such a feeling of indignation that no stalennan would ever try a second time to dis- establish her. Mr. Fryer said the Church party had a right to rule, and new they had the privilege to do SO. The Vicar thought they might congratulate themselves on the victory of that day. It was one they ought to have had long before, only they had been so considerate towards those who had pushed themselves so frequently into every office. They made a moderate demand, and if three members had been granted, no election would have taken place. Seeing this was refused they would have been a mean-spirited lot if they had not demanded their rights: He must congratulate those who had worked on the charitable way in which they had conducted thedi- selves towards their opponents. Be wished he could say the same about the other pasty. Things had been said for which there was no foundation of ti uth. He had in the past worked together in a friendly way with the other side ; but he would say that the overtures of friendliness had generally come from the Church side. lie must say that Mr. Hanby, (Wesleyan minister) had treated him in the way he could wish to be trea:ed, friendly relations were as great as ever they were. He hoped that whatever feeling might have been created it would aeon die away, and they woulll - try and meet together as fellow Christians in the future sun the past. The Board will now consist of four churchmen and three Nonconformists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ST. GEORGE OF ENGLAND.</head>
        <p>ST. GEORGE OF ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>The feast of St: George, the martyr, was cele- brated in St. .Edward's Homan Catholic Church, Runcorn, on Sunday. In the morning, High Mass was celebrated, and in the evening Benediction was pronounced. The Revd. Dr. Gastaldi officiated throughout tile day, and after the evening service delivered an address on St. George, which was listened to with great interest. Dr. Gastaldi.said : It was very commonly supposed that St. George was a myth, or imaginary saint, but be affirmed that he had a personal entity, and that his fame rested on a true historical founclati.m. Churches had been dedicated to his honour in Europe from the earliest time, and the edifice that stood over his tomb in Palestine was founded by Constantine the Great. The great national Council, held at Oxford in 1222, commanded the feast of St. George to be kept a holiday of the lesser rank through- out all England • and under his name and ensign, King Edward 111 in 1330 instituted the most noble order of Knighthood in Europe, consisting of 25 Knights besides the sovereign. For centuries the Coins of Great Britain had been distinguished by a representative of St. George, on horseback tilting at a dragon. This representation was no more than an emblema- tical figure, pUrporting that by his faith and christian fortitude, St. George conquered the devil, called tl dragon in the Apocalypse. According to the -After given by the historian Idetaphrastes, borp. -dlothet telte , Cii,padocia of noble christian parenta, the death n Di his father he went with his to stine she being a native of that coo;,ery, and haVi there a con- siderable estate, which fell to her see George. He was strong and robust in body, and fleeing embraced the profession of a soldier Was =idea tribe.tne or colonel in the army. By his courage atta conduct he was soon preferred to higher stations ty the emperor Diucleeian. When that prince waged 30. r against the Christian re- ligion, St. George threw up his commission and posts, and complained to the emperor of his severities and bloody edicts. He 4wes cast into prison, and tried, first by promips, and iftetwards tortured with greatcruelty, but nothing could shake his constancy to the christian religion and the next stay he was led tbrou,gh the city and beheaded. It was shown from the unanimous con- sent of the churches that he suffered martyrdom on April 23rd, 303. The reason why St. George had been re- garded as the patron saint of military men was greatly due to his profession, and Dr. Gastaldi pointed out that though many dishonoured the profession -of arms by licentiousness of manners, yet to show that perfect sanctity was attainable in all states there were the names of more soldiers recorded in the martyrologies than almost any other profession.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>AFTER THE GREAT DIVISION.</head>
        <p>AFTER THE GREAT DIVISION.</p>
        <p>SCENES IN TuE LOBBIES.</p>
        <p>The Second reading of the Home Rule Bill was carried in the House of Commons, on Saturday morning, by a majority of 4-3. Meer the House adjourned there was a succession of dramatic scenes in the lobbies. The Na- tionalists gave themselves over to rejoicing by cheering and waving their Eats. Dr. Tanner was busy amongst the Ulster Tories, and the scene for many minutes par- took of an unprecedented character. The bar was filled with Nationalists-drinking success to Horne Rule and the Grand Old Man, 'while in the-outer lobby Irish strangers in town conducted a series of demonstrations on their own account, singing, " God Save Ireland" and other patriotic ditties, 411 spite of all the police could do to preserve the dull decorum of the palace. Another cor- respondent telegraphs that -there was a scene of wild excitement in the outer lobby when the result became known amongst the cr3wd of Irish delegates and others in waiting. Their groans and hooting were met by cheers from the Home Rulers, and it was some time be- I fore the extra ,police could clear the lobbies. Mr.Bartley has already seventy amendments to the bill. Eight of the nine Parnellite members have given notice of amend- ments to the -Home Rule Bill when considered in com- mittee. The purport of these is that the new Irish Legislature shall be called the House of Commons, that the Senate-or Second Chamber shall consist of ninety-six members instead of forty-eight as now proposed ,and that the House-of Commons consist of 206 members, insbead of the 103,preposed. The object of this party's amend- ments to the financial clauses in the bill is to force the withdrawal of theolauses from the measure as they now stand. . .</p>
        <p>--- At the last moment Mr. Saunders yielded to the pres- sure brought to bear upon him and consented to vote for the Second reading of the Home Rule Dill. Also at the last moment the Gladatonian Whips managed te secure a pair for Mr. D. A. Thomas ; Mr. Villiers, the "Father a the house," whose improved condition on Thursday justified the hope that tie would be in his placeon Friday, being too unwell to leave his residence. The Government were thus enabled to obtain their full advantage; and the principle of a separate Parliament for Ireland was affirmed by precisely the same majority —43—as was cast in favour of the Union in the division of February 6, 1800, in the Irish Parliament. The majority in favour of the second reading was csanposed of 266 Gladstonians and 'Bl Nationalists, and the minority against the second reading 01 280 Conservatives and 44 Liberal Unionists, the remaining 19 members being accounted for as follows :—Absent, paired 14 ; tellers, 4 ; Speaker, 1. This is the first division on re- cord in which every member ,of the house has been accounted for, either in the official record of the voting or with the list of pairs. There were 656 members present when the question was put from the chair. This was a falling-off of nine on the "No confidence " division of last August, when the attendance was 665 out of a possible 668 (two seats being vacant), and an increase of 150 on the vote of cen- sure division on March 27, when 596 hon. gentlemen were in their places. Tim division on the second read- ing of the Home Rule Bill in 1886 was taken in a house of 657 members. Prior to the redistribution of seats in 1885 the record division was that ',Tun the second read- ing of the Russell-Gladstone Reform Bill in the spring of .1866, when, out of a total of 648, eight members only were absent. In March, 1831, when the first Reform Bill, introduced by Lord John Russell, was read a second time by a majority of one, the number of mem- bers presentacsei n which h 7 t; be aggregate but there h ed e o had vote previouslyreached 600. ben. 'the divisionclnelmoßniteGtfooeernlnewrabßc Gasc oigne's amendment on ==7:: the going 595.</p>
