      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Etttrro to tbt Obttor.</head>
        <p>Etttrro to tbt Obttor.</p>
        <p>CHRISTIANITY AND ITS •6 RES E l BLANCES." " Ex• Farmer "I am glad to say with what a deep feeling of pleasure I perused the letters recently published in your valued paper, relating to Christianity and its resemblances. To a thinking mind they contain a vast amount of nsost interesting information and cer- tainly tend to increase one's desire for more knowledge. la a broad sense it is evident we niust look to the Press rather than the pulpit for instruction, and for this reason I am sorry the correspondence is at an end. In my humble opinion there is a deal more to learn in those letters than in many of the average 50 or 60 tenni es' sermons one is accustomed to hear,cut and dry, in orthodoxy to-dar. Assertion void of argument will cot satisfy the inquiring soul—it verily adds te the weariness and monotony of this busy life. There are those who hold certain beliefs, yet if they were asked their reason for the same, they would give some curious replies. I heartily thank" Harry Esmond "and " Isos" for the light they have thrown upon questions at once diffi-ult but fascinating. Doubtless many others are hoping sooner or later to see in your columns the ques- tion asked by the latter fully explained.—Yours respect- fully, Macclesfield, April 26th, 1893.</p>
        <p>INQUIRER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>SOCIAL QUESTIONS UNION FOR WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL QUESTIONS UNION FOR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I call the especial attention of your readers to the advertisement in your columns to-day of a meet- ing of the above Union, to te held on Monday evening, in the Museum Lecture Room, at eight o;clock ? The design is to inaugurate a general movement in • this town and district to promote social reforms, on the lines of similar movements already existing in several large towns in the kingdom. It is desired to deal with questions of temperance, gambling, social purity, educa- tion and &amp;creation, labour, and the conditions ef home life among the poor. The movement is one that calls for cordial co-operation from members of all sects and parties. In Manchester the president is the Lord Bishop ..f the diocese, and in most towns all parties find in this enterprise a platform for common and harmonious action. As tar as ever it may be possible, it is desired to realise this happy co-operation in Warrington, and I write these lines to make an earnest appeal to members of both political parties and all religious denominations to attend this meeting on Monday, and join hands in a movement which may confer unspeakable benefit upon the whole communily.—l am, yours faithfully. JOHN YONGE, 13, Wilson-Fatten-st., April 27, UM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>"WANTED, A PARSOh."</head>
        <p>"WANTED, A PARSOh."</p>
        <p>Sir,—On Friday last, the body of Richard Worm% of live Crosses, a Wesleyan, probably never confirmed, therefore as the "Church Times" of 14th inst.says : "Not a full christian," was brought to the church gate for burial, the friends after waiting in vain fee: a Church parson, prevailed upon the Rev. F. F. Edmunds, Wesleyan, to Conduct the service, which he did, all honour to him. This is the fourth occasion to my know- ledge that these shepherds (supposed to be) have been away when wanted. They obey the Rubrics when an infant dies not having been sprinkled, and refuse to read the service. As the Romanizsrs in the church look upon Wesleyans and others outside their church as heretirs, are they going to follow a similar rule with them? They adwrato daily morning and evening ser- vice.; why ? Because these offices are specially used as acts of intercessicn for the people. So writes one of their clique. For my own part I prefer Flab. 7c.25 v. The judicious Hooker wrote, " To urge anything upon the church, requiring thereueto that religious assent of Christian belief whcrevrith the words of the holy prophets are received, to urge anything as part of that supernatural and celestially revealed truth which God bath taught, and not to &amp;Kew it in Scripture, this did the ancient Fathers evermore think unlawful. impious, oxeeroble." A layman has a perfect right to baptise, administer the Lord's Supper and read the burial service. So wrote Archbishop Whately.—Yours truly, Frodsham, Asril 21, 1893.</p>
        <p>R. koIILKES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>TREATMENT OF PATIENTS AT THE</head>
        <p>THE TREATMENT OF PATIENTS AT THE</p>
        <p>1-10 PE 110 SPIT</p>
        <p>Sir,— I have been one of those unfortunate indivi- duals who fell a victira to tba prevailing epidemic, con- aequomtly I had to spend some 22 days in the ablve hospital. My object in writing to you, is to correct the very false ideas, which exist in many minds, respecting the treatment the patients are subject to. I hays heard It rep•atedly state, that the patients are neglected and not sufEciently cared for. Sir, nothing could be fort he from the truth. I have to say that nothing could exceed the kindness and attention shown by the doctor, the morass ; in fart by every one in connection with the place. All strive to make one as comfortable AS cir- cumstances will permit, and when one considors bow miserably the place is ventilated (you must either be stifled in a wisoned air, or have the windows open and sleep in a draught) and the depressing surreundings, think it is to the infinite credit of the doctor, the nurses, and all concerned that the death rate is so low. The food is good, Well-cooked, and enough of it, with as little waste as is Dosaible under the circumstances. In con- clusion, lean only add, that no one, who may h. is unfortunate as to be struck with the disease, need he alarmed by a sojourn there, and the friends of patients may rest themselves content with the assurance, that all that skill, attention. and kindness can do is given cheer- fully and freely. Thanking you in anticipation of tlic insertion of ablave, I beg to remain—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>FRANK WILLIAMS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WHAT IS SALVATION?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS SALVATION?</p>
        <p>!,</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have been finding much amusement during the last few weeks in noting the ratiocinative vagaries of the Rector—who has set himself the super-flerculean task of setting the sects right—and showing them how very much superior is the only old original Anglican-Catholic- ism to their small attempts at making plain the way of salvation. The "instructions" of our worthy cor-Rector have not been received with the humility and docility which shoves on the part of the recipients any great amount of appreciation of the chastening. All the dis- putants are more coucerned to show how and where others are wrong, and in asserting that they, and they Only, are right, that they have neglected to consider the more vital and fundamental question as to whether they are not all wrong in a heap. All this ink-slinging arose through the implied, and oft-asserted, necossity of Salvation. But not one of them stayed to tell us the nature and properties of this dckideratum—of how the necessity for it came about. They are in this as Mahommed was with his cosmic scheme, where the world was supported on the back of an e:ephant and that again upon the back of a tortoise—but he tailed to tell us upon what the tortoise stood ! So it is with these controversialists. They will preach to weariness, and fill columns of this journal—dealing with the general question, and the necessity of carrying this blessirg to those who have it not. But the burning ques- tion, "What is Salvation ?" they leave untouched, or if touched, it is only to drop it like a hot potato. I am amtced, too, at " Harry Esmond's" annoyance at the parsons not taking up the cudgels intlwqiupa,r,irieltl between Buddhism and Christianity. But may out that this annoyance aiises from a false conception of I the duties and functions of the clerics ? Men inherit their belief—what they call their religious convictions— from their parents. It is a matter of accident. Now,in Order to keip this preciuus inheritance in good and use- able eenCitom, the legatee pars another man of his own special t rand to drill,lecture, and imbue his mind in the tenets of his sect. He is not raid to investigate and tell his patron the truth, but to insist that what his patron already thinks is the truth. Any attoript at independent</p>
        <p>kat,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <p>thought and speech on the cleric's part would be resented and punished by the legatee. The scepticism they so loudly decry is, however, gradually emancipating them from this galling and degrading intellectual bondage.— tam, &amp;c., 'SOS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>REV. T. ROSS AND THE RECTOR.</head>
        <p>THE REV. T. ROSS AND THE RECTOR.</p>
        <p>Sir,—When a man is employed to talk and he pleasesl those who pay him, the matter is more particularly' be- tween the employer and the employs, rather than the general public, no matter what may be the subject under notice: but when a report of the talk appears in the public Press, it is then a matter which concerns others also, and, as one of the readers of the "Examiner," I pro- test against the ridiculous contradiction which your report contains as to the means whereby a man may enterinto the kingdom of God. Mr. Ross is reported as saying "Baptism was nst the way into the kingdom of God," and " Except a man be born of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter the kingdom of God." Comment on such glaringly inconsistent foolishness would almost appear to be redundant, but perhaps it may not he out of place to think with the rev. gentleman "that it is far 'more satisfactory to look into the teaching of Scripture than to pin faith to what was said by the "Fathers" of the "Holy Catholic Church," and to see what is the posi- tion of those who listen to what he says when examined by the" teaching of Scripture." Mr. Ross asserts the Rector teaches "Baptismal regene- ration," and he (Mr. Ross) states "Baptism was not the way into the kingdom of God " ; and, as opposed to both, the Bible teaching is that without belief and bap- tism a man cannot enter the kingdom of God. What does Jesus Christ say additional to the above quoted passage, " He that believeth and is baptized shall be saved; but he that disbelieveth shall be condemned," Mark 16 c. 18 v. And what is the actual form of admis- sion to the Church of Christ in the recorded instances of the New Testament ? Peter says, "Repent ye,and be baptized every one of you, in the name of Jesus Christ," Sm., Acts 2, 38 v. And in the Bth chap., verse 12, we are informed that " When they believed Philip preaching coed tidings concerning the kingdom of God, &amp;c., they were baptized both men and women." Again, in the same chapter, we are told that Philip and the eunuch went down into the ater and "he taptized him"-38th verse. Paul (after his conversion) arose and was bap- tized—Acts 9. v. 18. "Lydia WAS baptized and her household"—Acts 16, 15 v. "The men at Ephesus were baptised"—Acts 19, 5 v. ; and on reference it will be found that these men had been triptized before by John. In the case quoted, however, they were baptized into the name of the Lord Jesus Christ.</p>
        <p>And what is the line of argument that the Apostles take when referring to baptism? Peter says (1 3c. 21c'.): "Which also after a true likeness (in the antitype, see margin) doth now save you, even baptism (R.V.) or the like figure whereunto even baptism doth also now save us." Sm. Paul in Romans 6,3, sc. R.V. "Or are ye ignorant that all we who were baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into his death? We were buried, there- fore with him through baptism into death : that like as Christ was raised from the dead through the glory of the Father,so we also might walk in newness of life. For if we have become united with Him by the likeness of His death, we shall be also by the likeness of His resur- rection.' Again,Paul says in his letter to the Galatians. 3 c. 27-29 v., "For as many of you as were baptized into Christ did pat on Christ." . . . . " And if ye are Christ's, then are ye Abraham's seed, heirs according to promise." Frrm the foregoing testimony I believe that baptism is necessary to salvation, and that it must be preceded by belief in" the things concerning the kingdom of God and.the home of Jesus Christ." If the opinions which Mr. Ross has of the Bible teaching areas opposed in other respects to the teaching of Jesus Christ and the Apostles RS in his pronouncement on "The way into the kingdom 'of God," I would recommend to his employers the con- cluding portion 01 his rejoinder with which I most cor- dially agree. "Oh! it was pitiful and sad that with the open Scriptures men should still listen to the Churcliand , be thus deluded and in their turn delude others"—only adding that the Church to me means here both Roman Catholic and Protestant places of worship.</p>
        <p>R. G. L.</p>
        <p>THE INIDIORTALITY OF THE SOUL.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the discussion which recently took place in your columns there were several references to the immortality of the soul. Some of your correspondents seemed to take for granted there was no such thing, while at the same time tpey cave their readers to understand they belonged to the" advMmti " section of thilkers, If Oleg succeed in doing so they have good reason to flatter themeelves. Such a claim however, entails a great responsibility, and it is to ba feared that in this csse theirwritings were 'conspiouous by the absence of argu- ment. What is more astonishing still, is that the sub- ject was entirely ignored in its scientific aspect. Such a defect, it is scarcely necessary to add, renders their I treatment of the subject of little value. There is a general impression among the public that science has positively proved there is no such thing as the soul, or a future life. This however, is not the case, I and, in corroboration of this statement, it may be said that some of the leading scientists of the day, hive written in defence of the immortality of the soul. It is a remarkable fact that some of those whe most strongly oppose this doctrine do so on the ground of an intense dislike to metaphysics, while at the same time, their strongest arguments am founded on the very, metaphy- sical principles to which they object.</p>
        <p>It is also ano less remarkable fact that much of the speculative science of the day is metaphysical. The , theory of the Conservation of Energy and the atomic theory are metaphysical. In like manner the ether ! whiela fills all space and which conveys the undulations of light is metaphysical. Neither ether nor atoms, &amp;e., are objects of the senses, and if knowledge is to be arbi- trarily confined to what the senses reveal, and if what is beyond their ken is to be superciliously ignored as ! metaphysical, then some of the most important theories of science are to be flung into the limbo of nonentity, such for example as the theory of the Conservation of ! Energy. This subject is regarded as one of the greatest discoveries of the century, and binds into an organic unity a number of facts otherwise regarded as hetero- geneous. According to this theory, heat, electricity, mechanical energy, &amp;c., are capable of transformation the one into the other. A certain quantity of heat is 1 capable of generating so much electricity or mechanical energy, and vice versa. But these different energies are never perceptible to the senses. The senses are cogni- sant merely of their effects ; the causes themselves are unseen. It should also be strictly observed that these causes are postulated to account for those effects in con- , serpasoce of an intellectual necessity. It is impossible for the human mind to regard phenomena otherwise ' ! than as the expression of a certain power Certain clusters of phenomena are regarded as produced by heat others as due to electricity. But what is heat, and what is electricity? This is a question by no Means easy to ! answer. Moreover, if thase various energies are upatile I of transformation the one into the other, which of them is to be taken as the type ? Aro we to take the lowest l form of energy, say the meclianical,as tha type, and pass upwards to chemical or vital energy, or are we to take the highest form of energy, say, vital, as the type, and pass successively downwards to the lowest form of energy ? Here lies a crucial question, the answer to which decides whether one is to accept a materialistic .or a theistic conclusion. If one regards the lowest form of energy as capable of originating the others including 1 life an mind, Wen such a view is materialistic. But ie one COMMNBCPS at the other end of the scale and takes the highest form of energy as the type, there arises the important question what do we know of the highest type of energy? Now it must be admitted that of the various types of Energy in themselves we know nothing with one single exception—the exercise of one's free choice or will. What heat and electricity are in themselves we do not know. But we certainly know one thing, arid that is the exercise of choice. In the conflict of our will with what is not our will, or with what is other than our- selves, there is revealed to us the highest type of energy, and by this moans every other energy or force known to Us is symbolized. It is surely much easier to regard the lowest forms of energy as derived from the highest type, than to regard the lowest form as capable of originating the higher. In the former case tho highest type has simply to divest itself of certain characteristics, while, in the latter, the lowest form of energy has to supply itself with qualities of which it was originally destil:ute. It will be objected that this argument leads directly to Theism. This is undoubtedly true. But it is impossible to start from the other alternative and give a rational explanation of the universe. Starting with mere atoms and the various forms of energy it is impossible to account for the universe. It is raven impossible to ex- plain what relation the various forms of energy holds to L the atom. In the infinitesimally small atom there are located the various forms of Energy—atomic, molecular, cohesive, chemical, kn. Do each of these energies reside in a separate part of the atom, or is the atom capable of originating the various energies? These are questions which cannot be answered, and, which it may be safely said, man can never answer while in his pvsent condi- tion. The theistic solution of the universe, on the other hand, presents Us with a rational explanstion which throws a light upon innumerable problems that would remain otherwise insoluble. although not affording a solution to every difficulty it affords the most. reasonable explanation of those profound problems which have baffled the subtlest skill of the ablest pantheistic and materialistic, thinkers.</p>
        <p>But what %earing has the existence of a Divine Being on 111 P iminortality of the soul ? Much, f-verF way. Admit the existence of a Divine Being and the existence Cl the soul follows as a corollary. If a Divine Being possessNl ot intelligence and will is immortal what. is there to hinder man—his own creation—with intern- genre and will—to exist likewise. There is surely nothing unreasonable in such a supposition. lo this it may be replied that death terminates the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29. 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29. 1893.</p>
        <p>existence of the soul, after which the body is resolved into its inorganic elements. But the chemical disinte- gration which takes place at death proceeds during life, only at a much slower pace. All through life the com- plex organised molecules of the body are gradually broken up into carbenic acid, water, /cc., that is, the very same products into which the body is resolved at death. During life, however, they are continually re- newed, the food one takes compensating the loss arising ! from the continual tear and wear to which the body is subjected. In tbis way all the elements of the body are continually changing, now, how is it possible for the soul to remain one and the same through all these silent revolutions? and ir it is capable of surviving these why should it not survive the more rapid catastrophe but still the same revolution which takes place at death? There is another feature of the soul which may as well be taken note of here. The ultimate atoms are subject to laws which are universal and invariable. An atom of hydrogen is indestructible and for ever the same. It acquires no habits nor experiences notwith- standing the unnumbered permutations through which it passes. But the soul at-quires habits and experiences every day. It carries along with it the past, while each day, every decision of the soul is moulding for weal or for woe its future destiny. This is the prerogative of personality, and what crowns our humanity with char- acteristic pre-erainence.—l am, Ise., A LOVER OF TRUTH. 11 April 24th, 1893.</p>
        <p>oD DFELLOWSHIP AT WA it RI N OWN,</p>
        <p>.lUHILEE OF THE "ADMIRAL NELSON" LODGE. One of the" epochs " that lave marked the life of Oddfellowehip in Warriogton, has just palmed; it is the jubilee, or in other words, the 50th year of the existence of the Loyal " Admiral Nelson " Lodge, a jubilee that was celebrated in whet must have appeared to all who belonged to the Order, a most elaborate and thorough manner. Wednesday night was the chosen time. and the Sankey-street Co-operative Hall the place for the celebration. Of course, there were many visiting brethern present, and the total number di an who eat down tothe tea, with which the proceedings were commenced, amounted to-150 ; including the wives and lady friends of the members. -The tea which was a very sumptuous bn- A as-supplied and laid out by Mrs. Booth, and after that had been partaken of the programme of the celebration proper, was launched upon. The programme was perhaps a little too lengthy, for it took from about eight o'clock until eleven to get through it. However, there were not many indications of anything ex- cept perfect enjoyment in connection with the proceedings. Besides the actual celebration of theiJubilee it was in- tended to make the occasion one for presenting to P.P.G.M. Adam Gardener Webster, a testimonial on behalf of the Childrens' Burial Saciety, in recognition of his services as president for 25 years. The testimonial consisted of an artistically worked illuminated address by Messrs. Brooks. and a purse of gold. Another object of the gathering was to help towards the foundation of a benevolent fued in connection with the lodge. When these proceedings were commenced there sat on the platform the chairman, Mr. John Bylands,,l.P., who was greeted with loud applause upon making his appear- ance, and Messrs. T. Buchan, J P., and T. W. Finney, invited guests, and the following invited brethren of the Order—U.M. Henry Flowers, Nerthwich • D.O.M. Claverhouse Graham. Burton-on-Trent; A. S. Campden.'Cambridge ; P.C.S. Thomas Mills, Prov. C.S. George Wilkins, Liverpool; P.G. Vicars P.G. Abrams, Liverpool: Prov, C.S. Henry Ewing, West Derby Dis- trict; P P.G.M. Davies, and P.P.G.M. Bunce. Then there were these district officers present :—Prov. G.M. Massey, Pro. D.O.M. Thomas, Rigby, P.G. Fred. J. Emson and P.G.F.J. Whittle, (hon. secs.) and the follow- ing members of the Committee :—Prov. C.S.J. &amp;nano, P.P.G.M. Matthew Knowles, P.P.G.M. George Perry, P.P.G.M. Frank Webster, P.G. Fred Taylor, P.G. Thomas Kenyon, P.G. John H Sherlock, P.G. Chris Bonehill, N.G. John H. Team V.G. Robert W. Collin, Bro. John Mawson, and Bro.. Fred Barnes. These members of the Conamitteeall worn medals commemorative of the Jubilee, and which were supplied by Mr. Chandler, Birmingham. It was announced that the first item would be the Chairman's address, but before this was delivered Bro. P.O. Wnictle read letters of apology from the Mayor (Alderman E. Greenall), who had gone to the West of England, Mr. J. Charlton Parr, and Mr. Rsbert Pierpoint, the borough member. The latter said that as there were so many measures before the House of Commons at the present time, he did not think any member would be doing his duty if he left the House. He also expressed his wishes for the success of the Admiral Nelson Lodge. Mr. Pier- point, it may be mentioned, is a member of the lodge. A letter was also read from the Bishop of Chester, who would have attended had he not beerr detained at the House of Lords. , Mr. Jona Itxtrarris (the chairman) next delivered a few remarks, but refrained from enteringupona lengthy speech, because, as Max O'Reill had once put it, chair- men were not brought to meetings to monopolies the time—they were like children "to be seen and not beard." (Laughter.) In the work which they were performing as Oddfellows they were performing acts of kindness, and christianity and benevolence, which made it a glorious thing for their Society to exist ; and they were able to do this because they were combined.,There was no doubt that combination was a new foand and useful power, a power which enabled them to achieve that which they could not have hoped for otherwise. (Hear, hear.) Combination was practically a modern thing, and it was a good thing but in a' great many cases it was abused, and when abused, instead of being made a blessing to the community it was made a curse. That was probably very much the case once, but now they were getting more sense, and combination had been improved. (Heel' hear.) They in their combination were doing a gcoci and beautiful work, and he hoped they would increase and develop it—(hear, hear)—and he also hoped they would have many happy returns of the Jubilee. (Laughter.) An association like their's was one which did not die ; and so it would be a body which when they had gone would by the aid of their efforts still continue in the growth 01 its blessed work, and enjoy many other Jubilees. (Hear, hear.) A flute and pianotorte duet, a selection from the Bohemian Girl, was performed by P. U. A. Boyle, and P. G. F. Einson, after which_ , Bro. P. MATTLIEW KNOWLES proposed the "Manchester Unity and Board of Directors" as a toast. In doing so he said that they had amongst them a toast which, the first time he had the pleasure of hearing it, caused considerable surprise. The toast was" May the tree of Oddfellowship be planted in the centre of the globe, and may it extend from polo to pole." (Hear, hear.) He had now been a member for over 40 years— (loud applause)—and as each year had come and gone since he first heard the toast, he had been more and more realizing the truth of the toast, and the possibilities of the great desire it expressed. Their order was indeed extending round the world, for they could not go into any country without meeting a brother Oddfellow. And it was because of the combination or unity which their Chairman had alluded to that they were able to de what good work they could for the welfare and well-being of every Oddfellow, and every good fellow in the world. (Loud applause.) With respect to the Board ot Directors, he might mention that all those who at- tained to the position of director were brothers who had been compelled to work their way up from the bottom rung of the ladder, and who had had to have talent and ability to assist them in coping with the im- portant business which came before the directors ; they were a body of men who had to make their own posi- tions, for they could not be pitchedforked into them like town Cauncillors. (Laughter.) Following this Mr. Walter Booth sang "My Sweet- heart when a boy," and then Grand Master HENRY FLOWERS, of Norwich, res- ponded to the toast of tile Manchester Unity and the Directors. He expressed his pleasure at being present at the ceremony that evening ; it was the third meeting that week he had attended in their county. (fleatshear7) He found himself in the town of Warrington for the first time in his life. He had passed through the town on his way to Liverpool in 1872, and then be wondered what kind of place Warrington could be, and he little thought then that he would te invited to be present in his capa- city of Grand Master. (Applause.) That engagement would be one of the last that he should discharee in con- nection with the office in the Manchester Unity, for in four weeks' time he should be going back to Norwich, and his friend, Bro. D.G.M.C. Graham. of Burton-on-Trent would fill his place. He did not know whether they had amongst them any of the local clergy, but he could mention that in the Manchester Unity they bad a great number, who assisted them very much. The speaker then-gave some interesting details as to the origin and growth of the Oddfellow's Order. Bro. P.G.M. C.S. CAMPDEN, next addressed the meet- ing and in the course of his remarks ,aid that he had often advocated that same provision should be made for ladies in their society. Men were quite willing to take the ladies to their areas and admit them in their house- holds, and why should they not admit them into their, lodges. He considered it WOUII be an unmistakeable boon to them if they were admitted. (Hear, hear.) Be did not Press for a recognition of their equality at pre- sent, but he hoped they would seriously consider the matter and carry it out ina short time. (Applause.) Grand Master nowses next presented Bro. Adam G. Webster with the testimonial on behalf .oh the Chil- dren's Burial Fund, of which he bad been president five years. He mentioned that the capital possessed by , that lodge was now £5.250. Bro. P.P.G.M. WEBSTER expressed his thanks for the gift, and in doing so mentioned that the credit of mootiug " the club belonged to two gentlemen— William Howard of 'Wands Brothers, and William Savage, for it was they who had first talked the matter over. True, he was the Bust president, and had been so since, but what had got the club on was its own cheek. It had started without any money—except a" meg " (Loud laughter.) But they kept along and by dint of cheek managed to get a fund of £lB lfis 3d. by 186E3,and their membership grew considerably through resorting to what some people considered the "cheeky "'method of getting members by offering to take children from other societies into benefit for a suitable sum. (Laugh- ter.) At any rate at the end of the first five years they had paid £153 in claims for 62 deaths and had a fund of £248 18s. sd. :at the end of the second five years they had £697 10s. 3d. funds and had paid £359 for 116 deaths. The next five years they had £1,378 Ils. id. funds, of which they had paid £355 for 111 deaths. In the next five years they had a capital of £2,201 Is. 3:1., of which they paid £438 for 108 deaths. In the text five years ending 1892, they had a capital of £3,256 16s. 11d., of which they paid £474 for 116 deaths. Dur- ing that time the total membership had increased from 288 in 1888, to 1,428 in 1892. (Applause.) He thought that showed a very satisfactory state of affairs. Bro. P.O. M. C. GRAHAM (Burtou-en-Trentl next briefly proposed the " Warrington District" and also the" Loyal Admiral Nelson Lodge." Brother Prov. C.S. &amp;mos responded and in the course of a few remarks stated that the lodge was first founded hy four members 59 years ago at the Swan Inn, Bridge- street, and there was one gentleman' known as No. 1, who had been a member 45 years, who was with them still. He himself had been the secretary for over 20 years. Following this came more musical delectation by P.G. Reddish, and P.G. John 11. Sherlock, and Bro. Fanshawe, At the conclusion of the meeting Grand Master Floe era was presented with a walking stick as a memento of the Jubilee. After the meeting a dance was held, the music being provided by Mr. Percy Randalre band, Mr. G. Riley acted as M.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AM)</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AM)</p>
        <p>ArilLETlcs.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Football has been very much " orph " this week, and there has figuratively speaking,' been a considerable amount Of " gnashing of teeth " amongst the admirers of the Rugby Code who nave been compelled to suffer for the short-comings of the few,who misbehaved themselves in the match against Salford.</p>
        <p>Respecting that particulir matter, I see that the " Athletic News" corroborates, to a certain extent what Mr. Warren said in an interview last week, viz : that the suspension of the Warrington groancl was done as an example to others besides Warrington folk. The paper in question says :—" Probably never, since the Lancashire County Football Club was instituted has the Committee had to deal with such a batch of unpleasant cases as fell to them on Thursday, and it took them over turee hours to dispose of the lot."</p>
        <p>I believe that the club have placed the matter of Runcorn's refusal to carry out their fixture with War- rington in the hands of Mr. Percy D.4vies, solicitor, and there is likely to be some litigation presently,</p>
        <p>By the way our first team " bhoye "are going to have a plunge on the turf at Wilderspool, again to-day with Leigh. It is going to be a good match, arid as brass hands are now becoming the fashion at football matches the spectators are going to be allowed no ground for grumbling, for they will have their hearts cheered by the performance of a band on the field. ,</p>
        <p>The "Athletic News " also this week has a most amus lag, and humorous descriptim of "The Rollicking Referees," being a match between the Lancashire Referees and the Sale Fmotball club. The match ended in a draw. The writer indulged in the following analysis of the two teams :</p>
        <p>"The referees may have won caps of crimson velvet, decorated with 22 2-5 carat gold lac"; they may now feel glorified because they possess jerseys of peculiar stripes, or no stripes at all ; but the winning of such mighty renown as accompanies those tributes of merit ought at least to have instilled within their wearers the knowledge that there is a. point in the life of man when aspirations must cease, and when ambition has no longer proper pabulum to feed upon. But look at the apothe- osis presented. On the one hand we have a body of intellectual giants of football and professors of the game, men old enough to know better; descending from their elevated sphere, as it were, and essaying to show ' by ocular demonstration the points which at other times they elucidate by the aid of a shrill whistle, a stern voice, and a long dexter finger. Ranged on the other hand the mighty warriors or Sale are beheld. Through- out a long season they have performed prodigies; rhino- ire() opponents, and petrified adversaries until they reached the very apex orf delight, and crowned their distinguished record by playing a drawn game with powerful Internationals."</p>
        <p>The P.D. says that he dosn't think that" Philistine " the writer a the above is " The Tramp," who visited Warrington : he's too "hifalutin" for such an itinerant, he believes t.</p>
        <p>There is likely to be another "Jolley row" caused over this match I am told. And the delinquents are the Leigh men. They Ire having to play for them Fitzgerald and Wilding, the two well-known members of the Cardiff Club. And the Cardiff Club also want those two men to play for them They say they have been "de- coyed "from Cardiff, and therefore they will bring the matter up before the Welsh Union. This will be very sad for the Leigh Club, says the irrepressable one, the P.D., and he makes the matter worse by suggesting they should Leigh-vs the matter alone. How drefful.</p>
        <p>Swinton struck another blow for the Lancashire Cup on Saturday, when the fates threw them into contact with Oldham. The game is said to have been one whose fastness and toughness made the fiery blood of enthusiasts jump about their veins in a most electrifying manner. Well, perhaps so ; but the P.D. says he don't see any- thing in the electric shocks you get on a football field, to nia after I It was a shock, perhaps not electric, to the Oldharaites, when they saw that the score was— Swinton, 1-1-0 to NIL! The scores in connection with this competition stand thus at present :</p>
        <p>MOULTS DP TO MONDAY.</p>
        <p>For. 4gst.</p>
        <p>Won.Loat. Din. GT. (I'.'T. Pq Swinton l5 ... 2 ... 3 ... IT 30... 4 6 ...23 Salford</p>
        <p>14 . 9</p>
        <p>-. 1 -. 4 .- 7 14.- 111...22</p>
        <p>Tyldeslev Warrington Widan l4 . 7 6 . 1 . 5 8 6 9 15 St. Helens Recreation 15 ... 6 ... 6 ... 3 ...12 13 ..10 10.. 15 Oldham Broughton Rangers Rochdale Hornets Broughton lB . 1 14. 3 . 4 5 ..16 34... 5</p>
        <p>Widnes record has just been broken—that is,it has been broken upon the public. It is the record of their play for the year. It shows that during the season they have won 15 matches, lost 15, and drawn two. They lial4e scored 18 goals and 27 tries (140 points) to their op- ponents 19 goals, and 21 tries. making 127 poiuts al- togethn*. This shows a very good year's work.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington Junior League Competition, the Parish Church have at length got the much coveted glittering cup, in their grasp. They met Walkers' Brewery on Saturday—l meant to say tl'ey met the football team of that ilk, not the institution itself. It was thought that the Brewery would be—er better team than the Church ; but the idea was far " too rum " to be true ! No, keep that brick to build a house with. Anyhow the Church did the trick by 1-4-3 to 0-0-2. These are the tabulated results of the competitions :</p>
        <p>RESULTS UP TO SATURDAY, APRIL: 22,</p>
        <p>For Agst. Flyd. Won. Lost, Drn. G.T. G.T Pts. Par;96 Church .. 8 ... 5 ... 1 ... 2 ... 612 1 4 ... 12 Stockton HeLth... 8 ... 6 ... 2 ... 1 ... 3 9 ... 2 4 ... 11</p>
        <p>Latchfo‘d St. Mary's Walkers' Brewery 5 ... 0 ... 5 ... 0 ... 0 0 ... 2 16 ... 0</p>
        <p>The members of those clubs who thought they were going to have the cup, will have a chance of feasting their envious gaze upon it next week, for the vessr,lwith medals, will be exhibited in the window of Messrs. Johnson. upholsterers, Bridge-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Another big crowd worked their way into the cricket field attracted by the novelty of seeing the final of the Association Competition for the medals. t. Elphins and the Warrington Association Club were the contestants and a warm game resulted in a win for St. Elphins by four goals te two. After that Mr. Finlayson presented the medals to the winners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>Chelford was the place of attraction for the Warring- ton Cycling Club on Saturday. Thers was a good turn out again and the enjoyment of the day beamed under the sunny influence of the Restraining Ones. After a hearty meal had been sent the way of most other good things, the knights of the wheel again indulged in a game of football, but as for locating it under any cede— well it was just ! ! Warrington was reached at a rather late hour. But who was the man who came over the handles in Knutsford-road, after trying to clear a perambulator?</p>
        <p>LYMM. Sam. Scan.—lt is reported that the seven sh•ep, ont of the flock of 45 at Crawley, near Lyrom, are fast recovering from the disease known as sheep scab, from which they were found to be suffering last week. TDB BAND OF HOPE DEMONSTRATION AND THE SMALL- PDX EPIDEMIC—It is stated that in view of the presence of smallpox in Warrington, it has been decided not to hold the Band of Hope demonstration in Whitweek, as has been done in previous years. It is feared that the crowds of excursionists from Warrington who would pour into Lymm on that day would create the danger of the spread of the infection. Mara OF Ma. GEORGIi BIZERETOL—Mr. George Brere- ton, of Sandy-lane, died on Monday,after a short illness, at the early age of 29 ears. He was seized a week ago with pleurisy and inflammation,ancLin spite of the most careful attention, gradually sank under the effects of ! this terrible malady. Deceased was highly respected, not only in Lymm, but in Warrington, where he was well ! known. His loss will be keenly felt by the Wesleyan body,he having been for some years past a local preacher' in the Warrington Circuit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
