      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>SEEN FROM IiEEUND A NEWSPAPER. PEOPLE WHO FREQUENT IHE WARRINGTON LIBRARY.</head>
        <p>SEEN FROM IiEEUND A NEWSPAPER. PEOPLE WHO FREQUENT IHE WARRINGTON LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>BY LUKE BLUNT.</p>
        <p>There is a book well worth reading. at the War- rington /livery, and one which is not bound in calf skin—except occasionally. And that is the book of human nature. You can read it there without danger of dog-earing the leaves. When you want to find out whether anyone else is using the book, watch for the man who is gazing with a limpid, careless, stare from behind a newspaper, and pre- tending not to see you anymore than the blind horse saw that he was chewing the hair of the sleeping ostler instead of the hay. If you are a young lady, beware of that man ; if he is about, don't commence to read the "Sporting Echo in the fond hope that you are unobserved; not even though you have barricaded the paper in with a fortress of parceisand other things. Whilst acting the part of "the man behind the newspaper I have seen some funny things.</p>
        <p>The morning time is not very prolific of char- acters; they are mostly the same people who fre- quent the library then. To a great extent they are men whose clothes are eloquently persuasive of the fact that the occupiers “ do not toil, neither do they spin;" and have net done so for a considerable time.. But they are always the most industrious readers when they get to the morning papers. They have a playful kind of way of devouring it, as it were. You will perhaps see four of them come in together.. They each walk straight to a morning paper, gaze a few minutes at it whilst they finish the thought that occupied them when coming in, and then they will shown sign of becoming interested. First they will make themselves comfortable by hoisting up one leg, and roosting as it were on the other. Then they will read the title lines to see what paper it is, and becoming satisfied they will go through and read the headlines on the articles. This takes about a quarter of an hour, and when finished, necessitates the expenditure of a few moments whilst the " reader " is changing his roosting leg. When he has arranged this little matter of personal comfort he commences the arduous task of Ruing through the paper to find the smallest paragraphs, because it is unwise to strain the mind with anything big. When he gets through with this, the leading article is turned to, and food for reflection is found in surmising what subject the article is. based uponr but thiS becomes uninteresting after a time, and with- out trouble being taken to ascertain the subject of the article, the advertisements are referred to. Then you can see the man's eye wander about the sheet like a fly on the bird lime stick you've set to catch it. But presently an interested glitter gets up in- to his eyes. He has probably struck a matriniouial advertisement. This is the sunniest, dreamiest, and most ecstaciving romance he can find. It says, may- be— „</p>
        <p>YOUNG LADY, of independent means, wishes to correspond with respectable gentleman of good breeding and prepossesing Ab,there is an outlet from his.povertyt His big soft heart opens to the woman, although he has not seen her; and his big soft mind jumps into Fancy's Canoe and sails right along through a blissful sea of ele- gance and luxury, and respectability and matri- monial honey; and then it comes back and says "with a respectable gentleman of good breeding and prepossessing bearing." He chews the word respect- able over cud concludes that he cannot claim any relationship; and after contemplating the ends of his toes as they peep from his shoes, like the teeth of a laughing man, and having startled the assist- ants by" britling "his hand on the stubble of his face, he decides that he is not " prepossessing." And thus the dream fades ; and he feels lonely again, like a last year's gooseberry. Then lie leaves the paper and at length you get a chance to read the latest news. It may seem strange behaviour; but its the thing that goes on in the idle mind of an idle person. This type of individual, by the way is only the exception ;I am merely alluding-to exceptions in this.</p>
        <p>During the afternoon, a miscellaneous type of humanity entereth the portals of this home and treasurehouse for the studious. I have been in and heard a German emigrant go to one of the assistants and say' Mein Gott., I vas losht 1" Ile wrung his bands and looked as though his soul was plunged I into the deepest depths of despair. Then he resumed, " Ver vill find mein broder Frederick— show me dot his name vas in de directorary." It took them a long time to make him go away ;and he had schrcely left the building when a young lady all flurried and breathless bounced in and said 'Oh, tell me, is Mrs. Woods in—she's not all out is she?" And the young man behind the counter said " don't know her. Is she a stout lady rOf : course she explained how stupid it was of her, and said she wanted to know if some of Mrs. Wood's novels were in! • Then it is a common thing for many fashionable young ladies to come into the reading room to look at the "Gentlewoman." ()nee I was guilty enough to " peep " from behind a newspaper at a yeung lady who wanted to see that magazine. Just before she arrived a big, lazy, child- ishly happy kind of man, had been amusing himself by allowing a fly to scramble all about his big rough hand until it crept through his fingers ; then he clenched his hand and tried to catch it. it flew away ; then came back ; the same V' ing occurred again. Then it flew on a snagazine opposite; the man had become enthusiastic now; a big grin was allover his face (he didn't know [ was watching!) ; he seemed to say to himself: " Now I got yer," then he craned over quietly and made a sudden grab for the fly. "Whizz ! Bang! Bang!" He had swept a good deal of current literature to the floor, and At the dame time an assistant came running in. what did the man do? He just picked up the " Gentlewoman," and fixed a concentrated glare on a page of " latest fashions?' Then that young lady came in. She marched round the tables till she found that big man with the magazine_ She saw that what he knew about patterns didn't amount to more than what a mule knew of whisky cocktail. So she took a chair right opposite him, and after trying to read a paper she threw it down and fixed her ten- der, but steady gaze upon the uncouth one. By and by, when things got quiet again, he stole a sidelong glance at the assistant; it was all right; so he let his eyes wander some more, till they met the gaze of the girl. They' dropped like lead. In a bit he raised them again ; she was still looking : he went red, and eonfused ; then in a bit he looked up again. But it was like an electric shock when he caught her eyes, and When she timidly told him the book was up-side-down, he got up and said" Te-ye--Txa the pictures arn't straight." And then he sneaked away outside.</p>
        <p>With the evening, come two very important personages—one is the small boy with dirty hands, and the other is the casual, who comes to kill time till the " doss-house " opens. There are not many of the latter, of course, lie generoll3 comes in about seven, when the place is warm and snug and full of people. It is always very quiet then— scarcely any sound save the hard, regular ticking of the clock. The casual is usually very tired and he sits in a quiet corner. Ile looks all alive and comfortable at first ; then the lulling silerice makes him feel sleepy he closes his eyes and nods oft a bit : then jumps up with a start. But by end by ho stops jumping up ; his breathing becomes heavy ;,then a gentle, regular snore comes ? Little boys bob their heads up and chuckle ; the snore developes and drowns the tick of the clock. Every- body looks at each other ; then at the tramp, They start again at the books ; hut it isn't any use ;the snoring acts like being tickled down the back with a feather. The snores at length reach the counter, an assistant looks up and sees everyone gazing with tender disapproval at the weary tramp and the assistant tip-toes to him, shakes his shoulder and says" lob ! she—wake up. This isn't a lodg- inghouse !" He wakes up and with that sang froid only inherited by tramps, walks out with his hands in his pockets.</p>
        <p>The troublesome little boys with dirty hands, do not, as a rule, arrive much before 0.30; then for about two hours goes on such trouble as to make it probable that the assistant will soon he grey-headed. Perhaps three of them will come in too•et her. This is something like the general dialo- gue that goes on :—Small Boy: "Er-h-have yer got Robinson Crusoe in, please-eh r Assistant: " Let me look at your hands—oh ! Go out and wash them. Let me see yours—ah ! Tom go, too ; and you Why Y011it.73 haven't been washed for a month; go 'long." They gojUst outside the door,where they expectorate upon their hands ; and then energetically polish them on their pants ; and return, and succeed in being admit ted as clean enough. Then after select- ing theological and other heavy wf:.rks, in their con- fusion of the titles, they ultimately arrive at a stage when the three of them are going to look over</p>
        <p>^ .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <p>the pictures in "British Bat tleon Land and Sea." Personal remarks about the bi4torical personages in the book are made, witlithe result that from mil- ling they get to sniggering, and then an occasional explosion of laughter, one of which induces the assistant to ask them to go out. The small boys are abhorred by the grown pereons in the library ; they talk so much in " stage whispers,' and crack so many hunabegs,and shuffle their feet so,that one, looking from behind his newspaper, can see the dire destruction they play upon the elders' nervous system.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>LATCHFORD BAPTIST BAZAR.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD BAPTIST BAZAR.</p>
        <p>TIIIC OPENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>, "Certain alterations to the chapel which hays. incurred a debt of .f.BO said the Bev. J.- W. Varley, "is one of the reasons why we hold this bazaar.' The rev, gentleman made thie statement on Thurs- day last, when the bazaar was opened at the Latch- ford Baptist schools. A large number of people, chiefly ladies,had gathered amongst the picturesque stalls to witness the opening ceremony, and' upon a raised platform were seated with the. Bev. J. W. Valley, the following gentlemen: The 'Rev. John Tongs, Mr. Ziba Armitage, Councillor H.- Roberts, and Mr. Davidson. Continuing his explanatory remarks, Mr. Varlay mentioned that his congrega- tion had not found the debt to be altogether a mill- stone round their necks; although they were anxious that it should be removed as early as possi- ble. Bat, the bazaar was not solely for the reason of raising that £BO. A much more substantial. reason was given by the Pastor. He pointed' to a few patches of the brick wall, which the ladies had not effectually hidden by their stalls, and then he said" We also require a little money for the , renovation of those schools—we want to remove what I think I may describe as the terrible state of the' walls ; a condition of things which I think is attributable •to the Warrington" porous bricks." Titer, were, he said, a great many bricks which were iinfortunately used in, Warrington that allowed the water to flow in unrestricted' streams through them into the room. And, of course although they were Baptists they did not like to be Undergoing immersion every time it rained. To effec- tually deal with these treacherous bricks, and make them watertight, and do other little alterations it would be necessary to raise a sum of £l5O. That was why they had arranged the bazaar. He had asked Mr. Ziba Armitage to open it for them and 'in doing so he con- sidered that they war.' inviting the assistance •of a gentleman who was probably more interested in the prosperity of their body in that district than anyone. Els then invited Mr. Armitage to open the bazaar. Mr. ARMITAGE Said it was very gratifying to him that —despite certain "instructions" which had issued from a perticelar local pulpit—there were, in that gathering, the signs of strength in a free church. He had been acquainted with the Wash-lane Baptists since the time when Mr. Wilkinson was the pastor, and since that time lie knew the body had been associated with work that was earnest and good and sympathetic; and he hoped that would characterize them always, and that their good work and influence would increase. He mentioned that the Ship Canal when opened was likely to cause a rapid growth of the population in the neighbourhood of Latchford, and thus would be brought into the circle of that chapel a much larger number of persons ; and con- sequently the probability was,that they would soon have to effect extensive alterations and enlargements. In pronouncing the bazaar ,open he wished the workers every success. • Councillor H. ROBERTS next moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Armitage. Although, he said, it was a very pleasant task to him,he thought that they ought to have had it performed by a Baptist. With respect to the Rey.J.W.Varley's allusions to the Warrington bricks, he never expected to hear a Baptist complain about being in a demi.) atmosphere. (Laughter). It might be the nature of the Warrington bricks to take in "wet," but there were other things in town which took in consider- ably more" wet "—(laughter)—and they oueht to mike them beer proof as well as making the bricks water proof: (Laughter.) He next mentioned the great pleasure he had in proposing the vote of thanks to Mr. Armitage. He had known Mr. Atmitage a long time, and he had had the pleasure of knowing his father. Ile characterized Mr. Armitage's faintly as one of the staunchest and most energetic and benevolent fatnilies connected with Nonconformity in Warrington. The vote of thanks was seconded by Mr. Davidson, and carried Unanimously, after which Mr. Ziba , Armitage responded. - The conclusion of what is sometimes described as "the speechifying" was the signal for the commencement of enrchasing. There were two stalls containing a rnisoel- laneotia burden of articles, plain and fancy, and these were under. the charge of :—Mrs. Varley, Mrs. Davidson, Mrs. Adams, &amp;Ira, Dutton, Mrs. Bennion; Mrs. Pearson, Mrs. John fairhurat, Mrs. Joseph Alhinson, Mrs. Albin- son, Mrs Isaac Wilkinson, Mrs. Carter, Miss McConnell, Miss Albinson, and Miss Carter. There was a flower stall under the care of Miss Boardman,Miss Blinston,and Miss ,Tones and a refreshment stall which was supervised by Mrajohn Fairhurst,Mrs. Ford,Miss Wells,and Miss Fair- hurst. The Rev. J. W. Varley had also arranged an advertisement stall whichle superintended himself. A descriptiokof any one of the particufar titans would be invidious ;.it is enough to say that each one contained a wealth of valuable and 'Useful goods, which reflected the greatest credit upon the ladiem by whose efforts the • wares had been got together. .</p>
        <p>-During the -evening musical entertainments were given, and the building became thronged with pur- chasers after tea.. The total amount realized during the day was £55.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>FRIDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>FRIDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The bazaar was again opened yesterday afternoon, when there was a good attendance. Amongst thoFe present were Mr. Thomas Morris, F.G.S. ; Revs. J. W. Varley, and J. C,ottam (EarleFtown), Councillor Plump- ton, and Mr.Albinson. After hymn and prayer, the Rev. j. W. Varley gave a short acJount of the object of the bazaar, and said Mr. Armitage came the day before and helped them considerably, and they hoped Mr. Morris had come with the same intentions. If they all did their best they would attainthe object they had in view. He also mentioned that he had received cheques from Mr. E. J. Guest,J.P , and Mr. W. Long, J.P. He then called upon NIL Morris to open the bazaar.—Mr. Morris, who was received with applause, said he considered bazaars a magnificent way for raising money. It was not so much the material that made the articles valuable, but the work the ladies spent upon them. He felt assured that they would get the money they required. He had great pleasure in declaring the bazaar open.—Mr. Albinson moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Morris, which was seconded by Councillor Plumpton, and carried. The tanceeds for the twojdays amounted to over £lOO. The bazaar will be open again to-day (S4urday) at 5-30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>FORTHCOMING COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council will be held next Tuesday, when amongst other matters the following will be considered.</p>
        <p>NEW BUILDINGS. That the plans of the following buildings,. Sm., be approved :—Seven houses in Ellesmere-street, for Mr. W. Adams ; two houses in street off Knutsford-road, for Mr: Peter Baguley ; ten cottages in street off Knutsford-road, for Mr. Mac Cue ; eighteen cottages in Watkin-street, for Mr. W. Cross ; two houses in Bostock-street, for Mrs. Mackie ; store-room in Dallam-lane, for Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Limited ; workshop and store-room off Knutsford-road, for Mr. Wm. Bibby; two cottages and shop in Winwick-road, for Mrs. Ashton; two cottages in Alder-lane, tar Mr. J. Browne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>TOE SMALLPDX: MURAL PATIENTS.</head>
        <p>TOE SMALLPDX: MURAL PATIENTS.</p>
        <p>_ 'the Health Committee have resolved that notice be given to the Warrington Rural Sanitary Authority that no fresh cases of smallpox from their district will be taken into the Hope Hospital after the 30th April, ex- cept from that portion of their district immediately adjoining the borough and within the extra-municipal portion of the township of Warrington.'</p>
      </div>
